      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>START WITH THE WIND.</head>
        <p>START WITH THE WIND.</p>
        <p>(Queries and correspondence should be addressed to "Spokes," c/o The Editor. !toplies cannot be sent by post, and all queries will be answered in our columns.)</p>
        <p>I see that an old, old problem—should one start an afternoon's ride with the wind at one's back or face it and get the worst over?—is beipg revived again, presumably in preparation for the spring gales. I remember this same poser being handed round the club room in the days when I toiled along on a solid. My views have undergone no change since then. I say emphatically: Start with the wind. There are heaps of reasons why, the chief of which are (1) That you have never got your " sea-legs " until you have covered half a dozen miles or so, and the help of the wind is much more welcome then when you are spinning home in the even- ing, strengthened and filled with new life by the fresh country air you have been breathing. (2) You never know when the wind is going to change. Winds, March winds specially, are treacherous in more ways than one. Grind against them for the run out, thinking of the free wheel run you are going to have home, and ten to one you will find when you come" out from the cottage where you have tea'd, they have switched right round and are blowing exactly the opposite way or else have dropped to a breathless calm. And there's another point, the wind very often goes down with the sun and the gale you fought against in the afternoon may have altogether disappeared when you ex- pect its help in the evening.,</p>
        <p>The Scottish Highlands. The Highlands of Scotland afford every con- ceivable kind of scenery. There are charming valleys and rugged mountains; there are lanes as sweet as any in the South of England; there are broad moorland roads, where , you can travel for miles without seeing a tree or a house. Taking Edinburgh as a convenient starting-point I would suggest this route to see the Highlands best :—Falkirk, 71; Stirling, 111; Dunblane, 5; Perth, 28}; Pitlochry, 271; Dal- whinnie, 304; Carrbridge, 33.4 • Inverness, 25; Fort Augustus, 33:1; Fort William, 324; Oban, 40; Inverary, 32; Arrochar, 22; Tarbet, 11; ferry over Loch Lomond to Inverwaid; Callender, 21; Stirling, 16; Edinburgh, 352; total, 4444 miles. A route which is crammed full of interesting places, and where, as the advertisement 'says, "There is not a dull moment," is difficult adequately to describe in a short note. At a later date I will try to deal with certain dis- tricts separately, such as the Spey or 'Whisky" Valley and the Trossachs. _ Between Valkirk and Stirling Bannockburn is passed, a spot dear to the heart of every Scotsman. The run from Stirling to Perth is along a fine -road, but the ride from Perth to Inverness is one of the most picturesque in Great Britain, passing through. some of the' finest Highland scenery. There are thickly wooded river glens, such 'as Kink.- crankie ; there is the rugged ascent of the Grampians. Some of the hills are very steep and the going is heavy, and' care is always necessary if only because of frequent wandering sheep. From Inverness to Fort William is a picturesque run along the side of the Caledonian Canal; thence to Oban there is many a wide sea view. Two ferries have to be crossed. To Inverary from Oban is a hard, but magnificent run to the shore of Loch Lomond. At Tarbet take the beat across the loch to Inversnaid, and then up the hill and over to Stronachlachar, by the little steamer, down poetic Loch Katrina</p>
        <p>I • • , ' • • 1 ; •</p>
      </div>
