        <p>; • ^ •</p>
        <p>and by Callander and Stirling back to Edin- burgh.</p>
        <p>Cheap British-made Cars. When buying a car one is naturally anxious to procure one that will last a good many seasons. A one-season car is a thing to be avoided like the very. plague itself. British manufacturers, realising this. point, have always kept this matter in view in designing their engines, chassis and bodieq. They have never accepted the hateful Amerkan ideas that "Just good enough" is good enough for them. But I think they have, if anything, erred in the opposite direction. They have devoted their skill to the production of a small number of cars of tip-top quality. But to do this, they have had to charge very high prices for them. Manufac- turers should not lose sight of the fact that the demand to-day—and the demand will be far greater to-morrow—is from the millions who want a car at a reaAonable price which is reliable and built to last. An Inclusive Price.</p>
        <p>When on this subject there is another point to which I would direct the attention of manu- facturers. Why can't they generally adopt the excellent American method of selling a car "all on"? I remember my own experience when I bought my present car—a car which by the way is atill going strong after a mileage of 27,500 with only one overhaul. I ordered a car—as I thought a complete car—the price of which was catalogued at £285. After a few days' interval I had a letter from the firm asking me whether I wanted a wind-screen (27 10s.), a hood (213), lamps (210 155.), horn (215.), speedometer £5 55.), a mat for the front floor-boards (21 10s.), and a few other odds and ends. That is to say that in- stead of buying a car, in my ignorance I im- agined a complete car, for £285, I had to pay. another £4O. And the :unfair part of it was that in the catalogue the car was shown with wind- screen, hood, lamps, and everything complete save the speedometer, and it was clearly marked "price £285." Manufacturers will ,find it pays them much better to give truthful inclusive price. It will certainly save their customers a great deal of annoyance.</p>
        <p>SPOKES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>CATARRHAL COLDS AND STUBBORN COUGHS.</head>
        <p>CATARRHAL COLDS AND STUBBORN COUGHS.</p>
        <p>The Remedy you can Trust is VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Every fourth person you meet has catarrh. it &amp;gins with running at the nose, the result of uatching cold. If neglected it soon becomes chronic. Then it passes to the throat, the stomach, the 'bowels: It causes asthma, deaf- ness, huskiness, hoarseness; it sets up dyspepsia and bowel troubles—there is no end to the dangers of catarrh. My life was a perfect misery with breathlessness and bronchial catarrh," says Mrs. Hall, of 89, Clarence- street, Shieldfield, Newcastle-on-Tyne. "I Was frightfully choked up, and if I ventured out I 'would have to stop to get my breath, perhaps twenty times in a street's length. Nothing did any real good till I got Veno's. That quite cured me." Ask for Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, 110., is. 3d: and 35., of all chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Farmer and His Learned Wife.</head>
        <p>The Farmer and His Learned Wife.</p>
        <p>An honest farmer in the State of Pennsyl- vania married a learned 'miss from a boarding school for his second wife. He was struck dumb with her eloquence and at her learning. "You might," said he, "bore a hole through the solid airth, and chuck in a millstone, and she'll tell you to a shavin' how long the stone will be goin' clean through.. I used for to think that it was air I breathed, but she telled me that I had been sucking i 4 two kinds of gin—ox gin and high gin! My stars I. I'm an abstainer, and yet have linen drinking ox gin and high gin all my life r'</p>
