      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>VIE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1916. JOTTINGS BY AN OLD THAT ODD DAY. SUCCESS IN THE, POULTRY JOURNALIST. More About Leap Year And The YARD.</head>
        <p>VIE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1916. JOTTINGS BY AN OLD THAT ODD DAY. SUCCESS IN THE, POULTRY JOURNALIST. More About Leap Year And The YARD.</p>
        <p>"In My Cottage." The announcement of the death of a- Grappen- hall man who had occupied the same house for 60 years, set me thinking -of other cases of the kind that I have met with. I have known at least two " octogeraniums " who have spent their 611 80 years under the very roof beneath which they first saw the light; and I have heard of quite a number of others. However, I will keep to Versonal experience, and I have a truly extraordinary instance in reserve.</p>
        <p>Human Limpets. Once on a walking tour I passed throtigh a district of which some of the Lancashire dialect writers, particularly Edwin Waugh and Ben Erierley, seem to have been very fond. I passed the public-house where the poker-weighing trick was (as he confesses in one of his inimit- able sketches) played at the formees expense; and I continued the ascent toward what Brierley describes as " th' riggin' o' th' world." A shower overtook me about midway between these points, and I sought shelter, in the nearest cottage. The occupants were an aged, brother and sister, and when I asked the latter about a hospital that I had passed, she quite startled me by confessing that, although the building in question was within a couple of miles, she had not seen it, having, in fact, never been so far from home for 60 years! Yet the scene, from the kitchen window was to me posi- tively foradding—a -stretch of bleak, rugged, treeless country, relieved only by brickworks and a coal mine. No wonder the old couple were on the verge of idiocy. Incautious Travellers.</p>
        <p>" Foyer's " story last week respecting " Pickles' " train experience might be paralleled by one told of Bishop Wilberforce, who, unknown to a couple of clergymen chatting in the same compartment, had the joy and felicity of hear- ing them. tell each other apocryphal stories about "Soapy Sara "—who, of course, revealed his identity before leaving , the train, and hugely enjoyed their embarrassment. 'I have often been struck by the curious habit' some have of thus freely and audibly comparing notes in a carridge containing strangers. I had such an experience only the other day. Two of my fellow-travellers began discussing the 'character of one with whom I happened to be well acquainted and the statements they made became so out- rageous that at last I interposed with the assur- ance that their allegations were baseless.. It was their own fault; it was impossible not to hear what they said. At a Bull-fight. The bull-fight scene now to be witnessed at the Star Kinema reoalled two acquaintances of my own who have seen the real thing in Spain, and have described it to, me in detail. One is a clergyman, who believes that one shpuld "do in Rome as the rum 'uns do "; the other is traveller for an engineering firm. lam bound to admit the layman was ,by far the more dis- gusted of the two. "It was a sickening ex- hibition," he said; "I was shocked and horn. fled, and simply couldn't stick it." I was tempted to seize upon his bit of slang for the purposes of a fairly obvious pun, but it was evident that he was too much in earnest for that. The Brotherhood Movement.</p>
        <p>The recurrence of the Wycliffe P.S.A. anniver- sary reminded me of a paper on the "spiritual ideals" of this movement which Professor Peake read not very long ago. It was certainly one of the most remarkable of its kind. Dis- cussing the features of such a meeting as I have in mind for the moment, he said the ideal was that from the first Moment to the last the mem- bers should feel that they are in the atmosphere of devotion. Hymns should be carefully chosen, but the standard of selection should not be too severe. While it was not -rcrtotly an edifying sight to watch an audience of men solemnly singing with gusto some mawkish and trivial ditty, yet they might so pursue the cult of virility as to rule out what it would be good for all of them to sing, on the ground that it was too sentimental. The professor readily allowed that a larger latitude might be accorded a speaker at a Brotherhood meeting than to a preacher in the pulpit; but strongly insisted that Brotherhoods had abdicated their proper function unless they afforded a foremost place to the spiritual side of their ministry.</p>
        <p>Wesley's Playful Humour. As I read what the reverend lecturer at Legh- street Primitive Methodist Church had to say about this, an instance of Which I heard many years ago came to my mind. It is said that the founder of Methodism was strolling by the sea, when three young SOapegraces who were near -resolved to haye a little fun. They planted themselves in front of him, and the spokesman exclaimed "Oh, Mr. Wesley, have you heard the news? The Devil's dead!" 'Dear, dear," was the quiet rejoinder, "and whatever is to be- come of you, poor fatherless children?"</p>
        <p>The Patient Angler. "Poor results," "very disappointing,"" lack- ing in size"! were some of the phrases occurring in last week's reports concerning angling in the Gowy and other local waters. I was reminded of the story of how a prominent politican con- trived to beat the day's record of a certain 'club, and so won the distinction of president for the evening. His catch consisted of a Vb. barbel and a 311 b. trout, so, on noticing that another had caught a trout which looked larger than his, he seized a convenient opportunity to ram the barbel, down the throat of his trout— which on ", weighing in" turned 41b. Another member begged his trout to serve at a dinner to some mutual friends, and subsequently said to the donor, "There were some very extra- ordinary circumstances about that fish you gave me; I had no idea that the trout was so voracious —yours had swallowed a barbel." This was warmly contested by an eminent naturalist who was present, and, as the other would not be denied the evidence of his senses, they had a lively set-to. The politician smiled and—yes, actually—owned up.</p>
        <p>Philosophic Indeed. The foregoing is not given as an example worthy of imitation by any member of a local club. Such as have had "poor results," &amp;e., may be com Mended rather to the brother of the rod who tells how he fished at one place, and. the , fish unanimonsry declined to respond; at another ' and/still failed to connect; at a third, and came empty away; at a fourth, and preserved his unblemished record; at a fifth, and yet lacked the luck to persuade any fish to come out of :the wet; at a sixth, and brought away a firm con- viction that there, wasn't even As much as a pickled herring within five miles of the place. After this had gone, on a while with the like results, he began to fancy himself for "the thrilling honour of establishing a world's record of the longest succession of blank days," for ' he was not as other, men who merely had two blank .days, and then went out and caught three tittlebats and a defunct tom-cat before they could taste the chaste delights of a blank day again. An ex- perienced angler took him in hand, and led him hither and thither in the dete'vnination to make him catch . fish; still, nothing doing "Then," he goes on, "flushed with triumph at the thought of my unsullied reputation for noth- ing,- I laid myfrod on the ground. with the line in the water,. while I o,te thy lunch, and-,--a silly little fish came and caught itself.- The spell was broken; I was no more on the lofty pinnacle of uninue non-success: I was but as other men after all, and I lost.' all interest in -the snort." Really, it is our old friend, Mr. Carter Platt's story,. though of necessity severely Curtailed. Warrington's 9s. 4d. Rate. .. . Although I have forgotten the occasion, I have an idea that it was Lord Belper who a while ago declared that if local government is to be carried on hr the spirit which has so lonranimated its administra`tion, there must he a change in the attitude of the Central bodies, a redistribution of the duties, and greater freedom from irritat- ing regulations. The ratepayers must always provideu substantial part 'of the money for local purposes, and , their. representatives tnusi., periodically go .to them for sanction to the scheniel. of expenditure proposed. But many of the new and costly duties cast upon the authori- ties are not of their own seeking, and though their cost has to be, added to the rates, the rate- payers and their representatives have no authority to revise, reject, or COnfrol the out- lay.</p>
        <p>THE BACKYARD FANCIER.</p>
        <p>There is no doubt that when this war is over says a writer in "The Feathered World." poultry will be wanted, and those that are lucky to have stock will be in the swim. The back- yarder, or, rather, his good wife, will have to see to the fowls, for most of us are being wanted, and are likely to be wanted, so' " mother" must try and rear a few extra broods. Backyarders must feed for eggs and get all they can out of their stock. I think the fancier who can stick to his hobby is doing a lot for this country. We must study our birds and not waste the smallest bit of land. Fill the ground up with vegetables, for they will be useful for the birds. Turnips can be grown, and are a good feed for poultry when boiled and mashed. and if boiled in pot liquor and a little meat added, or with a couple of handfuls of meat meal boiled up make a nice tasty meal. This should be put in the pot where the potato peelings, Sm., are being boiled up. It is st to feed on the d ii best foods for best results. egetables mean a lot. and especially to close - n birds.; they are half the battle. .</p>
        <p>IMPORTANT BACKYARD POINTS. The backyard fancier needs to be very patient during the chicken rearing season. His lack of room is the greatest handicap. A number of small pens would prove a great boon.</p>
        <p>If these can be arranged the chicks can always be given a change of ground.</p>
        <p>And change is of the utmost importance where size is desired.</p>
        <p>A plentiful supply of green food will consider- ably hclp matters: In addition to being given raw, green food should form a fair proportion of the mash. Ants eggs, dried flies, ttc., will also assist in the matfer of size. Cooked meat is also useful if given regularly.</p>
        <p>Backyard Pests. The greatest is the red mite. • Paraffin or creosote will speedily settle ac- counts with this nuisance. The dung heap is also a nuisance—to the neighbours. If the manure is stored- in a drT place, smell will be practically non-existent. See that the feikee is sufficiently high and free from holes. Or the garden will suffer. The cock crowing nuisance is one to be tolerated. 4co really practical mire has yet been devised. -Unless it is intended to breed, there is no 'necessity for a male bird. He only affects the egg supply in the matter of fertility, .</p>
        <p>The Necessity of Exercise. Most backyard pens are extremely limited in range. Birds kept in confined runs are apt to put on flesh. This naturally follows lack of oxercise. Hem which are overfat cannot produce many eggs. They are also apt to begin feather eating.</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO COTTAGERS AND SMALL HOLDERS OF CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>- Having offered to co-operate with the Board of Agriculture in their scheme for the DISTRIBU- TION of SITTINGS of EGGS of PEDIGREE PURE BREEDS of POULTRY to COTTAGERS and SMALL HOLDERS by supplying Sittings of Eggs from my Best Breeding Pens of WHITE WYANDOTTES and WHITE LEGHORNS, the Board referred my offer to the Cheshire C.C. Edu- cation Committee. who inform me that they have .not taken the Government Scheme up this year. I have decided under the circumstances to supply direct to any Cottager or Small Holder who cares to apply for same, Sittings of Eggs from these pens at the low cost Of 3/6 per Sitting of 12 Eggs, timer_ tiles replaced once (if returned carriage 'paid): 15 unreplaced. Kindly supply particulars of assessment of holding at time of order. Purchaser to pay car- riage or postage. No applicant to be allowed more than 3 Sittings in one year.,. _</p>
        <p>PEDIGREES OF PENS (Trap-nested). No. 1, WHITE LEGHORNS. • COCKEREL from 282 HEN by 260 SIRE, mated to Pullets, daughters to Cam's Grimley win- ners and 2nd Seddlescombe winners. No. 1 WHITE WYANDOTTES. COCKEREL from 281 HEN by 272 SIRE, mated •to Pullets from sisters of Pen 19, Harper Adams Comnetition winners. 1914-15. These Birds are all from the strains that hold' the world's records, Cam's. of Hoghton. All applications taken in rofgfin't. 4.13P1y direct to HMARSHALL) BRIERFIELD POULTRY FARM, , ASTON, Near NANTWICEL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>History Of The Calendar.</head>
        <p>History Of The Calendar.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR MOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>No. 11.</p>
        <p>The arrangement outlined at the close of our last article was called the Gregorian Calendar, and was imposed upon the world by a Papal decree. Italy, Spain and Portugal adopted it at once, and France fol- lowed in a few months. The Protestant coun- tries held back, as might have been expected. The first to accept it was Scotland, who began in 1600, at the same time making January Ist the beginning of the year. A century later most of the German States adopted it, but Eng- land, from some prejudice, delayed until 1752. For 152 years there would be some curious cal- culations for those who crossed the border between England and Scotland. The English year began on March 25th, the Scotch on January Ist. Thus a Scotsman who crossed the-Tweed on, say, March 20th, 1750, would find that he had strayed back into the year 1749, and that it was March 10th. •</p>
        <p>Even more delightful to a Scotsman with a sufficient supply of humour would be the sen- sation of walking across the bridge at Berwick early in 1700 and reminding the stick-in-the-mud English that they were still grovelling in the benighted days of the 17th century. , The change was made in England by an Act of Parliament passed in 1750. This ordered that September 2nd, 1752, should be called Septem- 'ber 14th, and that the first day of the year should be January Ist. The story of that Act of Parliament gives a not very flattering side- light upon the mental condition of our ances- tors, who made a great outcry and formed mobs who went about with the cry "Give us back our eleven days." Russia still keeps the old calendar, and is now about 13 days behind the rest of Europe. So are all the States in which the Greek Church is • supreme. , These who have pondered the fads we have set forth will not' need to be reminded that our , calendar offers many curious points and leaves room for curious happenings. Those of us, for instance. who lived from 1896 to 1904 saw seven consecutive years without a leap year, an ex- perience which nobody- will have unless they live to see the year A.D. 2004.</p>
        <p>And what of the odd case of those who are born on February 29th and only get 'a :genuine birthday every four years. and who had to wait eight years at the close of the century? • In their youth we sympathise with them; in later years, when birthdays become a nuisance, we congratulate :them. There is, by the way, ia Berlin a- Leap Year Club, to which nobody can belong except those horn on February 29th. They have a,birthday party every time February 29th comes round, and in 1904 held a very special festival to mark the lapse of eight years which had brought them no birthday at all. On that occasion the Chairman was a dear old gentleman who was celebrating his 21st anniversary. In ordinary life he would have been an octogenarian; in the club he was just emerging from the strip- ling stage and "coming-of-age." Have any of my readers discovered that it is possible so to start in life as never to enjoy a birthday at all? This is the sad case of anyone who may have the bad fortune to be born on February 30th. To illustrate we will mention the case of a baby girl born on a voyage from San Francisco to Yokohama in 1904. • It comes about in this way. ,If we set for4l upon a vessel to make a journey round the world we either gain or lose upon the calendar. If we sail past and meet the sun, we gain;- if we go with the sun, from east to west we lose. In order to make the actual number of sunrises and sunsets experienced during a journey corre- spond with the days•shown on the calendar an extra, day must be put in or put out, as the case may be. The mariner puts in a day at the end of a month, or a- day, in nautical language, is "heaved 'overboard," according to whether he is taking his long journey eastward or west- ward.</p>
        <p>This adjustment is made •at the time of crossing the 180th meridian which runs through the Pacific Ocean. It so happened that the captain was going to Japan from America in February, 1904. The• leap day was in the calendar and to make things right the captain had top add another day to February and call it Febiruary 30th. It so hapiened that on this day a little girl had the misfortune to be born. She was duly registered as having begun life on February 30th, 1904. Perhaps my ingenious readers may be able to tell her how to get a birthday. For myself I can only think of one plan. She will have to take a passage upon a slow vessel-in some leap year and arrange to be crossing the 180th meridian at the end of the month. It could be done by arrangement with the skipper, the weather, and with Father Time, but it would be a lot of trouble. Possibly the little maiden has thought it all out and by this time given it up as a bad job. "</p>
        <p>In the first article which' appeared in "The Examiner" on this subject, the word Popes was printed for Pontiffs. The sentence was to the effect that in the days of the Romar. Empire the Pontiffs were the calendar makers and could shorten or lengthen• the years to please their friends or embarrass their opponents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>ENTERPRISE IN WARR INGTON.</head>
        <p>ENTERPRISE IN WARR INGTON.</p>
        <p>In accordance with the spirit of pregress manifested by Warrington lattly, another very notice- able .improvement has just been made in the busy Horsemarket-street. Not only the public, but some of our local architects and city fathers have passed some flattering comments on the New Shop Front just completed at the premises of Messrs. Suits, Ltd., the enterprising and well- known Tailors, by Messrs. Roberts and Co., shop fitters, of Manchester, The New Front has been carried out in very light polished Oak, and forms a striking contrast to the rather sombre surroundings. The design is a pleasing com- bination of the modern and classic. Curved spandrils, with leaded light panels at the top of the window, and. polished gun metal mouldings with ornamental cast base's to the vertical bars at the bottom.; give a most pleasing and artistic effect. The entrance lobby, is paved with black and white tesselated tiles; and the 'walls are lined with oak wainseot panelling. The interior of the shop window has a moulded white ceiling, while the enclosure is fitted with mirror panel- ling. In spite of the lighting restrictions, hand- some Electroliers with antique Silver finish fur- nish a brilliant scheme 'of lighting arrange- ment. Messrs. Suits, Ltd., --who are making a speciality of Ladies' Costumes, Tailor-made, from 305., to measure, are to be congratulated on the transformation effected and on the happy design and carrying out of 'their new shop front scheme. They have been established in War- rington nearly ten years, and during that time have made thousands and thousands of Suits, I Overcoats, Raincoats, &amp;c., for the local and sur- rounding inhabitots. Their success is ,not to be wondered at. To niake a suit of the same ~material for which' the old-fashioned and high- priced tailor charged from ~t 3 U. upwards, at as low a figure as. 205., was .no small achievement. , Yet this is what Messrs. Suits, Ltd., do. Owing to the number of branches they control and the unlimited power of Purchase and organisation which this commands, they are able to produce a high-class garment at: less than half the price other tailors charge. In these days of increasedl prices, it is of interest to note that owing to the excePtional' stocks on hand at. the commence- ment of the War, Messrs. Suits; Ltd., have not • been compelled to advance their prices what- ever, and still continue to make Gentletnen's Suits, Overcoats, or Raincoats from 205., am! Ladies' Costumes from 30s. Their Ladies' Rain- coats at 21s. are splendid. Boys' clothing is marked at very low prices, and itis worth noting that the weight of the materials used for their juvenile clothes is the same as for the men's. This is important, boys being able to get through anything.. In view Of Easter being near at hand, the public may with confidenqe inspect the delightful Suitings, Costume materials, Raincoats, both Ladies' and Gent's, and Juvenile Garments .now ,being displayed at Suits, Ltd.'s, establishment, 17, Horsemarket-street, War- rington.</p>
        <p>Proprietary Privileges. The owners of certain proprietary whiskey,: have decided to put them up sixpence i bottle. In tesponse to this Move the. owners of, certain nroprikary sixnences have, decided not- to put .them down.---cf Punch.'!</p>
      </div>
