      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL SECESSION.</head>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL SECESSION.</p>
        <p>Democratic Decision To Wait</p>
        <p>For Mandate,</p>
        <p>OFFENDING RESOLUTION UNMOVED.</p>
        <p>The Trades and Labour Council are going to act democratically- _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Although refusing to withdraw the resolution declaring that they would use ever k means in their power to oppose compulsion, they decided on Tuesday evening that before any further action be taken re compulsion the same should receive full consideration of all affiliated branches and future action be based on the inandate received. Mr. J. Shimmin (of the Postmen's Federa- tion) explained that his society had decided not to withdraw from the Council, but they asked that the latter part of the "offending resolu- tion" should be expunged—" We pledge our- selves to oppose any and every form of compul- sion to the utmost of our power, and call upon all free men to rally to the movement for its resistance." , Mr. J. Bennion (of the Co-operative Society) seconded. The Secretary (Mr. Charles Dukes) said they had not reached the end of compulsion yet, and it was wise that they should not tie themselves up. The resolution related to all kinds of com- pulsion, as they would see from the terms of it. There might be suggested compulsion for un- attested married men, or a compulsory tax on Wages. What would they do then? The Chairman (Mr. Plinston) considered there was a good deal to be said on both sidgp, and he thought the proper remedy and safeguard was that, prior to any further action being taken, a mandate should be asked for. If that were done then they did all that was necessary, but if they expunged it altogether it could be implied that their opposition to compulsion was with- drawn. - - - -- -</p>
        <p>The Secretary hoped there would be no twelfth hour repentance. A Black Spot.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shimmin reiterated that what the Post- men wanted was that no action should be taken without consulting the branches. Some of their members were of the opinion that the resolu- tion was a black spot on the books, and there was a danger of some official probably taking it into his head to do something in the matter, for as long as the resolution remained he would be perfectly right in doing so. The decision as announced above was carried without a dissentient.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Regrets, But No Secession.</head>
        <p>Regrets, But No Secession.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Co-operative Em- ployes' Society stating that, following .ft speech by the Chairman of that Council, they had agreed. not to secede from the Council, though they greatly regretted their action in not giving more notice of the business concerned, so that the delegates could have tested the feelings of the branches on the question. Engineers Withdraw.</p>
        <p>The Associated Society of Engineers sent in their resignation as a protest against the Coun- cil's action, and the three delegates (Messrs. T. Owen, H. Hewitt and Savory), who disagreed with their society, paid a tribute to the Coun- cil's work' as they sang their swan songs one after the other. . The Council were loth to lose the society, and it was agreed to send another letter asking the members or committee to receive a deputation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SCHOOL EXEMPTIONS.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL EXEMPTIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Walker And Some</p>
        <p>Recent Criticisms.</p>
        <p>Mr_ George Walker, the, representative of the Working Men's Infirmary Committee on the Education Committee, speaking at the annual meeting of the former committee last Saturday, referred to the address recently de- livered by Mr. A. Knowles, president of the Teadhers' Association. They were told, he said, that. economy had run riot and that economy in, education was one of the worst forms of economy that could occur. In War- rington, some of them, While not advocating economy,' had advocated judicious expenditure, and the fact that the Chairman of the Finance Committee had just submitted estimates show- ing that the rate for education was equal to an advance of twopence in the X, did not point to, any undue "economy" locally. Imperially, there was a large expenditure, and some of us thought we did not get full value for the money. The School Attendance. Committee were stated by the teachers to be too liberal in granting exemptions to school children before reaching the age of fourteen. That was a statement more easily made than proved, and all the circumstances must be known before an opinion was given. The committee di, not set before themselves any hard and fast line, but they kept in view the amount per head necessary to sustain a -family, and also the suitability of the employment to which the child was to be sent. If they thought the earnings of the child, however small, were necessary, to assist in the feeding of the family, they allowed it to_go to 'work. They thought that in such a case food came first and educa- tion next. The exemptions averaged about 800 a year, out of 14,000 scholars, and more than half of them were of children who had passed Standard VII, or ex-VII. Some of them held the opinioh that when a child had passed the seventh standard it was not worth while to retain it at school, because it was simply mark- ing', time. Of the others, some had not got beyond the fourth standard, and lie asked what was the use of retaining at school children so mentally dull that they could not get beyond the fourth standard •before they were fourteen? Elementary education had not answered anti- cipations; it was ime of those problems we should'perhaps be able to rescue from chaos after the war.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Fairclough said he knew from ex- perience the difficulty of ixercising one's judg- ment in applications for exemption, as a great many of 'OM came from the homes in which the greatest destitution prevailed.</p>
        <p>Irish Eloquence. . . In the Irish House of • Commons in 1795, dur- ing a debate on the leather tax, the Chancellor of the Exchequer; Sir John Parnell, observed "that in the prrecution of the present. war every man ought to be ready to give his last guinea to protect the remainder." Mr. Vandeleur said that. "however that might he, a tax on leather would press heavily on the bare- footed peasantry of Ireland." To which Sir Boyle Roche. replied that this , could be easily removed by making the under leathers of wood.</p>
      </div>
