      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>VALUE OF TIMBER.</head>
        <p>THE VALUE OF TIMBER.</p>
        <p>A sate of growing timber on Lord Harringtow's estate near Derby last week realised £2,259 for 174 trees, consisting of oak, ash, and elm. A. large and particularly fine ash tree, standing in the grounds of Elvaston Castle, was offered, and bidding rose from £4O to £BB, when it was with- drawn, Lord Harrington' reserve price being £lOO. Th 6 figure of £BB is said to be'a record.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SCHOOLBOY FARMERS.</head>
        <p>SCHOOLBOY FARMERS.</p>
        <p>Interesting suggestions as to how boys might be employed on the /and have been made to the ".liaily-praphic " by Mr. Edward Holliday. "There are thousands of boys throughout the country," he said, "who could very well dis- pense with a half-day at -School, and, as I know from experience with farming friends in Dorset and Wiltshire, their .services would‘be-of real value: They could very well look after- poultry, feed cattle and -pigs,, cut vegetables, and in many other ways help on the land. A couple of old men could .easily supervise the work of a dozen boys, and, I am convinced they would not' only be of service, hilt farmers would only loci', toe glad to employ them and pay them at the rate of at least six shillings a week. It would be good training for the lads, and I think they would suffer very little by only doing a half-day at school."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>SULKING HABIT IN HORSES.</head>
        <p>SULKING HABIT IN HORSES.</p>
        <p>This vice, like most others, is more easily prevented than cured. _ No one method can be used, because the vice results from different causes, and occurs in horses of different tera- perament. The horse which refuses to get 'up after either lying down or being thrown by hopples of various sorts is probably sulking. if a sharp slap on the muzzle and other ordinary means fail, more drastic methods must be used. Occasionally a sound whipping will get results, but this method much more frequently aggra- vates the condition and confirms the habit. The most successful method I know of is that of simply tying the horse when down in such a manner as to keep it lying flat. After being safely tied it May be left for two- or three hours. At the end of that time the horse may be untied and allowed to rise if it chooses, or if it will do so with ordinary persuasion. If it still refuses the treatment should be reapplied. 'Too frequent opportunities should not. be given to get up. The interval between these opportuni- ties should be increased -every time. During the time that a horse is lying on the ground tied it has an opportunity to become quiet if left alone, and the temper which has developed may gradually subside: Then comes a natural desire to rise ,and probably one or more efforts will be made by the horse to get up. It finds itself bound and then the untying process appeals to it as a form of aid from a human being. Such treatment first humiliates the horse, and next leads it to believe that man can be of service when it is in difficulty. After this treatment has been applied once a horse is not inclined to repeat the bad habit unless ex- tremely provoked. If it is repeated the same treatment should be applied. I'have not known of any case which two or three treatments of this sort would not correct. If the majority of horses are properly handled in breaking and training they will not develop such habits. When they are developed they are always more or less difficult to cure and uncertain Of treat- ment.—" Breeders' Gazette."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>ROSETTE COLEWORT.</p>
        <p>There is nothing to surpass this in the way ot greens. Market gardeneis -around London. plant. large breadths of it, and though by no means a novelty, it is far from being so wgil known as it deserves to be. The seed should- be sown not later than the end of Mar, and the plants put out wherever room can be found .for them. They may be given a space of 12 inches apart each Ivo, and provided that the ground is' in good heart they will in the course of the season form neat and serviceable heads...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>HUMOGEN OR BACTERISED PEAT.</head>
        <p>HUMOGEN OR BACTERISED PEAT.</p>
        <p>Th has been very much boomed of late and remarkable claims are made for its manurial value. Since first I heard of it I had very little belief in it, and recent trials have borne out what I then thought as to its value. In the report on field trials at .the Midland Agricul- tural and Dairy College, Kingston-on-Soar, it was used as a tap dressing in powder form to wheat at the, rate of 7cwt. per acre, "but pro duced no result whatever." It was also tried in its fibre form with potatoes, and in their ease "it has given entirely negative results, as in the case ef wheat." Apparently, therefore, little or no help is to be got from humogen increasing food prodnction. It has also been tried in the Royal Horticultural Gardens at Wisley, and the following is the report: "The details of its manufacture are not divulged. On this account most of the eiperiments pcissiblo with it are of an empirical nature."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>MOVING HARDY PLANTS.</head>
        <p>MOVING HARDY PLANTS.</p>
        <p>At this season, when growth is on the move, hardy herbaceous plants should, if the weather is open, be divided and replanted. Hardy plants are gross feeders, hence it,is all important that the roots should have a good depth of soil to ,which has been adtled plenty of manure. If cut flowers in qUantity are the object for which the plants are grown it is a good plan to grow in a quarter of the kitchen garden a selection of the most suitable for this purpose, so as to leave the borders furnished.—Wick, in ." Farm and home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Othitrth</head>
        <p>Othitrth</p>
        <p>'REV. T. T. VICKERS TO LEAVE.</p>
        <p>After having been in charge of St. Clement's Mission for six years, the. Rev. T. T. Vickers, Senior curate at the Warrington Parish Church, about to leave, having accepted a curacy at "le Parish Church at Liverpool, under the direction of the newly-appointed Rector, the r. G. W. Hockley. Regret will be felt at his 'departure, for his work has been greatly ap- Pre.Oated in the parish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>REV. F. LUKE WISEMAN</head>
        <p>THE REV. F. LUKE WISEMAN</p>
        <p>AT BOLD-STREET.</p>
        <p>unday was a special day at the Bold-street u'egleyan Church, for the Rev. F. Luke Wise- an ex-President of the Conference, con- -I.lleted the morning and evening services, de- ,lvering two eloquent and impressive sermons, Ln. which his main contention was that after cne war militarism must be replaced by some- Europe generally was; bankrupt of an answer, and in his view the only one which would be a satisfactory alternative was the aubordination of the nations to the doctrine of the Carpenter of Nazareth. If that were not done, other wars would follow. Special music was rendered at all,' the ser- vices, and in the afternoon a large congrega- tion listened to Dr. Gaul's cantata "The Holy City," rendered by the choir in a splendid vnanner. The various solos, duets and quartets, together with the choruses, were greatly enjoyed. Mr. W. H. Payton played the.accompaniments, and Mr. F. Hickman conducted.</p>
        <p>Th,e Mayor (Alderman P. Pearock).presided. The morning anthem was "0 Saviour of the World" (Goss), and the evening anthem "Plead Thou My Cause" (Mozart), a quartet being splendidly rendered by Miss Taylor, Mrs. Small- h;haw, Mr. Goostrey and Mr. Pearce_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>FOR NEW PREMISES.</head>
        <p>FOR NEW PREMISES.</p>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Methodists Hold</p>
        <p>Sale Of Work.</p>
        <p>In order to raise funds for the new building hind and various church expenses, the Legh- street Primitive Methodist Church held a sale of work in the Schoolroom, Legh-street, on Wednesday and Thursday. The opening cere- ni°nY on the first day was performed by Mrs. Lindley, the Rev. A. J. Chessum presiding.</p>
        <p>The reverend gentleman said that in common With all other churches they had lost many "wag men. The membership of the Church Was very small—about 125—and between them they had to raise £2OO per year to enable, them to meet their expenses. They could all realise What a big financial strain had been put on the Church, and most of the work had to be done in six months of the year. They were just a little behind with their funds, but they were infinitely better to-day than three or four yea is ago, and they had .everything possible to cheer them on. The chief aim of the sale was to get as much as pos- sible for the new building fund, for the present They was not at all suitable for their needs 'hey had already £360 towards the fund. In declaring the sale open; Mrs. Lindley said she was very pleased to be present to help them in their good work. She fully understood the many difficulties with which they had to con- tend at the present time, but hoped and felt sure that their efforts would be rewarded. (Applause.) Mr. A. E. Hoyle proposed a vote of thanks to lirs. Lindley, and the Rev. A. .1. Chessum seconded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>STALLHOLDERS</head>
        <p>STALLHOLDERS</p>
        <p>The stallholders were : Men's Stall: Mr. 11. 0. Bennett and Mk A. Ritchen. - Young Ladies' Stall: Mrs. A. E. Hoyle, Miss Hilda Spruce and Miss Whitlow. CongregEttional Stall: Mrs. Spruce, Mrs_ Snape eind Miss Crampton. Handkerchief Stall: Mrs. Atty and Miss Jones Sewing Meeting Stall: Mrs. Dutton, Mrs Chessum, Mrs. Atkinson, and. Mrs. H. 0. Ben- nett.</p>
        <p>Plower and Fruit Stall: Mrs. Kitchen anii Mrs. Mills:</p>
        <p>Refreshment Stall: Mrs. Creswell, Mrs. Worth- ington, Mrs. E. Bennett and Miss Griffiths. Bran Tub: Miss Hughes. There were various entertainments during the day, these being in charge of Mrs. Spruce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>CHILDREN'S DAY.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S DAY.</p>
        <p>Master Charles Landor presided over Thurs- thq's proceedings, and made a neat little rech, in -which he said he was glad to see so wrY present, especially boys and girls, upon A({)11 the future of the Church depended. the .. _„ pBsu Bridge, of Glazebrook, then received rbeS from the children, who have beeu collecting for the past few week'. She made a sPeeeh to the children, urging them to keep up their good. Work. Mr' A. E. Hoyle moved, and the Rev. A: J. Chessurn seconded a vote of thanks to Miss 13ridge and to the youthful Chairman. The Rev. A. J. Chessiim then announced that the takings on Wednesday realised £79 6s. 81d They hoped to reach £lOO before the close of th! sale.</p>
        <p>During the afternoon several girls very grace- tally executed a: Japanese dance, while Jessie Uratt recited in splendid style. The amount realised altogether was. £l5O.</p>
        <p>- - On each of the two evenings entertainments Were given, the artistes being Mrs. C. Worthing- ton, Mrs, A. E. Hoyle, Misses M.. and E. Jones and Mr. J. Dakin, soloists; .Mr. R. Syers, solo violin; Mrs. S. J. Hamilton and Mr. F. H Uanner, pi' noforte duettists. Humorous qems by Mr, (I. 1;3. Starkey were a-e,lompanied by Alis.; • Starkey.</p>
        <p>THE 1101 - FIRES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>'tenants' Defence League Suggested</head>
        <p>'tenants' Defence League Suggested</p>
        <p>For AX arrinol OIL</p>
        <p>Concerned with the increase • in rents •which itls taken place almost on u Ainteral scale •in .74rlington- during the past twelve months, the, :rades Council,. at their Meeting on Tuesday, I°lllted a sub-committee, which was instructed 4Vkeep its eye on everything appertaining iereto, and take steps to inaugurate a Tenants "efertee League. EsPecially now that married men are being tailed to the Colours, declared Mr. Edwin ..Plinston r(the .chairman) we shall have to see that homes in Warrington are protected. r. Seabtiry (assistant secretary) said that,. -111.0fe551ng to be only recouping themselves the.c.:tra --keg imposed, landlords in some ses had raised the rents more than double the lalount necessary, and under the Rents' Act, 'tenants were due to a return.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>TIIE MISSES HODGE !</head>
        <p>TIIE MISSES HODGE !</p>
        <p>Stretton And Appleton Women ,Asked To Work On Land.</p>
        <p>That worneli should work on farms in greater numbers in order to replace Hodge who has gone to the Front, was advocated at a meeting held in the• Stretton Schoolroom on Monday evening, presided over by Mr. H. • Beresford Melville. Others on the platform were the Hon. and Mrs. J. E. Cross, Mr. James Openshaw, and Mr. NV. Peter Rylands, while in the audience there were the Rev. and Hon. C. F. Cross, Rev. S. Thompson, Mr. W. Bainbridge, Mr. Ellison, Mr. Rose, Mr. Marsh, and Mrs. Beresford Melville.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenall wrote expressing his approval of the scheme. The Hon. J. E. Cross said that as long ago as last April he saw that if this war continued there would be a great shortage of, labour on farms. Yet these farms would have to be kept going, and in order to do that, the Board of Trade commenced a scheme for the employment of women in agriculture. County committees were formed, and that of Cheshire had as its President, Lord Newton. They set to. work in June, and had represention from all over the county. Their district -*as represented by Mrs. Cross, and they had arranged to get women trained. The latter had worked very hard, doing all sorts of work, and it was a real treat to watch them. At the schools provided for the training of the women, however, they were only allowed to remain two months, and that was not long enough, for in that time they got hardly any training at all, whereas if the farmer would take them on and himself train them, and supervise their work, the women would soon improve. By this scheme they had, since September, put well over 400 girls and women into situations. This was not a bad start (Applause.) It was essential that every bit of spare ground should be cultivated. A quarter 'of a million women at least were needed, and the different ladies on the county committees had arranged their registers for each district, and the women would be asked to give in their names.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Women's Work Repays Farmers.</head>
        <p>Women's Work Repays Farmers.</p>
        <p>When the women once enrolled, the com- mittees confidently expected that they would not give up the work. Women should not be dis- appointed if they did not get work very soon after enrolling their names. They would be a great help td the farmers, for they could do either/light or heavy work. In conclusion, Mr. Cross said that the ivar had to be won, and this could only bq done by men, munitions, money and food. Without food our armies would be useless, and it was the duty of their war com- mittee to find women to work and produce this food. If the farmers would employ the women and persevere with them, he was sure they would be amply repaid for their pains.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WOMEN WHO AMAZE.</head>
        <p>WOMEN WHO AMAZE.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Peter Rylands said that since the war 'commenced Messrs. Rylands Bros. had lost over thirty per cent, of their men, and the places of a large number of them ~ag been filled by women. In the town the women had been very quick at coming forward. They -seemed to have been. afraid of nothing, and had buckled to and done their best. It was a credit to them and their employers. Farmers should not say it was better for their men to stay at home than to go, unless they were absolutely essential. Farmers must realise that they should put up with the inconveniences. When they did commence to employ women they would.be amazed how quickly they did the work and stuck to it. They should releaKe every man they could possibly spare for the 2...TEny. Yet production must be kept up, for the moment a country's production was less than its con- sumption it was disastrous. If four million men who were producers were taken away and their places not filled, thus causing production to diminish, the country was going to ruin. That had to be met. He was happy to notice that there had been a flow of women and girls into the industrial army, and„ when the war was over and tfie. men came back, they would be able to say with pride; "Bill, I did your bit while you were away." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>THOROUGH CANVASS.</head>
        <p>A THOROUGH CANVASS.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Openshaw, himself a farmer, observed that farmers had been showing an objection to employing women on their farms, and he (the speaker) could well understand them, for the farmers had been used to employ- ing highly skilled labour, which had all now gone. How then were farmers to get along? The only thing was to employ women. He would like to see them go to work properly clothed' and shod, and looking workmanlike. He could not think that they were going to allow agriculture to lag like it had done in past years, for he was convinced that the women, once they had taken up the work, would not turn back.</p>
        <p>At the close of the meeting the Chairman an- nounced that every house in Stretton• and Apple- ton would be visited, and the scheme placed before the womenfolk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>MOTOR AMBULANCE.</head>
        <p>MOTOR AMBULANCE.</p>
        <p>Working Men Say One Is Needed For Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jos. Elwell, speaking at the annual' meeting of the Working Men's Infirmary Com- mittee on Saturday, said that when accidents occurred at the works there was sometimes considerable delay in getting the ambulance, because the police were unable to find an ivailable horse. He moved that the attention 3f the Town., Council be called to the need there was for a *motor ambulance for the use af the town.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Cooper (Monks, Hall) seconded and iaid he recently witnessed an amount of un- necessary suffering through the late arrival of :he hcrse ambulance that he wished never to witness wrair. The time had arrived when his should h- altered, and if the ton n would Acit find proper efiui;drcn^. the working men could d.o a.</p>
        <p>Mr. G., E. Knott (Rylands Bros.) supported, and said it was a scandal that in such a time of need there should be such difficulties as those which sometimes presented themselves. He thought it was better to be a little gener- ous :in these matters :rather than cause suffer- ing; an ambulance should be available when- ever it was 'called for. (Hear, hear.) it seemed a trivial uffair, and he hoped the town would not only go into the question of provid- ing the ambulance, but would put it in the hands of proper authorities and chat it would always be available• when. called by telephone. Mr. Cook agreed, and said that a motor ambulance provided by the Leeds Working Men's Committee cost £BOO, and cost about £l6 a year in repairs. _</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Wadsworth was in full sympathy with the resolution, but thought the Corporation, and not the working men, should provide the ambulance. (Hear, bear.) He thought the Fire Station would be the proper place to keep the motor ambulance. The resolution-was carried..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>4 ' THE CONCENTRATED NOURISHMENT OF BEEF.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>TILE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1916.</head>
        <p>TILE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1916.</p>
        <p>41.4.4...+4.0+44 +4,44 4•00+0,4P. 44440646+ .4•++++.44o+ ..4•4•44 .4;4 • +• + 4.) 4.4 c., +4. • 04. + 4. 4. 4. + •++• ++ • +l. 4. ":" •</p>
        <p>44. WOMAN'S WORLD 4.4. • 41,</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE'.</p>
        <p>Russian Heroines of the War.</p>
        <p>The intense patriotism of the Russian women has led to many examples of feminine heroism in the Russian Army, of which there are but few parallels on the Western Front. The Cossack women learA to fight when quite young, and many accompany their husbands to the wars. To a Russian, Western women appear weak in comparison with his own country- women, but the difference lies in the position accorded to women in the land of the Czar. There men do not claim to be the stronger sex to the same extent as in Western countries, con- sequently there are fewer divergencies between the sexes. In Most Rusqiaa families, brothers and sisters are comrades from infancy, and when older they attend the same Universities, which doubtless accounts for the decided mascu- line mentality of Russian women of good educa- tion. The Laws of the Geneva Convention.</p>
        <p>The rules and regulations of the Red Cross Society are not always fully understood even by members of the Voluntary Aid Detachments The society is bound by the laws of the Geneva Convention, to which all nationalities belong, although each has its own separate bodies of members. The laws, which are by no means intricate, are necessarily not a few in number, but every working member is made acquainted with them, in his or her own language, on join- ing the society. These laws may not be lightly disregarded, for a breach of one may lead to the whole unit being regarded as combatants The work of members is strictly confined to the nursing, aiding, and attending of sick and wounded soldiers and sailors, and when in Uniform a Red Cross nurse could not feed hungry unwounded soldiers, although she would doubtless advise them where to procure food. For this reason the V.A.D. girls should not wear their official uniform, but one improvised for the occasion, when working in canteens, at munition factories, or railway stations. ,</p>
        <p>• 4********** IC•4********** * •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>• el.' Children' B Corner. +</head>
        <p>• el.' Children' B Corner. +</p>
        <p>4:. 4. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. + •:. 00</p>
        <p>•:. • • •.•-• • +4O t...•,-.:. + ix. • 44++ 4. • • 4.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>MOST WONDERFUL INVENTION SINCE 1815.</head>
        <p>THE MOST WONDERFUL INVENTION SINCE 1815.</p>
        <p>Two Prizes This Week.</p>
        <p>.31Y DEAR CHILDREN, Considering that the subject was rather a difficult one this week, you have done remark- ably well. Some spoilt their chances by naming several inventions instead of one. Last week Heathside Boys' Schdol claimed the winner; this week Heathside Girls' School claims her. The winner is</p>
        <p>Niece Mona Archer, 11, Heathside (381), who has been previously successful in my com- petitions. She is to be specially congratulated upon her repeated success. A prize also goes to Niece Fanny Osborne,</p>
        <p>who wrote a remarkably good letter. As will be seen from the extracts I give from several of the letters sent in, my nephews and nieces have covered a wide area of invention and have made some interesting remarks.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK. Next week we will have an essay upon What I Would Do If Zeppelins Came To Warn i ngton.</p>
        <p>That should be an attractive subject and not so difficult as writing about inventions and inventors.</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>LOYAL TO INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>LOYAL TO INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Working Men's Contributions</p>
        <p>A Record In War Year;</p>
        <p>MAYOR'S SURPRISE.</p>
        <p>A most gratifying report was submitted to the annual meeting of ,the Warrington Working Men's Infirmary Committee, held last Saturday* in the Council Chamber at the Town Hail, and presided over by his Worship the Mayor (Alder- man Peter Peacock). The contributions received from the works during the year 1915 was £1,182 3s. 4d., an in- crease of £94 14s. 6d. upon the amount collected in 1914, 'although that year's figures were a record.</p>
        <p>There was received from all sources the total a 21,309 Os. sd. (including £22 3s. 6d. proceeds of the annual concert, and £4B ss. 11d, from the mugical festival and Hippodrome performance in the summer) against £1,280 ss. Bd. in 1914, or an increase of £2B 14s. 9d.</p>
        <p>The expenses were £l7 18s. 11d., leaving a balance of £1,291 is. 6d. to be disposed of, against £1,262 Iss. 9d., an increase of £2B ss. 94. Of the disposable balance, £1,275 6s. 6d. was given to the Infirmary against £1,113 9s. Bd., an increase of £l6l 16s. 10d., or, allowing for a special war fund collection of £4O in 1914, a net increased contribution to the Infirmary of £l2l 16s. 10d.</p>
        <p>Among those supporting the Mayor were Mr. J. H. Smethurst (chairman of the Infirmary Board of Management), Mr. John Fairclough, Dr. Fox, Mr. Geo. Walker (vice-chairman of the committee) and Mr. J. H. J. Hampson (hon. sec. of the committee). The Chairman (Alder- man Burton) was unable to attend, and letters of apology were received from Sir Gilbert Greenall, Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Mr. Ziba Armitage. Mr. Arthur Down. Mr. F. A. Frost and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. There was a large attendance of members of the committee.</p>
        <p>FEWER WORKMEN BUT MORE MONEY.</p>
        <p>The Mayor congratulated the committee upon the large attendance, and said their enthusiasm was fittingly reflected in the result of their year's labour. In spite of the war and the de- pletion in the ranks of the working men in town they had actually called from. the works £9l 14s. td. more than in the previous year, which the total so collected, £1,182 3s. 4d., was the largest amount ever collected by the committee. He had sought an explanation for this hand some increase in the.. circumstances, and their hon. secretary told him it was undoubtedly due</p>
        <p>MR. GEORGE WALKER. The New chairman.</p>
        <p>to• the extra exertions the committee had put forth for a noble institution they did not wish to see suffer. The Grappenhall Tanning Co. stood out in his mind as one of the best ex- amples of the way this increase had been brought about. They had increased th6r con- tribution from £5 Os. Bd. to £47 10s. sd. owing, he was told, to the active interest taken in the matter by Mr. H. Stanley. Pearson and Knowles showed a gratifying increase from £55 16s. 4d. to £lO6 10s., the Wire Ropery increased from £6 14s. to £l5 175., and so on. The amount handed over to the Infirmary was £1,275 6s. 6d., or an increase of £l6l 16s. 10d. for the year. (Ap- plause.) He must repeat that he really did not understand how the working men had achieved such a result in Rich a year. They had certainly put all their energy into the work, and their method of systematic collections had, been thoroughly justified. Since 1901 the population of the town had increased by 10,000, from 64,000 to 74,000, but their contribution to the Infirmary had increased in the same period from £742 17s 10d. to £1,275 6s. 6d., or nearly double. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>UNCLE JACK.</head>
        <p>UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>WINNING ESSAY. By Niece Mona Archer.</p>
        <p>"I think the greatest invention from 1815 to 1916 is the railway, with its engines. The inventor was George Stephenson; he was born at Newcastle. His father was a collier, earn- ing 28s. a week. Like many boys, George had no education, and when old enough he worked at the engine in the colliery. George was always examining the parts of the engine, and wished he had been educated like the inventor Watt. Pis first step to fame was the making of a lamp by him; it is still used by colliers. Before the invention many men were killed, but in inventing it George Stephenson saved many lives. He now thought of railway lines, which were put from Stockton to Darlington for waggons to go over them for trade. Stephenson then made a locomotive, which he called 'My Lord.' Railway lines were then put from Manchester to Liverpool, and on these was placed the first engine, 'The Rocket,' also invented by 'George Stephenson. "George won £5O for the wonderful invention of 'The Rocket.' After this trains were made to carry people and the old stage-coach wa.s quite forgotten. We owe great thanks to George Stephenson for his invention of engines; it is one of the quickest ways of travelling. This famous man died in 1848. The first engine was made in 1830."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>BICYCLES AND SEWING MACHINES.</head>
        <p>BICYCLES AND SEWING MACHINES.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the most interesting letter received, although it did not name, in my opinion, the most " wonderful " invention, came from Niece Fanny Osborne, 31, Leicester-qtreet,</p>
        <p>who is one of my newest members. It is so good. in fact, that I shall send her a prize equal to the first. Her, letter is an exact model of the letters I require. Here it is:— The Most Wonderful Invention from 1815 to Now, and Who Invented It.</p>
        <p>FANNY 0811011 NE, 31, Leicester-street, Evelyn- street School, age 13, No. 753. " Deal TxCie Jack,—l. did not like this sug- gestion very much; for I thought. it was more for boys, but, of course, you know it was a boy who chose it. 'Anyhow. I will do my best. The first .thing I ii ill mention is the Hobby or Dandy Horse, the forerunner of the modern bicycle,. which was introduced into England in 1818. The most funny part of this bicycle was that it. had no pedals, but they .were added about 1866, and the bicycle was finished off by Dunlop in 1888. Ile also invented the pneu- matic tyre, and the introductien of tns free- wheel and two anti. tree-speed gears that finally, gave us the biepie 0; to-day. " The next thing is the sewing machine, which was constructed by Thimmonier, who started a faetory, in- which 112,31-..7, these machims were employed. They wizr founded in 1830, and made much bettnn 1851, by Singer; and that was wher: fnger's Sewing Machinp gets its name to-day. Now, I think those are my mos‘ wonderful inventions. —T remain, • "FANNY OSBORNE."</p>
        <p>OTHER GOOD LETTERS.</p>
        <p>Many other good letters were received, from which I make the following selections : Niece Mabel Langley, 178, Padgate:lane</p>
        <p>"In 18/5 a number- Of clever working men invented machines for spinning and weaving cotton and wool. The towns became much larger and clever men began to make big buildings called mills. James Watt made a ,steam engine, which was of great use. A great number of these were soon fixed up in different parts of the country. " The most wonderful inventions from 1815 to 1916 are aeroplanes and wireless telegraphy." , • Niece Phyllis Lightfoot, 4, Dudley street, although only pine years of age, nearly de- prived Ileathside of providing the winner of the prize by herself, a Beamont School girl,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>A 0. , •</p>
        <p>The Season's Coatings. Covet coating has so firmly established itself, and been so much improved since it first mad.. its appearance in the tailoring department, that it bids fair to rival,, navy serge in popularity. Hitherto coats and skirts of this material have been made in the simplest styles possible, but of late tailors have introduced various. trim- mings in contrasting braids, embroideries, and soutache. Collars and vests of white pique and of various coloured silks, are also used. This season's coatings are in very attractive shades, ranging from yellow to brown, among which are shades tinged with green. Navy and black serges are combined this -spring, as they were last season, with taffetas, but with a difference. Skirts are composed of alternate flounces, four in number, of the two materials, for afternoon frocks, and frills are used also on the bodices. The combination is not, of course, new, but the present modes admit of a more pleasing adapta- tion of the idea than hitherto Embroideries of soutache are quite a feature of this spring's serge tailored suits. Sometimes motifs of this embroidery are effectively used on a deep hem of serge, and are placed some distance apart.</p>
        <p>Fashionable Spring Colours. The long taffetas coat will be very much in evidence this spring and summer. It is shown in many styles, and the trimmings are equally various. When the cost is a matter of no im- portance, such a garment is not an extrava- gance, because it can be worn over an old- fashioned dress, or with its help fewer dresses will be necessary. Some models are trimmed with ruchings, frayed in the old-world way at the edges. Naturally the skirts of all styles are very full. The cape-coat in silk is a variant or the coat-frock, the cape effect being obtained by means of a deep tuck; this is quite a picturesque garment and looks well in gab cord. This spring, purchasers of new clothes will require to study carefully the colours as well as the materials they select. Blacks and greys of last year are to give place to bright colours, but in soft and harmonious shades. Greens, blues an I pinks will be the prevailing tints, tans and greys ranking next in favour. Mack and white checks are always worn at this time of year. brown and white and grey and white trimmed with coloured collars and cuffs are also popular.</p>
        <p>winning it. She chose the railway engine as the most wonderful invention of the last hun- dred years. The railways have been "so im- proved that we cannot do without them now, because they .can take passengers from• one end of the country ,to the other in a very few hours. They also bring most of our food-stuffs and coal and the "mails."</p>
        <p>Niece Laura "Clayton .13, Guardian-street, Heathside School.—"l think the most famous invention since 1815 is the wireless telegraphy. lt was invented by Marconi, whose mother was ,an English woman and whose father was an Italian."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harold Brown, 40, WeMeld-street, Heathside School, says the greatest invention is the gun that carries fifteen miles, though he thinks the venen who fire them are the people to be prsed. Nephew Sam Brown, 40, Wellfield-street, Heathside School, names several inventions, and, of course, I asked for only one—the most wonderful. The most famous, he says, is the steel warship which guards our shores. Be also names submarines, the railway opened for passenger traffic between Stockton and Dar- lington in 1825, George Stephenson being the engineer; and wireless telegraphy, invented by Marconi in 1895, when 22 years of age; first message sent arcoss,,the Atlantic in 1901. Nephew Joseph Jeavons, 9, Elizabeth-street.— "The most famous invention is the 'Zeppelin, made by Count Zeppelin. The Zeppelin is about two hundred feet long and carries twenty-I two men, and is very difficult to hit by our airmen. Another famous invention is the wireless telegraph invented by Edison." Nephew William Jeavons, 9, Elizabeth-street, Arpley-street School.—" I think the most won- derful invention is the French '75.' When it is fired it moves round like a machine-gun. "The next is the torpedo net, which is made somewhere in England,' The net hangs from the side .of the Dreadnought and is a great safeguard to our sailors and soldiers. When a torpedo is fired from a submarine it strikes the net and causes the torpedo to be a dead one."</p>
        <p>Nephew Robert Smart, 41, Price-street, Heathside School. thinks the most famous in- vention is wireless telegraphy. It was in- vented by Marconi. Its main use is for the communication of messages over the sea- If a ship meets with an accident in mid-ocean an- other ship can know in a very short space of time through this wonderful invention. Niece Florence Smith, 18, Ellis-street, gives submarines, aeroplanes, trams, motor cars and bicycles, the last-named being much appre- ciated by workpeople and errand boys, who get muck quicker to the places they want to reach.</p>
        <p>Niece Annie. Massey, Sandy-lane, Lymm, Oughtrington School.—" But for the penny post introduced by Sir Rowland Hill we should not have been able to send away letters. Then there is another great inventor, Stephenson, who invented the engine called 'The Rocket,' in the year 1829. It ran from Stockton to Darlington. There is another great inventor, called . Count Zeppelin, who invented the Zeppelin, which is very large and dangerous." Niece M. Brown, 35, Forshaw-street, Bearnont School, names wireless telegraphy. The man who invented it was Marconi, who had it named after himself. _ _ _</p>
        <p>. Niece Louisa Holden, 35, Wellfield-street, Evelyn-street School.—George Stephenson's steam engine.</p>
        <p>"WHAT A LOVELY BOOK !"</p>
        <p>Nephew Robert Smart writes:— " Dear Uncle Jack,—Just a line to let you know how pleased I am with my prize. Every- body I have shown it to has said 'What a lovely 'book !'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WHY ENVELOPES ARE SCARCE.</head>
        <p>WHY ENVELOPES ARE SCARCE.</p>
        <p>May I again say that it is not necessary to use envelopes to send letters to Uncle Jaelc: If letters are folded up neatly and addressed on the back to me at "The. Examiner" Office, that will be quite sufficient and will save the Country so many envelopes. Do you know why envelopes are so scarce and dear? There are several reasons, but the principal one is this: There are now about five million men serving their King away from home. Each one has a letter from home and each one sends a letter home, say, once a week. I am sure every man write* more than one letter a week, and he will receive more than one from all his friends. But say only one letter goes each way every week, that is 10,000,000 envelopes a week that two years ago would not have been needed. Machinery is being erected where possible to deal with this extra sudden demand, and in the meantime we must use as few envelopes as possible. Every envelope saved is 'a service to the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>VERY WELCOME LETTER.</head>
        <p>A VERY WELCOME LETTER.</p>
        <p>' "99, Kimberley-street, Warrington. "Dear Uncle Jack,—My mother sends The Examiner' to my Dadda In France every week, and I look at the Children's Corner. My brother Ernest and I wish to join your Corner very much. We both go to St. Barnabas' School. My age is 12 and my brother is 10.—Your loving niece, I§ABELLA SANKEY." Very pleased to hear from you, Isabella, and hie you will ile one of our active members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>760 William Jeavons, 9, Elizabeth-street, nine rears of age; Arpley-street School. 761 Ellen Mitchell, 20, Dolman's-lane, Bridge- street; 12 years of age; St. Vary's School, Buttermarket-street. 762 Florence Gallo!), 20, Leonard-street; 10. 763 Thomas L. Hatch, 3, Thynne-street; 12; Warrington Sedbndary School. • 764 Stanley Hulse, 33, Fairclough's-avenue; 10; Parochial School. 765 Itabella Sankey, 99, Kimberley-street; 'l2. St. Barnabas'.</p>
        <p>701 'ErnPct Fankeyt 90. Uimberley-street; 10; St. 13ariut Das'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>INFIRMARY SUPPORT MAINTAINED.</head>
        <p>INFIRMARY SUPPORT MAINTAINED.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Smethurst added his congratula- tions to those of the Mayor upon the success of the committee's efforts on behalf of the Infirm- ary during the year. They thought that the many calls upon the generosity of *the public would cause the Infirmary to suffer; but. he 'was thankful to say i not only had it not suffered in their committee, but it had not suffered to any great extent in any other direction. That the money they raised was wisely spent was guaranteed so long as they had a careful man like Mr. John Fairclough as chairman of the House Committee, while their representatives, Mr. Daintith and Mr. Wadsworth, had also looked after the spending of the money they raised with so much difficulty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ALDERMAN BURTON'S fIfIIfIEMENT.</head>
        <p>ALDERMAN BURTON'S fIfIIfIE- MENT.</p>
        <p>"Fributes To His Work And Worth.</p>
        <p>The Mayor said they were all very sorry to learn that Alderman Burton, who had given so lavishly of his time and ability to the Infirmary through that committee, felt that the time bad come for him to retire from the chairmanship. (Hear, hear.) Alderman Burton was their treasurer 1897-99; vice-chairman 1893-96; and chairman ever since. He had loved the work, and his chairmanship had been a most success- ful one. (Hear, hear.) He had had the valu- able assistance of their friend, Mr. Geo. Walker, who was never happy unless he was working in some good cause—(hear, hear)—but at the moment they must conceptrate their thoughts on their retiring chairman, and he moved.that:— • •</p>
        <p>• "The Working Men's Infiimary Committee receive with deep regret the announcement of the resignation by Alderman Thomas Burton, J.P. of the position ot chairman of the com- mittee, which he has held since the end of March. 1907.</p>
        <p>"They desire to place on record their high appreciation of the valuable services rendered by him since the formation of the committee, and of the courtesy and impartiality displayei by him when presiding over their meetings, and in his intercourse with the members.</p>
        <p>"The committee sincerely trust that he may long be spared to his family and to the insti- tution, which he has so faithfully served for over 40 years."</p>
        <p>" I think," concluded the Mayor, "that this resolution covers all our thoughts." (Applause.) Mr. Geo. Walker iteconding, said that Alder man Burton was known personally to every member of the committee; it ,was in fact his in- timate and personal acquaintance, anti his consequent influence with them, that had been the foundation of the success of the committee. (Hear, hear.) They did not wish to lose the services of the Alderman's personality, and had induced him for several years to withhold his resignation, but he now insisted on resigning in justice' to himself and the committee. He (the speaker) had worked with Mr. Burton since 1885 on that committee; and he cordially seconded the resolution.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Shaw, a Monks, Hall representa- tive, said those who knew, Alderman Burton as a works manager and also as-chairman of that committee, had special cause to regret that the</p>
        <p>time had arrived - when he felt bound to relinquish his post. He would like the occasion to be marked with something more than a reso- lution. (Hear, hear.). Mr. Smethurst handed over a resolution passed by the Infirmary Board of Management. handsomely illuminated, and framed in gilt which he asked the committee to convey to Alderman Burton. The resolution ran:—</p>
        <p>"The Infirmary .and Dispensary, Warring- ton.</p>
        <p>"To Alderman Thomas Burton, J.P , War. rington.</p>
        <p>"The Board of Management at their meeting to-day heard with great 'egret that riu had retired from the chairmanship of the IN orkia„; Men's Committee of this institution, which you had filled for nearly nine years, and resolve.l to tender in this manner the appreciation of the services you -have rendered, not only in that position, but also as a member of this Board for very, many years. "They sincerely trust that your health may be restored and that they may for a long time have the benefit of your influence and advice.— Yours- faithfully,</p>
        <p>"JAMES HENRY SMETHURST, Chairman, "J H. T. HAMPSOI%. Secretary. "February 2s, 1916." Mr. Geo. Walker accepted the gift, and ex- pressed Alderman Burton's appreciation of it. Mr. Hampson, as hon. secretary, expressed regret at the resignation ot Akterman Burton, whom he had always found to be a thorough man, and with whom it had always been a pleasure to work.</p>
        <p>THE POOR PUBLIC-HOUSE COLLECTIONS. Mr. Geo. Walker moved the adoption of the accounts submitted by the Hon. Secretary, and said it was most gratifying to find the employes of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles taking their proper, place in the list again. (Hear, hear.) Mr. R. 4 Cook (Pearson and Knowles) seconded, and drew attention to the fact that only £4 2s. 10d. had been collected in the public-houses, a decrease of ET 10s. At Leeds the collections from the same source had increased, from £6BO to £730. The Leeds committee also raised £BOO by means of a gala.</p>
        <p>The vote was carried, and thanks to Mr. Cook and his colleagues at Pearson and Knowles were added.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>DR. FOX AND INFIRMARY WORK. Difficulties Of The Time.</head>
        <p>DR. FOX AND INFIRMARY WORK. Difficulties Of The Time.</p>
        <p>Dr. Fox congratulated the committee upon having raised this handsome sum, which was equal to a fifth of the expenditure of the Infirm- ary, and enabled them to do work • which but for the contribution would have to be left un- done. Medical work done by the Union, Insur- ance Committee, the Health Committee and</p>
        <p>a</p>
        <p>Education Committee was paid for, without difficulty, out of the rates; the Infirmary was the one institution, which perhaps did more than all the others put together for suffering humanity, which was dependent upon public charity, and he was proud to think the wort had not been diminished for want of funds. By a careful consideration of the question of instru- ments and drugs the expenditure at the Infirm- ary in this one department .had been reduced from £l,OOO a year to £6OO last year—from one• third to one-ninth of the total expenditure. -Ready For Zepps And Other Reptiles.</p>
        <p>Last year the Infirmary dealt with 12,000 people, so that one-sixth of the total population received some benefit from the institution. They had placed 48 beds at the disposal of the mili- tary authorities, and had treated 74 soldier- patients, with not a single death. The accom- modation in this ward was second to none, and as the inilitary hogpitals were equal to taking all their cases at present the ward would be available if we had a visit from Zeppelins or other reptiles of that class. They could accom- modate 50 patients without disturbing the ordinary business of the Infirmary. Their in- patients numbered over 1,000, of whom 22 were received in a dying condition, there having been 32 deaths among the other patients. They had nearly 1,000 operations, many of them of a serious character, including 44 for appendicitis, and only six deaths. Home patients had num- bered 1,300 against a thousand last year; out- patients numbered 5,000; and accidents attended numbered 5,000 as against 4,000 in the previous year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Must Be Relieved Of Trivial Cases.</head>
        <p>Must Be Relieved Of Trivial Cases.</p>
        <p>The coming year gave the`medical staff and the management cause for anxiety. The staff was constantly being depleted by the needs of\ the war. While they were determined to keep the ordinary work of the Infirmary going they thought children suffering from their throats and noses !night be provided for by the Educa- tion Authority, as was done in many other places in the country—more pressing work demanded the httention of the Infirmary. Another way in which the Infirmary could be assisted was in the matter ckf accidents. Many of the Minor accidents might be attended by the capable first- aid men in the works, especially at night, and then sent on for treatment at the Infirmary the following morning. They were willing to do their best, but if the nearest doctor was sent for in the first instance it would help their small staff very much indeed. Serious and important eases the Infirmary would eontinu to attend. There was one other exception—children suffer- ing from defective vision in the out-patients' department. Those cases, like the throat and nose eases, should be undertaken by the Educa- tion Authority. as they were undertaken by two-thirds of the Education Authorities of the country. (Hear, hear.) If it was thought by the working men that they had cause for com- plaint he asked them to investigate, but to give the Infirmary staff credit for wishing to do all they possibly could ! the honour of the institution was as dear to him, as he was sure it was, to them. (Applause.) The motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>NEW CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>THE NEW CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>The Mayor was re-elected president, and Alderman Burton was elected the first vice- president. Mr. J. Elwell moved that Mr. Geo. Walker be appointed chairman of the committee; and Mr. Fairclough (who had taken the chair on the departure of the Mayor to fulfil another engage-</p>
        <p>The following were re-elected to represent the committee on the Board of Management of the Infirmary :—Messrs. S. Walker, W. Daintith, T. Wadsworth, A. C. Binks, W. E. Clegg and R. Cook. Messrs. Daintith and Wadsworth were re- appointed on the 'House Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>FENNINGS'</head>
        <p>FENNINGS'</p>
        <p>THE GREAT PREVENTIVE &amp; CURE OF FEVERS &amp; INFECTIOUS DISEASES.</p>
        <p>Read FENNINGS' SORE THROAT "Everybody's Doctor," FiEvER sent free on application to , ALFRED FENNINGS. WITH ONE DOSE. Cowes, I.W. SOLD EVERYWHERE IN BOTTLES AT is. 3(1. EACH, ..,. - WITH FULL DIRECTIONS. 4 *</p>
        <p>CURED</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>HEADS COVERED WITH R INGWORMS.</head>
        <p>HEADS COVERED WITH R INGWORMS.</p>
        <p>Striking Triple Cure By Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>Every mother of a school-going family should read how Zam-Buk cured Mrs. Mitchtll's three children of ringworms. Mrs. S. Mitchell, who lives at 14, Young- street, Custom House, E., said to a London reporter:—" I noticed a lot of scurf on the crown of my 4-year-old child Gracie's head. A similar patch then came at the back, where a ring appeared on ,the scalp. The dry scurfy patches spread all over the girl's head. Eventually, red rings broke out, and the scalp became raw and angry. The sore surface dis- charged Unpleasant-smelling matter. Gracie wasi very restless owing to the severe irritation. She had to have her head all bandaged up. "My daughter Alice (aged 7) was next sent from school, with the same ailment. In ~her case it began on her forehead with little scurfy patches, which eventually broke into sores. Both girls had to have their hair cut off. Gertrude (aged 12) also had a ringworm on the crown of her head. The condition of the two younger children in particular caused me great anxiety.</p>
        <p>"I got some ringworm ointment, but it proved a total failure. A prescription given by a rela- tive was also without good effect. Another ointment likewise failed to cure the obstinate ringworms. After reading about Zam-Buk's many cures in bad cases of this complaint, I made up my mind to try the balm. "Alice was the first to experience the bene- fit of Zam-Buk. With the • very first applica- tion I noticed that Zam-Buk allayed the inflam- mation. The scabs were softened by the balm, and the raw, itchy surface underneath was cleansed and covered with healthy new skin. Her hair then grew splendidly. Incredible as it may seem, I was able to send Alice, back to school in just over a fortnight. "The teacher was surprised, and wanted to know how Alice had been cured so quickly, so I told her about Zam-Buk. I followed up the Zam-Buk treatment in the cases •of Gertrude and Gracie, with equally splendid results. There is no doubt that Zam-Buk saved Ger- trude's lovely head of hair, for which she was awarded a prize last summer. Gracie's ring- worms had been specially obstinate, but even they had to yield to Zam-Buk."</p>
        <p>I</p>
