      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</head>
        <p>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</p>
        <p>Notices of births, marriages, and deaths, in memoriam notices, and acknowledgments of sympathy are inserted in "The Examiner" at the, following rates:—Not exceeding 20 words, 1/6 each; every additional- seven words, 6d.</p>
        <p>, . Marriages. HAMBLET—HOTCHKISS.—On the 16th inst., at Mornington-road Wesleyan Church, Southport. by the Rev. A. J. Morton, of Padgate, John Hamblet, of Summerfield, Padgate, to Alice, youngest daugh- ter of the late Wm. Hotchkiss, of Lindley Drive Farm, and Mrs. Hotchkiss, Bishops Castle, Shropshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Deaths.</head>
        <p>Deaths.</p>
        <p>BALL.—On. March 13th; at 2, Price-street, Henry</p>
        <p>--Ball, aged 54 years. - CADE..-7On March 14th, at 70, Buttermarket-street,</p>
        <p>--Robert Cade, aged 77 years. CLARKE.—On March 14th, 1916, at the Sanatorium, Brighton, Gunner Norman Clarke, No. 117,277, Royal Field Artillery, of Field House, Broomedge, Lymm, aged 22 years. DOWN.—Elizabeth Down, Undercliffe, Stockton Heath, Warrington. Born 7th April, 1840; died 13th March, 1916.</p>
        <p>LAWTON.—On March 14th, at 9, Tilley-street, • Thomas Lawton, aged 59 years. MORRIS.—On March 14th, at 74, Orford-avenue, Hannah Morris, aged 83 years. WHALLEY.—March 17, at Hermanside, Hale, Cheshire, John Reuben Whalley, formerly of Mill Green, Bold. Funeral at Whitley Church, Monday, 3 o'clock.</p>
        <p>In Memoriam.</p>
        <p>IMMPHR.IES.=In lov./1.91 pipmory. of. 'C,n-pora:</p>
        <p>Humphries (Reggie), killed in aclion in France, March 16th, 1915. Farewell, dear dead, farewell, though far thou sleep, And o'er thy lowly grave. we may not weep, The hearts that love thee in the homeland here Are mourning thee to-day, deep and sincere.</p>
        <p>FUNERALS BY J. MOUNFIELD &amp; SONS. The Best in all Grades. Office: lb, Church- street. Tel. 99.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1916.</p>
        <p>CONSCIENCES AND CONSCIENCES.</p>
        <p>We have an apology' to make t 6 a respected correspondent who recently sent us a publica- tion to read in order that we mitt see and understand the position of those ' who, 'in various ways, oppose themselves to war and object to take any part in it. Frankly, .we have not rfad the paper; we have, as a matter of fact, no time to read abstract theories at this juncture. Ordinarily we are against war and object to fighting; but these are not ordinary times. As a matter of fact,, the reason why we have not been able to find time to read our friend's literature is that we are more than fully occupied dealing with the most dreadful actualities. War, with its awful. results, crowds our columns to bursting point every week. We see our neighbours returning to the hospitals con- tinually, maimed for .life; we are _frequently advised that other neighboues who went away in the full bloom or early manhood have been buried in a cemetery "Somewhere.' We hear that bombs are being dropped not "some- where," but on English towns, and that men, women and children are being slaughtered as they were slaughtered on the Lusitania. Last week "The Times" reproduced a letter from a German soldier to a. German maiden he was wooing. What were this youth's methods of .approach? The usual endearments? No. In his eyes his chief reermendation was Ithe number of French- men he had bayonetted and the number of Frenchwomen and girls he had served in like manner. Here is his most eloquent passage:</p>
        <p>Dear Grete Mayer, I will arrange the ring so finely that you will be able to wear it on your arm at once and you have a nice souvenir from a German warrior who has been through everything from the start and has shot and bafpnetted so many Frenchmen and I have altsii:lnyongtted many French-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>That the recent drinking Order is having a desirable effect in Warrington may be judged from the fact that during the last four weeks only twenty persons have been convicted, as against thirty in the four weeks immediately prior to its coming into operation.</p>
        <p>Order in the town is admittedly improved, and cases of semi-drunkenness are rarer.</p>
        <p>A little band of Warrington Benedicts who attended the Manchester meeting which brought into being the National Union ot Attested Married Men have since been doing some quiet propaganda work, and they hoPe to establish a. branch here.</p>
        <p>With this object in view, a meeting is to be held in the Co-operative Hall on Wednesday, and I should not ,be surprised if this latest addition to the extensive family of unions receives considerable support.</p>
        <p>Their desires are: the systematic revision of the decisions of the Tribunals in. reference to single men; the " unstarring" of single un- sjdlled men; a better allowance for married men when called up; . and also that unattested married men shall be brought under the .3ifilitary Service Act.</p>
        <p>Those who want to have a clear understand- ing of the position of married and single men in Warrington cannot do better than read the speech which Mr. C. E. Parker,n.member 6f the Advisory Committee, delivered to the Liberal Association on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>Fancy Mr. J. A. Seddon speaking under the aegis -of the Liberal and Conservative parties, with the Mayor presiding! But on March 20th that gentleman uill do so, in the Parr Hall. .</p>
        <p>, The fact is that for several months Air. Seddon has been travelling in the United States and France, addressing meetings Of working men and others, and, telling them the story of England's glory.</p>
        <p>His mission, undertaken at the request of a member, of the Government, brought hini into -contact with all classes, from President Wilson downwards, so we may expect an inter- esting and profitable evening.</p>
        <p>I have attended both of the annual meetings of the political organisations in Warrington, that of the Conservative Association being held last week and that of the Liberal Association on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>Both of them kept the truce admirably, re- fraining from the usual party doctrines; in fact, the party politician is at present suffering gladly from self-imposed tonsilitis; he does not writ to speak on party politics and has locked all his pamphlets in the cupboard.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parker just 'saved himself at the Liberal Meeting. He had finished his address proper, and then stood speechless for a moment or two. ,Friends continued to look expectantly and then, hurriedly taking his seat he remarked amidtt laughter, "I was wondering whether to say something about Free Trade, but I won't."</p>
        <p>While at the Liberal Club I saw a novel souvenir of the war. The ,percussion end of a British eighteen pounder shell had been bored and a small clock inserted into it.. The shell- had then been placed inside a nickel-plated German stirrup, and, spaced regularly round the top of the shell were: four different cartridges of combatant nations. It was most unique and even artistic.</p>
        <p>These, I may add, have been broiight from France.by Mr: J. H. Knowles, school attendance officer, who has been discharged owing to his</p>
        <p>Tleard. after; Tuesday's Tribunal.—Conscien- tious objector: "I object to, fighting, but I should like to have five minutes, with Alderman Tinnion!" F suppose the Alderman had been asking a few of his queer questions for querulous customers. ,</p>
        <p>By the officials of the• Tribunal, conscientious objectors are now known as the C.O.'s. Walt: until they come under the thumb of the O.C. (officer commanding)!</p>
        <p>A lady delegate to the Infirmary Working Men's Committee was the novelty witnessed at the annual meeting of that body last Saturday.</p>
        <p>The lady working man was welcomed by the Mayor, who invited her to come from the back of the Council Chamber to a front seat, remark- ing: "This is the first time we have been honoured by a lady at our meetings. I hope the number will increase, and that they will take</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>• Mik,.-:•t'.4,1..N=1</head>
        <p>• Mik,.-:•t'.4,1..N=1</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATUR,DAY, MARCH is, 1916.</p>
        <p>an active share -in the work of the committee.' 1. EARLY CLOSING The remark was received withi approval., ' •</p>
        <p>"The waster, deserves to want; his wickedness is great; he 'sins grievously against God anu man." I ag,ree with the Rev. A. M. .Mitchell of Burfonwood, who writes as above in his "Parish Magazine"..for March. The sins under. the head of waste in Warrington at the present time, are Many and nOtorious.. The reckoning day has yet to come.</p>
        <p>"Why not try -good, -honest, 'straightforward 'Vegetarian Jumble Pie ,With it no mystery is connected. The non-flesh eater knows his diet and goes in no fear of mystery dishes as do other tolk."</p>
        <p>So says the Same reverend gentleman. " Jumble " pie has a sinister sound, but the real mystery about vegetarian diet is how it succeeds in satisfying some people. I remember attending a vegetarian banquet once, and at the close a fellow journalist said, "We will go home now for something to eat."</p>
        <p>Gossip from the trenches: "A chum of mine who came back from leave a few days ago was looking very down in the mouth. Seeing him thus I thought he was downhearted over his leaving home again. But I found I was wrong. ", I asked him what was the matter. 'Oh,' he said, when I got home I met a lady friend of mine and, to swank, I started speaking French to her. When I had finisjaecl she said, Oh, Sidney, how sudden! We can get married on Friday.' And now :I only get a tanner a day.' , .</p>
        <p>- "Poor chap, he has got another war on when this One is over."—" (Mort] PariAl Magazine." VIGILANT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>LYMM TRIBUNAL.</head>
        <p>LYMM TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>Two Of Three Sons To Enlist. At Lymm Tribunal last night three brothers appealed-as being indispensable to their father, a farther, on the grounds that he had 62 acres of land; five.horses and 50 milking stock. • Two brothers were refused and the applica- tion of the other wit.- postponed until October. EXEMPTION FOR FARMER. A farmer appealed, stating that be. was the Itenant of a 'farm occupying 86 acres, and had 17 milking stock. ! Total exemption.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Concert In Aid Of Local War</p>
        <p>Funds.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Musical "society's conclud- ing concert of the season, in Parr Hall on Wednesday evening, had variety and a distinct note of Cheerfulness as its principal features. Yet in the two choral songs, "The Last Post" and "The Eve of Waterloo," there was a con- trasting, and timely strain of ;sadness which 'served the double purpose of reminding one of the fateful days through which we are passing - and of , providing a sombre background for the lighter items in the programme. • To the society's choral members the two short works just mentioned presented little in the way of difficulty except that arising from a great lack of male voices—an unavoidable defect for which great allowances mutt be made. Although Sir Charles Villiers Stanford's work has been heard before at .these concerts under more favourable circumstances as regards the balance of the society's choral voices, it has certainly never been sung by members with a ,deeper insight into its meaning and a more intelligent realisation of its emotional possibilities than was the case on Wednesday evening. Orchestral , members, too, combined to bring out to the full that consistent breadth and largeness of idea whiqh, while highly characteristic of the music, gives to the whole work its intense vigour and great declamatory power. The playing of the bugle-call by Lance-Corporal H. Stanley deserves a special meed of praise. It was a little disappointing that, despite the fact that Mr. H. J. Taylor's "Eve . of Waterloo." was, on the whole, well performed, '•this • comparatively new Work did not altogether realise the promise of a moving performance' which an examination of it holds out, and it was noticeable that the audience clearly failed to grasp the full import of the combined story and musical setting. A probable explanation is to be found in- the ever-changing character of the incidente embodied in Byron's verse and the restlits.sness which seems inevitable in any music which attempts the due portrayal of these incidents. A more 'intimate acquaintance with this choral ballad is, we imagine, all that is necessary fully to appreciate the series of graphic tone-pictures it .presents—tone-pictures in which a strong element, of descriptiveness, and an intense draM.atic feeling are apparent. • With the Part-songs which made up the remainder of the choral contributions to the programme, former musical society concerts have made us familiar almost to the point of wearisomeness. "The, .World's llay Queen" was-a little unsteady in the early part, and the entry of .the sparse male voices at the be- ginning was unpleasantly contrasted with that of a large body of trebles and ,altos closely afterwards. Moreover,' it waS not until towards .the end • of the triple-time section that intona- tion became true and a full degree of sonorous- ness was attained. 'lf the society must have something .-.liumorons .to enliven - its -practices and public • --Performances there are surely 'plenty of things of this kind to choose from besides "King Arthur," a theme which mem- bers have. now well-nigh worn • threadbare. "Beauteous Morn," too, a lovely mnsical gem for female voices though it is, is not 'without compeers to which attention might well be turned. And, if Bishop's good Old,time choruses and glees are in favour, many others by this composer might be suggested as a change froth ".Now tramp o'er moss .and fell." The tenors and basses performed prodigies in the way of making up for their paucity of numbers by energetic and full-voiced, singing- alike in the two choral songs and in these lighter pieces, and it was .with great credit to herself that Miss Elsie Hewitt, One of the society's members, sang the solo part in "Now tramp." The instrumental soloists in the persons of Miss Kathleen Daly (solo violin) and Mr. jOseph Greene (solo pianoforte) proved to be highly. capable musicians. Miss ,Daly is not by any means at the zenith of her powers. Her tone, while not great in volume, is singularly pure in quality 4- and her • double-stopping excellent. Possessing distinct temperamental advantages allied with marked executant facility Miss Daly's future is full of the promise of a distinguished maturity. In. the "Introduction and rondo capriccioso" (Saint , Saens), the " Witches'....Dance (Bazzini),—and Dvorak's ever-welcome "Humoresque," one had a repletion of gdod things at Miss Daly's</p>
        <p>hands. Mr. Greene is, too, a young musician of highly-gifted attainments. With a style already so -markedly individualistic and with so pronounced a command of technique it needs little prescience to forecast for him a brilliant futpre as the outcome of Study assiduously Pursued. Mr. Greene's playing of the Liszt Legende St. Francis walking on the waters"— and • the three ,pieces by ilachmaninoff, Scriabine, and Chopin, is no small test ,of the qualities of a pianoforte exponent, yet it was through such an ordeal that he came with high distinction. MiSs Lucy Nuttall's songs ,were rendered in that well-rounded contralto voice and in that' steady tone which one has come to associate with her singing. In such songs as "Ship that pass in the night," and "A Home Song" her style is soniewhat stolid and lacking in expression', although, on the other hand, she was able to impart a most sympathetic degree of animation to two encore ditties' of another class, "Keep on hoping.", (Maxwell) and You'll get heaps e' lickin's" (Clarke), both of which immensely pleased the audience. Sir Edward Elgar's recen* masterpiece—the " Carillon " music accompanying the, recital of Cammaert's poem "Sing LBelgians; sing !" proved a great success, though one that must he qualified; when judged as a whole, owing to the greatly exaggerated . gesture and manner adopted by Miss Nuttall in speaking the Words.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crossley did loyal service throughout the evening in pioneering the society's united forces and in conducting the orchestral pieces', which included the "Raymond" overture and a National Fantasia, "Our Empire." Mr. W. H. Payton, played the very onerous pianoforte parts and the bells in "Carillon," and Mr. R. H. Pearson was at his usual post as the capable leader of the orchestra.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
