      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Market To Close At 10 O'clock On Saturdays.</head>
        <p>Market To Close At 10 O'clock On Saturdays.</p>
        <p>TRAMS TO CEASE EARLIER.</p>
        <p>A distinct step forward has been made (hiring the week in the proposed early Closing reform in Warrington. We understand that a petition is being largely signed by the shopkeepers of the town, who are almost unanimously in favour of the earlier closing, calling upon the Markets Committee to close the Market earlier 'on Saturdays., in which case they will fall into line. In the meantime, however, the Markets Committee have acted. On Monday they de- cided to submit a new bylaw to the Local Gov- , ernment Board for their approval, this bylaw providing the following:—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>HOURS FOR THE MARKET.</head>
        <p>HOURS FOR THE MARKET.</p>
        <p>During the summer months, from May 1 to September 30.7—Wednesday and, Saturday, open at 6 a.m.; every .Other day, 8 a.m. During the winter months, from October 1 to April 30.—Wednesday and Saturday, °poi at 7 a.m.; every other day at 8 a.m. • 'Throughout the whole year the Market will close :—Wednesday,. at 8 p.m.; Saturday at 10 ; Thursday, 1- p.m.; other days, 7 p.m. It will take about a month to get this bylaw passed by the London authorities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>CARS AND 'BUSES.</head>
        <p>CARS AND 'BUSES.</p>
        <p>• It has been decided by the Tramways Com- mittee that the last car. on Saturday night shall run at 11.5 to Stockton Heath and the Cemetery, and at 11 'o'clock to Latchford, Sankey and Long- ford. These time will,apply to Monday; but on Tuesday, Wednesday; Thursday, Friday and Sunday the last car for Stockton Heath and the Cemetery will leave at 10.35, and on the other routes at 10.30. The last 'bus will leave for Beivsey and Gorsey-lane at eleven on Mondays and Saturdays, and on other nights at 10.30. Theses times come into operatiownext Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>THEATRES.</head>
        <p>THE THEATRES.</p>
        <p>The Royal Court Theatre and Palace manage- ments hak-e co-operated in the movement, and in consequence of the curtailment of the car service, their respective places of entertainment. will commence as follows :—First house, 6.45; second house, 8.45. The Saturday evening per- formance will be a.s usual, viz.? 6.30 and 8.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>LATE OPENING NOW NEEDLESS.</head>
        <p>LATE OPENING NOW NEEDLESS.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>wish to give my hearty support to the movement for the earlier closing of -shops. The time i 4 Most opportune. The operation of the Lighting Restriction Orders has clearly proyr,d how unnecessary has been the hitherto pre- vailing custom of shops and markets being • open so late on Saturdays. My experience is that people are now shopping earlier and trade is practically finished by 9.30, and apparently without' any inconvenience; even the few who shop after 9 p.m. could, with a small effort, transact their business before that time. . I cordially support Mr. Craik'S suggestion for closing at nine on Fridays and Saturdays, and -at seven on other' days. But the, question : What action is necessary to bring the ' movement to a successful issue? The voluntary ' action of shopkeepers in the ma' in streets is not sufficient, as there are those tradesmen; • both in' and out of the main streets, who still re- main open until nearly midnight tti catch the trade of the few who will not conform to the customs which the new order of things require. The drudgery of long hours in shops is intoler- able, and those who are compelled to endure them, either by force of circumstances or the severity of harsh masters, should be protected by law. Has not the Town Council power to frame a. bylaw or secure ways and means which would make the. livesvof shopkeepers and their assistants more pleasant?, If. &amp;ml,l. power exists I sincerely hope that the Council will be induced to act immediately in the matter, as the long days are returning and any inaction of the Council will land us hack in the rut and drudgery of the old regime..,- •, It is not shopkeepers, only who :wonld benefit by earlier closing, for it is obvious that, by such, ,there would ensue economy in time, labour and e pense, and people would be at home when they ought to be.</p>
        <p>I hope that the Council will be thorough if they act, and keep a vigilant eye upon, and rigorously prosecute those who defy the law by opening on Sundays.</p>
        <p>J. RICHARDSON.</p>
        <p>86, Buttermarket-street</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>SUPPORT FROM THE MARKET.</head>
        <p>SUPPORT FROM THE MARKET.</p>
        <p>Present Awkward Position.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am very pleased to see that you have taken up in "The Examiner" the question of the early closing of the Markets and shops. , I .occupy two stalls in the Warrington Market, and owing to the action of the Markets Com- mittee in painting the skylights in the Market Shed in order to coMply with the. new Lighting Order, am put to great inconvenience in ex- posing and endeavouring to sell my goods :under very trying conditions. The paint on the skylight's may have obscured the artificial lights from the Markets for an hour or two each' evening; but it has also had the effect of. excluding the biggest' share of the daylight and all sunlight throughout the day. The exclusion of the daylight and, the enforced existence for many hours of •the day in a ghostly blue light,, in my opinion, is injurious to one's sight and health, and is a great disadvantage to both 'buyer and seller of goods. Moreover, the stallholders are put to the expense of providing artificial light during many more hours of the day than they had to do before the Lighting Order came into force.</p>
        <p>, Why the Markets Committee did not close the Markets earlier and comply with the Lighting Order in a proper spirit I don't understand. The tenaAts were not consulted, and seeing that the lights do not need to be extinguished until 'nearly 8 o'clock at night, which will 'be gradu- ally extended to nearly 11 o'clock by the end of June, I think the decision of th committ4 has been most unfortunate, both in the interests of the stallholders and the public. I. kno'w that the shops keep open because the Markets are kept open, and would gladly close if the Markets were closed at a much earlier hour than they do at present.- Late shopping is a bad habit. There is no earthly reason wfiy the shops and the Market should not close not later than 7 o'clock on every, night of the week with, per- haps, the exception of Saturday night, when every place of business ought to be closed not later than 9 o'clock.</p>
        <p>There is an agitation throughout the country for the earlier closing of shops, and the great ob.stacle in the wall is the keeping open of the public Markets. The new I4ighting Order fur.; nished a,„splendid opportunity of putting .early closing to the test; and I am sorry our Markets Committee have not had the wisdom to try its effect in Warrington. .</p>
        <p>Yours faithfully. \ A STALLHOLDER.</p>
        <p>icar On Selfishness And NarrowQess.</p>
        <p>The Friday Lenten addresses were continued at the Parish Church yesterday, when, in the afternoon, the Rev. F. R. Gillespy, Vicar of Alvanley, gave an address to women on "Responsibility," and in the evening the Rev. Bernard 0. F. Heywood, Vicar of Swinton, spoke to men on "The. Ruin and Hope of Civilisation." There were large attendances. The 'Rev., F. R. Gillespy' pressed home the question of individual responsibility for the religious welfare of others, pointing out the 'tremendous influence for good which women could exercise. He urged those 'present to ask themselves had they done all they could in thN respect. It was very sad; but he thought it true, to say that there was a tendency to narrowness, to selfishness in their religious outlook. The philosophy everyone for himself or herself was made :in Germany, and he did not think there were any. there anxious to have anything to do with things of German origin, and he hoped none of them wOuld adopt that kind of philosophy. God had placed upon theni a great burden of responsibility, and also a treinendons power,, the influence • of woman- hood. -</p>
        <p>Next Friday's addresses will be given bi the Rev. A. D. Tupper Carey, Canon Residentiary of York, both afternoon and evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
