      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>NATION BEFORE PARTY.</head>
        <p>NATION BEFORE PARTY.</p>
        <p>Liberal Association Reviews Work Of Past Year.</p>
        <p>PATRIOTIC SUBSCRIBERS.</p>
        <p>Interesting facts as to the work done in .the past year were, dwelt upon at the annual meet- ing of the -Warrington Liberal Association at the Liberal Club on Wednesday ,evening.</p>
        <p>Mr. C:E. 'Parker, who Presided, stated that whereas they started last year with an 'over- draft of A:65, they now had a balance of over 5:39. in hand. One of the reasons for this was that they had had no. special poli- tical activities during the year, although they had lent the Secretary (Mr. 11, A. Sanders) to help in recruiting, in the same, way that the Conservative Association had lent Mr. Herbert Hayes. They had done a great deal of useful work, especially clerically, and, if their services had not been given, the expense to the State would have been considerable. (Hear, hear.) _ 435 WORKERS IN ARMY. -</p>
        <p>The treasurer's report having been adopted, the hon. secretary (Councillor J. T. Locker) Sub- mitted his. He said that party politics were not interesting them just now, as their time was more fully occupied in another direction., At the request of the Government, and with the co-operation of the Conservative party, the Liberal Association had devoted the whole year in carrying out recruiting inquiries, military registration, and Lord Derby's recruiting scheme, and was still continuing the work. As the expenses in wages, rent, rates, &amp;c., had con- tinued, and as the services to the country had been given voluntarily, he felt sure everyone would appreciate the generosity of the sub- scribers in the way they have helped the nation in this great crisis, and, at the same time, placed the Association on such an excellent financial basis. They had had many acknowledg- ments and thanks from headquarters and from the War Office, and were especially thanked by Lord Derby for the way that they, along with their friends from the Conservative headquar- ters, organised and carried out the recruiting campaign prior to the one conducted in Lord Derby's .name. This campaign commenced on September 6th and finished 'on the 12th, and in , addition to route marches, musical festival, &amp;e., no fewer than 166 meetings were carried through successfully. . The Liberal Ward Committees had been con- siderably depleted during the past 12 mOnths, owing to many answering the country's call for soldiers. In addition to those enlisted under the group system, no fewer than 435 actually known workers voluntarily enlisted before the group system began. Many friends and workers had been wounded, and many they would see no more, having made, their. last sacrifice for their country and those at home. To their relatives they tendered their deepest regards and sym- pathy, and assured them that the - sacrifices would not be in. vain. Councillor Locker added that in the Warring- ton area they had done their duty in a manner which was almost unequalled by the rest of the country. - In moving, the adoption ,of the report, Coun- cillor John Wilkinson referred to the financial side as being in a very, favourable position. It was very creditable to the subscribers that whilst there had been no propaganda work on behalf of Liberalism, they , had helped . the authorities in lending the services of the secre- tary to help the, registration and recruiting schemes. Mr. P. H. Brown seconded, and the report was adopted. MR. C. E. PARKER PRAISED.</p>
        <p>In proposing the re-election of Mr. C. E. Parker. to the presidency of the Association, Alderman Tinnion said it would be better for the Progressive party and the Liberal Associa- tion in Warrington if they had More men like Mr. Parker. There was no man who had taken more responsibility on his shoulders, not only in the, past, but principally during the present crisis. In every possible way he had assisted by the very intelligent manner in which he had placed all important matters before the public, particularly in the press. He had always been most generous in his contributions to that Association, and to many others. They were proud of him; and delighted to have a gentle- man of his capacity to preside over their Associa- tion. There were many things they would have to consider later on. They were not going to change their opinions and not going to cut adrift from the ideas of Liberalism, although it was possible that in the changed condition of the world they might have to "carry on" in a different way. The Association was delighted to have as their president a gentleman who held to his principles tenaciously and, helped to carry his principles through to a successful issue. He (Alderman Tinnion) hoped that, Mr. Par)cer would be long spared to them. If people only knew half of what he had done for the towli they would appreciate him more than. they did even- new. (Applause.) , Councillor Gough seconded, and thought that in Mr. Parker they had the right man in the right place. During the past year they had , joined hands with the Conservative Party, and he believed it was the best thing to do under the circumstances, although they had not fore- gone their principles. They had not in the slightest degree wavered in their adhesion to tho principles of progress. ( NOT THE COSTLIEST SACRIFICE. Mr. Parker, in reply, said he could not help but be 'touched by the kind things' said about . him. A man could only give what he had. It might be materially and in other ways he. had been able to give more than others, but he ought not to have any more credit than any other mail who had given. of his best. Family circum- stances had prevented him giving what was the costliest sacrifice—a son to the Army—and it seemed to him that he ought to makeup for that inability as far as possible by giving of what he had. He had occupied the position of president for at least fourteen years, and if lie went on much more be would certainly be en- titled to a pension-. (Laughter.) He wourd not think of giving up the position at, such a time, but if the Association could find one who could fill the office better he hoped they would not fail to indicate to him their desires. The Vice-Presidents were re-elected, as was also Councillor Wilkinson to the chairmanship of the committee, Mr. Parker- remarking that he had always been a loyal member of the party. Councillor Gough having moved the re-election of Councillor LoCker to the hon. secretaryship, Alderman Tinnion seconded, saying that he was one of the most respected members of the Liberal party in Warrington. In acknowledging his re-election, Councillor Locker recalled that during the past year he and other Liberals had come into closer contact with members of the Conservative party, and he was more than pleased to observe the good spirit with which they had entered into the whole of the services they had endeavoured to render. It had done both parties good to have been brought together in that way. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>FOOTBALL FOR BOYS.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL FOR BOYS.</p>
        <p>Presentation Of Shield To'Evelyn Street School.</p>
        <p>_ - Yesterday afternoon was the occasion for ;the presenting of the shield to the Evelyn-street School team, on the success attained during the past season in connection with the Schoolboys' As4ociation Football Competition. A splendid record stands to the credit of the school, for during the season the youthful enthusiasts have scored 93 goals against two. Since the inaugura- tion of the competition Evelyn-street have always been to the fore, as the following will show :—First season, third; second season, second; third season, second; and fourth season, first. ,The large ro6m was crowded :with .the boys' section of the school at the oPging of the pro- ceedings, and resounded with applause at the entry of the Mayor (Alderman Peter .Peacock), who presented the shield. In a brief addresOthe Mayor tquehed Upon the beneficial effect of sriort in the lives of. individuals, for, said he, it brought out all the good points and aimed at 'furthering not only gentlemanly instincts, but helped, us to play the game in life. It was also helpful in creating sturdier men, who were more able to stand the rigours of-climate. . Three songs were Suitably rendered by different-secetions of the school prior to the pre- sentation, and just before the close a patrioti number entitled " The rally tb e7s.e Motherland" was presented. Amongst' those present were Messrs. C. E. Parker, G. Dale, W. Myles and J; Moore Murray.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Sick Nursing Lectures. The first of a: ,course of sick nursing lectures in connection with he British Red Cross Society, is to be delivered by Nurse. Phillips in the Cairo-street Schools on Tuesday eveningt</p>
        <p>£5OO for liwelling-houses, and Land. Messrs. A. IL Clive and Co., at the Blue Bell. Horsemarket-street, sold a'freehold dwelling- house and three freehold cottages, together with a plot of land, situated in tythgoes-lane, obtain- ing 2500, all three lots being sold together.</p>
        <p>, Hospital Sunday. , To-morrow is Hospital Sunday, , and the churches and chapels throughout the district anticipate realising_ a substantial sum. The names of the preachers for • the day will be found in our advertisement col-onus. '</p>
        <p>Special -Services at Golborir .Street. tielborne-street Baptist Churcn are to-morrow 'holding 'special anniversary services in aid of church funds. The .preacher for the day is the Rev. J. P. Hargreaves, of Chester. In the after- noon a musical service is to,be 'held. • .</p>
        <p>Lymm Hut. , The .2300 'necessary for the Lymin hut has been raised. •</p>
        <p>Mr„ W. Stewart Royston and the V.C. Next week we shall "publish a special article written by Mr. W. Stewart ,Royston, ‘vho recently had .a V.C. visitor at his home in. Pad- dington. It is full of interest and, as everything does that 34. :Royston writes, points a definite moral.</p>
        <p>Dramatic Story</p>
        <p>of the. War.</p>
        <p>The, 4-o-1-7-7Oithe-1-var-told in prose, verse and .song by the children attending St. James! and Fairfield Schools, recently given so successftlly in the Parr Hall, is to be repeated on Monday and Tuesday evenings, at 7.30, in St. George's Mission, ,Hopwood-street. '••Large audiences... should be assured.'</p>
        <p>Wedding At St.. Peter's Chime*. An interesting and ..pretty wedding was solemnised at St. Peter's Church on Tuesday, th- èontracting • parties being Miss Nora Cooper Toft, daughter of Mrs. H. Toft, The Cottage,. Appleton, Cheshire, and Second-Lieutenant Frank Cecil Coops, of the. King's' (Liverpool) Regiment', son of Mr. Charles Coops, of Whalley Range, Manchester., _</p>
        <p>Grand Concert at 'Whilecross Hospital</p>
        <p>" Thanks to Miss Marie Houghton, supi,orfed by a number. of well-known local artistes, • the, inmates and wounded soldiers in the Whiteeross. Hospital were again splendidly entertiined on Saturday, and thy showed-th* appreciation cf' the efforts put forth ,on their behalf .very. heartily. The programme was a ',cry attractiva one, and varied in such a niannei that thfre was not a dull moment. At the close Mr. Lox- ham gave fitting expression of • the .gratitude of the audience to Miss Houghton, and the artistes generally for the exceptionally fine programme they had provided. A feature of the concert was the playing of thrOe Pianoforte solos by MigS•Xenaky, who is a pupil of .Bachaus. . The other' artistes were Mrs. F. Short, -Misses Doris Read, May-COok,..Peggy and joan Cooper, Dora .1-Tope, Mr,QW. Pearce and Mr. Chas. Lambert. The concert was arranged by Mr. J. Beddard.</p>
        <p>Dr. Naden EleetPd Presalent of Learned Society.</p>
        <p>At the Literary and Philosophical Society .meeting on Monday evening, Dr. J. B. Nadel' as elected presiden.: in succession to the Rev H. V. Pigote who has held the position for two rears. Dr. J. S. Manson was re-elected ,hon. secretary, and Mr. W. Sileock hon. treasurer. Mr. F. G. Ruddock retied from the Council, which is now as follows:—Messrs..J. LonglAnd,. j. Fulton-Smith; H., IL Taylor, 0., Lofthotise, Grosvenor and Miss Broadbent.</p>
        <p>The stateMent of accounts showed that .the session opened with a balance in hand of £.17 ss. 4d.; the amount, received in subscriptions was £30,-10s., and the sum now in hand was £37 95.. 10d. The total membership was 137, five new members having joined. There are at least five members serving with the military forces. During the evening the Rey. E. Gunner -de- livered an interesting appreciation of Charles Lamb, declaring that nothing but gain could result from an acquaintance with the life and writings of Charles Lamb, for no ,cleaner writer ever handled a pen. No man was ever freer from all 'shade of envy and to- Wards contemporary writers, or showed more un- selfish interest in the good qualities of their. work, or a greater tenderness for their faults., No essays existed" in our language that could be compared with those of " " in the won- derful qualities of their 'stile. , Nowhere could be find a critic in art and literature of saner judgment, or more level-headed in, his outlook upon human -life as he saw it surging around him in the London of a century ago. Above all the moral -rectitude of Charles Lamb, his , supreme unselfishness, as shown in.' his life-long devotion • to an afflicted • sister whom he passionately loved, as well as his never-failing fidelity and tenderness •of • heart towards his friends, entitled him to our undying gratitude.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Presentation to Chorister.</head>
        <p>Presentation to Chorister.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion ot the evening service at St. Peter's Church .on Sunday, the Ni.icar (the Rev. B. -Percival), on behalf .ot the members of the choir, presented a- set of carvers .to Mr. James Shaw, a chorister, in celebration of his recent marriage. _ The -Vicar spoke of. Mr. Shaw's several years' connection with the choiri-during whichtime he had attended regularly, and of the interest which he, took in the.work of the choir. - Mr. H. Sudlow -wished Mr. Shaw much .happi- ness, and the latter suitably replied. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Sunday Post: Labour Protest.</head>
        <p>Sunday Post: Labour Protest.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. J. Shimmin (of the Postmen's Federation) the Warrington Trades Council, on Tuesday evening, protested agains, the proposal of the Retrenchment Committee to knock off the Sunday delivery of letters, &amp;c. It was contended that especially at these times the money earned by postmen on Sunday .was requisite for their comfort. For their wages, to be reduced by 2s. or 2s. 6d., a week would be a hardship on those concerned. The Council de- cided to ask the Chamber • of Commerce to Sup- port their protest, on the ground, of business inconvenience.</p>
        <p>Warrington Caledonian Association. The members and friends of .the above asso- ciation were entertained by the President (Mr. J. L. Davenport) on Thursday.. During the</p>
        <p>repast, Captain Mathams recited a f,ew verses of a poem he had composed on the Haggis, Which went astray on Burn's • /Night, these being greatly appreciated. After the repast a smoking concert took place, the music being' supplied by the , following :- 7.Madame Small- shaw, Captain Mathams, Messrs. J. L., Daven- port, A. Pearce, J. ,Clayton, J. Robertson, G. Marshall, J. Brawn and J.., Hunter. The accompaniMents were played by Mr. Donald Pearce and Mr. A. H. BroWn. Mr. Murray, in a few. well-chosen remarks, proposed tile. health of the President, and hoped that he might long be spared to fill the chair as dent - dent of the Caledonian Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Shunter Gets Medal for Bravery.</head>
        <p>Shunter Gets Medal for Bravery.</p>
        <p>Bravery was rewarded.- at the Police Court on Monday. • Having on January 17th, heard that a woman was in the Bridgewater Canal, Frederick Leonard Simpson, of 114, Oldham-street, Latch- ford, ran along Knutsford-road for 200 yards and then jumped into the Water. He first noticed a child floating on the surface, and then discovered that the. mother was underneath the water. The run had made him very exhausted, and in effecting a rescue he also had to. be helped out of the water. , The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) in pre- senting him with an illuminated address and silver medal from the Liverpool Shipwreck and Humane Society, congratulated him, and added that perhaps his duty as a shunter on the L. and Railway, where he had his life in his hands continually, inade him forget his own peisOnal risk in saving .theliToman.and child.</p>
        <p>Picture Presented 10 the Art Gallery.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the business of fhe Museum Committee on Tuesday afternoon, Councillor H. Roberts, addressing the Chairman (Mr. T. J. Ridgway), said that he had been requestea by his friend—Mr. Frank Houghton, of Woodside, Appleton—to offer to the commit- tee, as an addition to the permanent collection of pictures, a valuable and beautiful painting. The subject. was "Pastoral," and the 'artist. was a Belgian of high repute, named "Ravori," a fact which gave much additional interest at the present time to Mr. Houghton's generous offer. Councillor Roberts added that on several occasions of late Mr.. .Houghton had intimated his desire to do something to express his in- terest in Warrington, and this gift was the outcome of his intentions. The . committee, after .viewing the picture, unanimously decided to accept Mr. Houghton's valuable gift, and ordered a special resolution of thanks to be recorded on the minutes and a cony thereof to be sent- to Mr. Houghton.. It will, be remenibered that Mr. 'Frank Hougliton's father (the late Mr. W. D. Houghton) presented to the Art Gallery, some years ago, a very fine ,example of the work of the late 'Clarence Wliaite.</p>
        <p>Betsey Adult School Holds Bulb</p>
        <p>Show.</p>
        <p>The 'third annual bulb show in connection with Bewsey Adult School was held .on Satur- day. Three classes Of flowers had' been ,grown from bulbs, viz., hyacinths,, daffodils and 'Mu- ' Mr. Upton judged the ,exhibits. There was a 'record entry. . Mr. Upton commented most favourably upon the quality of the .show, especially the hyacinths, which .showed to ad- vantage.. Tea was served later to a: large corn- pay of . members and friends. In the evening a concert was held, which was presided over by Mr. Alfred Tilling, jun. who ,also presented the prizes. In the hyacinth section Mr. George Boyle and Mr. Pearce took the honours with fine exhibits, whilst in the daffodil and nar- cissus section Mr. and Mrs. Sam, Sproston carried off double honours. •</p>
        <p>cissi</p>
        <p>Mr. Tilling warmly commended the purpose of the show and wished it every success in its 'endeavour to promote the growth and love of flowers. The small financial outlay, coupled with the happy results, made 811C1i 'efforts worth while. 'ln fact, all growers had really splendid" prizes in the flowers themselves. To be inter- ested in flowers refined and ennobled, the mind, and Nature well repaid her lovers, for Nature was prodigal in her gifts. The school members feel much encouraged to go forward and hope that next year all the local Adult Schools will :join up in a large show.</p>
        <p>Commercial Travellers Associalion. The quarterly general meeting of the Warring- ton and District Branch of the United Kingdom Commercial Travellers' Association was held at the Lion Hotel On Saturday evening. The 'Chair- man, Mi. H. Walsh, presided over a good at- tendance ;of members, which included Mr. W H. .Owen (vice-chairman), Mr. W. I'. Ashton (hon. treasurer), Mr: T. Percival (hon. sec.) and Mr. A. 0. -Hilditch (hon. assistant sec.) It was unanimously decided again to nominate Mr. W. G. Hayes for the Executive Council of the Association at, the forthcoming conference at Liverpool, and also • for the Vice-chair of the North-Western Federation.- It WAS mentioned that. he had, been appointed hon. treasurer for the conference, and that Mr. Walsh is also a member of the Conference Committee. The Hon. SecKetary presented the balance sheet of the recent whist drive in aid of the "Warrington Cot" fund, in which a profit of over £lB was shown. Thig Was considered very satisfactory and Mr. J. W. C. Taylor proposed that the best thanks of the members be given to lhe Ladies' Committee for' the 'substantial' help they had\ rendered by taking charge of the refreshments. This was carried with applause. Mr. Hayes eported that he had now received about ,65 for the " Cot" fund, and it was de- cided to remit • another 25 guineas to the schools. This Would make up half the amount required for which they were entitled to elect an hon. life. vice-president. On the motion of Mr. P. .03rien, seconded by jr. W. H. Owen, Mr. A. J. Allen was Unanimonsly elected. It was also resolved ,that ;the, sum • of Ave guineas be subscribed from the branch funds towards prOVicliiig a.. motor ambulance for . the Red Cross society,*Prtanoted by the, association. in conjunction with the Kent commercial Travellers' Association. .</p>
      </div>
