      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>HIGHLY-RESPECTED LADY.</head>
        <p>HIGHLY-RESPECTED LADY.</p>
        <p>Death And Funeral Of Mrs. Down After Long Illness.</p>
        <p>After a long and painful illness, Mrs. Eliza- beth Down., widow of Mr. T. J. Down, passed away on Monday afternoon at her residence, Underclyffe, Stocxton heath; . t The daughter of Mr. John Guest, Mrs. Down was. born in 1840 at the house adjoining her father's tannery, in Tanner's-lane, and thus belonged to a- family which was much respected in. Warrington.. In 1865 she married, the late Mr. T. J. Down, managing director of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Company, and one well- known in the district by reason of his philan- thropy and musical ability, the last-named being particularly manifest by his interest iii the .military and striing bands bearing his name.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Down had two children, viz., Miss Annie Guest Down (who died, on the 4th of June, 1898) and Mr. Arthur Down. For thirty years from the date of the marriage they resided at Walton Villas, Lower Walton, but removed to Underclyffe. in 1895. ' While living at Lower Walton, Mr. .Down had erected a wooden building, for us ei as a photographic studio, and it was in this room that the practices of his orchestra commenced, in the 'autumn of .1883, and also those of the military band three years later.</p>
        <p>Both in Mr. Down's photography and in his band work Mrs. Down took the greatest pos- sible interest, and was most helpful in many ways. Although she herself never assisted musically in the band—it may be mentioned that: she was always a kind and considerate hostess—Mrs. Down was yet keenly apprecia- tive of the art ,of music, ancl had, by her early instruction, laid a' good solid foundatcon for the subsequent musical growth of her son and daughter. To Mrs. Down the members of the bands have been indebted for much of the comfort they have enjoyed at the practices. Up to the .Ifist she took the greatest interest in the weekly' practices. Before Mrs. Down became crippled bY rheu- matism, she used to take an active part in the. parochial work of St. Thomas' Church, Stock- ton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Despite the inclement weather, many people attended the funeral, which took place on Thursday in Stockton Heath Churchyard. A choral service was held in the • church, the officiating clergy being the Revs. Jas. Collins, E. W. Clarke and H. V. pigot. The 90th Psalm was sung, as well as the hymns, Thy way not mine '0 Lord" and "Through the night of doubt and sorrow." Among the mourners was Miss Booth, who had dev(ited all her time to Mrs. Down since, 1904, when skilled attention became necessary. The principal mourners were:—Mr. Arthur Down, Miss L. Booth, Dr. illation, Mr. T. J. Down (of Rixton), Mr. George E. Guest, Mr. Herbert W. Guest, Miss Guest, Miss Gornall, Miss Mary E. Gornall. The members of Down's Band present included Messrs. B. ;M. Foster, John Smith, R. Whitfield, Walter Ray, I'. MOlynepx, W. Davenport, H. Davenport, T. Pittam, Walter Howard, George Clarke, F. Pendlebury and L Balniford ; and other friends we noticed were the Rev. Hon. C. F. Cross, Messrs. W. Bainbridge; 11. Hatton, L. A. Voisey, J. H. Clarke, J. Seward, W. 11. Payton The church officials were: Messrs. James Richardson, "W. Mclntyre, T. R. West- brook, John Hallows and Fred Berry. Mr. F. Rose presided at the organ. The large: number of wreaths sent are an additional indication of the esteen). in which Mrs. Down was held. The list included,:—Mr Arthur Down, Miss L. Booth, The. Maids at Underelyffe, Dr. and Mrs. Hutton, Mr., and Mrs. Down, and Mr. and Mrs. T. J. Down. Mrs. and Miss Guest, Mr. and Mrs. George E.- Guest, Mr. and Mrs. Herbert W. Guest, Mrs. Gornall anil family, Miss Gornall, 'Mr. Friqnk Gornall and Mrs. Bennett,' Rev. Frank 11. and Miss 'Mary E. GOrnall, Members of the Orchestra and of the Military Band, Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall, Mr. J. j. Whitley, Mr. J. H. -Clarke, The Rey. the Hon. C. F. Cross, Mr.. and Mrs. Vere, 8.. Davies and Mr. L. 'Davies, ,Mr., and Mrs. flepherd;Dr. and Mn 4. Geary, Mr. and Mrs. , VoiseY, grs.iSegariOwen, Mr. and. Mrs, W. IL Payton. :Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Sankey,' ,Mr. and Mrs. Taylor (Hatton), Mr. Robert Lowe, Nurses Taylor and Tornkins, Nurse Wilkinson, Nurse Mervyt, Miss Howe, Mr. ulna Alm :17, Berry Mrs. 0-tkes , ,</p>
      </div>
