      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>HONOURED BY BOWLERS.</head>
        <p>HONOURED BY BOWLERS.</p>
        <p>Sir A. H. Crosfield, Receives Illuminated Address.</p>
        <p>RECREATION &amp; THE WAR.</p>
        <p>An interesting feature at the annual meeting of the Lancashire County Amateur Bowling Association; at the Albion Hotel, Picadilly, Man- chester, on Saturday, was the presentation of an illumingted address to Sir Arthur Crosfield, Bart, their late• president, expressing their satisfaction at ".the signal' and well-deserved honour" of baronetcy- conferred upon him, and eonveying their congratulations to Lady Cros- field and himself. ' Representatives of clubs situated all overlie minty were present, and the warmth •of his reception was a reflex of the sentiments con- tained in the address (most artistically' designed and framed), which was as follows: . _</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COUNTY AMATEUR BOWLING ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>At the half-yearly 'meeting of the Associa- tion, held on October 30th, 1915, a resolution Was moved by the President, and unani- mously adopted, that the Association present an illuminated address to their late Presi- dent, Sir Arthur Henry Crosfield, Bart, ex- pressing their great pleasure and hearty -congratulations to. him and .Lady Crosfield Upon the signal and well-deserved honour it has pleased His Majesty to confer upon him, 41 ad expressing the Association's earnest hope that Sir Arthur and Lady Crosfield may `Do spared many years to enjoy the same. Signed, on behalf of the Association, John W. G. Coombs (president), Jas. Lawrence (deputy-president), W. H. Hindley (hon. treasurer), Chas. Smith (hon. sec.), G. F. Freeman, J. Newton, S. Smith, J. C. Poyser, Albert E. Wright (sub-coirimittee).</p>
        <p>In Sir Arthur, declared the Presidtat, tneT had one of the great supporters of the amateur bowling world. From the early stages of the Association he had been a kind and useful vice- president, .quiet and not lop vibtrosive as some vice-presidents, but always there when required. (Applause.) Duri.ng the whole of the 25 or 26 Sears he had been With them they had had nothing but the most pleasant associations with 'him. The name of Crosfield wits well known throughout the county of Lancashire, and, in .another sense, throughout the world. Sir, Arthur was One Of a splendid family, one of the best known members being his first cousin, the late Mr. Sidney Crosfield, wh4 was a great shot and a good sportsman. With 4regard to the ser- vices Sir Arthur had rendered in the bowling 'world and other branches of sport, it Would take lira (Mr. Coombs) until next Saturday to enumerate them. (Hear, 'hear, and laughter.) At all times he had been kindly and generously 'thsllgsed, and when recently president of that Association,- and of the Lancashire and Cheshire 'Bowling Association before the severance took Place, he ably rendered assistance with his 'counsel. (Applause.) That Association owed him a magnificent debt of gratitude; and it was the greatest pleasure of his (the speaker's) life to interpret the feelings of the Bowling Association 'l3f Lancashire and his old county, Cheshire. He trusted Sir Arthur would receive the address as 'a small token 6f the high esteem in. which he -was held. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>LUSTY OLD LANCASHIRE.</head>
        <p>LUSTY OLD LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>In responding, Sir Arthur was first frankly PersonaL Mr. Coombs, lie began, had been a .great deal too -kind in what he had said— (Voices; "No, no !")—but it was -perfectly true that for 25 or 30 years he had taken a deep in- terest in the game of bowls as played in lusty -old Lancashire and in cheery Cheshire. "You refer to me as hailing from Cheshire," he con- tinued, "but I can never, make out which ,eounty it is that I ought to represent. I really believe I am a kind of hybrid—(laughter)—be- -cause while I used to Jive and sleep at night in Cheshire, I worked all day in Lancashire. it is, therefore, a little bit difficult to know where one stands: perhaps we had better say I have a leg in both. (Laughter.) But if Mr. Coombs now calls me a Cosmopolitan, I suppose that is because I have lately pitched my tent in Lon- don, so that lie thinksn'hflVA ilevelOPed intb hit of a Cockney. (Loud laughter.). But what- -ever I may have been able to do to help to extend the popularity of the 'time honoured English game of bowls in old Warrington, and Perhaps to some small extent over a wider field, and whatever littl6 I may be able to do to help !the game in the future, has been done, and I can assure you will be done, con amore. (Loud cheers.) " "I have valued this ganie very highly, but, of course, it is 'not the only game I have beer, interested in: It is quite true I cannot enter into competition with my late lamented cousin, Sidney.Crosfield, who at cover-point and also as deputy to ` Monky HornbY in the Lancashlr3 'County Cricket Club, was an uncommonly use- ful cricketer,- and at his best has never been surpassed in certain varieties of shooting. We Still have his elder brother With us. He is an all round sportsman of even greater ability, and more varied attainments. lam sorry to say. that I cannot for a moment enter into competi- tion with these distinguished representatives of our family, but still I have tried, as the phrase 'goes, to " "do My bit." (Applause.) ' A Vigorous American.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur confessed that bowls happened to be the first game he was ever associated with. Ile began when only a little taller than the table at which he was standing: it was in the days .When he lived in Cheshire and his father used to la.ke him out on summer, evenings and employ u,,uu to carry the footer. (Laughter.) One of "le earliest episodes of those days he could l'emember was that in which an American guest of huge proportions and herculean streliTth•figured. " was in the days when I was carrying the tooter," Sir Arthur proceeded. "The American gentleman was invited to have a game, and theY started in the usual scientific and quiet way. I fancy that the American thought his friends were playing rather a poor game, •for when it came to his turn to deliver his wood, he sent it crashing through all the bowls on the green, rushing it up the bank. and finishing in a dump of rhododendrons. (Great laughter.) " There are a good many people who make the sad mistake of telling us that in the present circumstances games are opt of place, and that it is questionable even if we ought to meet, to- gether to talk about them. Those contentions were well meant, and a kind and sympathetic feeling inspires them, but a greater mistake cannot be made. The first people to correct that error are the very heroes who are fighting for old England in the trenches. In all this unspeakable tragedy of war there is nothing more Jemarkable and gratifying than, the way 011 r fellows at the Front, within few minutes uf carrying their lives in their hands—the Supreme moment of their lives—can still play their game and keep their humour, as well as 14,00 d humour. Don't tell me that that does not nein to make them the more formidable and in- vincible opponents of the Huns. (Applause.) Virhat the Duke of Wellington said about Waterloo having been won on the playing fields Of .ton is just as true of the war to-day. In strenuous times we've all to do our bit, The moreiefficieiitly if we keep fit. alnes at htme and at the Front helped us to achieve this. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Priceless Asset.</head>
        <p>A Priceless Asset.</p>
        <p>"Nerve, coolness, individual resource, and 'self-reliance, physique, energy, boundless cour- age, and loyalty to leaders on the one hand, and capacity to lead on the other—these are quali- ties on which are hanging the fate and the destinies of civilisation and 'empires; and how 1111°1 '`we owe them to the playgrounds of Britain!, (Hear, hear.) Already these quali- tie • th e priceless asset of our nation—are tell- :11g their tale, united as they are with the ''„ash, spirit, determination, dud noble fortitude of Prance, and the wonderful combination of 'endurance and enterprise of Russia. (Hear, 'hear.) Bearing in mind the indomitable spirit Which pervades every one of the allied nations, their fixity of purpose, we feel, and we Kilaw, gentlemen, that their cause must finally 'Prevail. Yes, and there are some others besides the Allies and Neutral Powers who know it 043. The ,Kaiser knows it, and his co-conspira- tors. anil co-traitors to civilisation know it—an-a 'none better. All their hideous, horrible methods ;clf frightfulness are *evidence of a spirit of in- teasing desperation, 'which makes the leaders of 7.liiins more and more reckless. Theirs is a 4.1110 icy of atrocity by which they hope, but hope vain, to horrify and terrify the Allied troops var!d sicken the Allied nations of the war. • ..1" I -think we ire 'entitled not only to hope,</p>
        <p>but to feel very'confident that the time is Sast. approaching, wilen it wiW be sufficiently laad unmistakably clear to the nations*bt Christen- dom that the ultimate fate of the Huns is sealed. Now we have been told by that German 'Chauvinist, -Maximilian Harden, that if Ger- many has to succumb, that Shall not be until she has brought down the entire. civilisation. of Europe with her, and shattered it in the Same debacle. That, of course, means. What Germany is already doing her best to employ—the last extremities of frightfulness. I, •therefore, sub- mit to you, gentlemen, that the period of the war to which I have just alluded, will: he a psycholdgical moment for a little 'bit of very straight talking to the Huns. If will be time then to warn them that just in so far as they try, by breaking every international law and treaty, and by every .devilish contrivance, to .reduce warfare, to the. ne phis ultra of bar. barism, by so much will the Penalties in Store for them" at the conclusion' of the war be the more severe." (Loud applause.) Germany Without The Pale. .</p>
        <p>They knew • the oft-qtioted. declaration of Burke, "I refuse', to.. draw up -an - indictment against a. whole, ,nation.'" Well, if -ever • they were tempted to: make an exception to that grand maxim, Germany would be that excep- tion to-day. , But let them, be thankful . far tile .sake of the human Face that there were in Ger- many a good many millions of people who, if they had had the power, and had not been snowed under from, the very outbreak of the war would never have permitted- or tolerated such an atrocious crime against 'civilisation. The German nation, however,. had - incurred the most frightful responsibility in not having united to send the miscreants of Prussia to the right-about, and terrible was the penalty that she deserved, and would have to pay. (Applause.) The Central Powers had placed 'themselves out- side the pale of decency, and we had to see that in future we 'kept • them in their place. (Loud applause.) , In 'Conclusion, Sir Arthur again acknowledged the kind feelings which had prompted so Many bowlers to give him a testimonial which, so long as he lived, he would regard with feelings of, he dared say, human pride but Still more of real gratitude to Lancastrians and the good in- habitants of Cheshire. (Applause.) • During the more routine part of the meeting Sir Arthur's name was included amongst those of the vice-presidents, and Mr. G. F. Freeman, of Warrington, among those Of the patrons. The representatives present 'from Warrington were: Works' Association, Messrs. W. Patrick and F. Dean; Bull's Head, G. F. Freeman an,! A. Regan; Cemetery Club, J. Willett, W. Woods and J. 'Plant. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SOLDIERS' COMFORTS.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS' COMFORTS.</p>
        <p>Voluntary Organisations Combine To Save Overlapping. THE ARTICLES NEEDED.</p>
        <p>. The Director:General of Voluntary Organisa- tions, on inquiry by the Warrington. -Branch as to what comforts are most wanted . at the mdment, has replied, asking for as _many mufflers as possible to be sent to him up to the :end of March. The mufflers should be 58 inches long and 10 inches wide.</p>
        <p>_ • Perhaps the work, or principal function, of the Director-General .of Voluntary Organisa- tions is not as yet fully grasped by the public .at large. The organisation was only begun a. few months ago in order to apply more effec- tively and with less waste the flow of comforts so 'aenerously contributed by societies aud indi- viduals all over the country and to guide and increase the Volume of this stream by providing a satisfactory channel.</p>
        <p>- There has been in the past considerable over- lapping; in some cases there has been an immense superfluity of mufflers, mittens, dx.; socks have been 8o plentiful...that the men have cleaned their boots with them, and, at the same .time, a mile •or two away, there wiis scarcity—a faminle, of woollies—because some local gifts had not arrived or some particular unit had not been so fortunate as another in enlisting the •Practical sympathies of those at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Forwarding Arrangements.</head>
        <p>Forwarding Arrangements.</p>
        <p>The trench warfare in Flanders and France has, by its .nature, rendered .it comparatively easy .for local committees to send sacks of com- forts to the 'battalions raised in their own districts, but these Circumstances will. not be permanent, and a recent memorandum issued by the Director-Genral contains this warning sentence :</p>
        <p>"Exhaustive inquiries prove that, having regard to the railid movement of troops not only from place to place, but from one theatre of war to another, it is an ever-increasing problem to secure prompt delivery of consign- ments earmarked for particular units." The Director-General has made arrangements whereby A special officer (termed the Military Forwarding Officer) will deal with all the gifts forwarded through this department. lie will act as the connecting link between Command- ing Officers in the field and \ the Director- General; .he Will have stores of comforts ,and hospital requirements to draw upon.. A Com- iiiatiding Officer applying to him avoids the delay that must necessarily occur when he writes home and makes his wants known to a local committee.. Whatever a local association contributes to this "pool" of comforts will go to the credit of its particular -unit; thus, if two regiments are asking for mufflers and theTe are , not enough for both, the. one raised in a district where there is a local association which has sent supplies by this channel will have priority over the other. The Director-General will lie - , able to ;-.eep- local associations informed from time to time as to whether the units in which the locality is, particularly interested liave a "credit" or "debit" with the Military For- warding Officer. - Supporting Associations. ,</p>
        <p>The Warrington Branch (which has been accorded the right to use the words 'Approved by the War Office ") was started in the end of November last, the Mayor kindly .consenting to be President, Mr. J. H. Smethurst chairman, and Mr. Henry Collin to be the lion. secretary. The War Hospital, Committee incorporated it- self with this new movement, which- also re- ceives ' the cordial support of the Territorial Association, Women's Liberal . AssociAtion, Cairo-street Sewing Society, and the Moore Working. Party. The 'Branch is • taking the title of ,The War- rington • Association for the Provision of Comforts for Troops and Hospitals,. approved by the War Office, and has already complied with two requisitions—one consisting Prity.4- pally of hospital 'garments (for use at the .St. Lazaire Hospital at Rouen), and .the other of mufflers, mittens, &amp;c. With respect to', the months • of April and May, the Director-General has% asked for bed- socks, operation stockings, nightingales, hos- pital bags, bed-jackets, helpless. jackets, pneumonia jackets, and pyjamas. . The patterns and all particulars 'about the making of these articles can be -obtained from Miss White, Holly House. The .committee trust Hi* the women of Warrington will do their- best to make the local branch of this great War Office organisa- tion an unqualified success..' .• - Support Needed. -</p>
        <p>Subscriptions are most urgently needed ,to provide materials for the articles mentioned in the above, and may be paid to the :Borough Treasurer; or to Mr. Henry • Collin, lion. secretary, Marson-street, Warrington. Subscriptions Already Received or Promised. s. d</p>
        <p>Students, Warrington Coininercial,</p>
        <p>Institute Mr. H. Roberts</p>
        <p>10 10 0 5 0 0</p>
        <p>Warrington and District L.G.O. Association 2 .16 1 S. Moss &amp; Sons, Ltd. ,0 Mrs. Breretcrn Fairclough Mrs. J. A. Edelsten. Miss Timewell '• , o' Mr. 'John Houghton .0 0</p>
        <p>Mr. Ernest Houghton</p>
        <p>Mrs. Frith</p>
