      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1916.</p>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN ED. WALMSLEY.</p>
        <p>Struck By Shrapnel When Silting In</p>
        <p>Hug-Out.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Edward Walmsley, 72. Back DaHam-. lane, Rifle Brigade, has- been killed in action. He was the son of Edward Waliusley, and with him was engaged by Mr. Stewart, salt dealer, Turner-street. He joined the Army soon after the outbreak of war. He .was 22 years of age, an,old Wycliffe Day. School boy, and played in the Rugby Works' Competition for Welsby's team. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Gained The Heart Of Everybody.</head>
        <p>Gained The Heart Of Everybody.</p>
        <p>Second-Lieutenant Begg sent word of the death in the following sympathetic letter : " Dear Mr. Walmsley,--1 am afraid I have</p>
        <p>Killed</p>
        <p>very sad news for you. Your son, Rifleman Walmsley, was killed on Wednesday, February 9th, by a German shrapnel shell. I don't think he suffered anything, as he only lived about thirty seconds after he was hit. He was sitting just inside a dug-put when he was hit, and all he said was Take me out,' ,and then died. He was a great favourite with all the ,men, and his quiet ways gained the heart of. ,every- body. His death will be 'a great loss to us all, and will be deeply felt. I can assure you that you have my deepest sympathy at your great sacrifice. .I enclose letter and a Testament be- longing to your son."</p>
