        <p>RIFLEMAN EDWARD WALMSLEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>RIFLEMAN ALBERT BAILEY</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN ALBERT BAILEY</p>
        <p>Killed Along With Rifleman Roswell.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Albert Bailey, of. the 10th King's Royal Rifles, who, before the war, lived with Iris sister, Mrs. Shields, 28, Marsh House-lane, was killed by shell-shock on February 29th, along with Rifleman 'Boswell, whose death we reliorted. last_ week. _ _ _</p>
        <p>'Mrs. Shields has received a letter from the Rev. F. J. Hazeldene, chaplain, who also *rote to Mrs. Boswell last week. Another letter has been received from Corporal A. :Read, a native of TAymm,,ond of the King's Royal Rifles, ,who I.losoni.friend of the late Rifleman Bailey. The letter reads:—</p>
        <p>• "Just a line or two, which .pain me very much, to write that Albert passed away on the</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>RIFLEI AN ALBERT BAILEY. Killed.</p>
        <p>29th February, together .with another War- rington 'chum '. of his. You will perhaps be coniforted to know that .he passed Away quite peacefully. He was. a very good chum of mine, and •a good and kind-hearted lad. He had .won the respect of all who knew him. Albert and his section were in a dug-out, when a shell, knocked the parapet in, and the shock killed him and his churn. He died a British death and we have lost a good soldier." Rifleman Bailey was not a Warrington man, but' followed his employment as a printer in North Wales. Enlisting in September, 1914, he was sent to the Front in July of the follow- ing year. When on furlough he worshipped at St. George's Mission. Mr. George Bailey, an elder brother, is now at the Front with the King's Own Royal Lan- caster Regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>OUR TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>OUR TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>Soldier Vocalists 6ive First-Class Entertainment.</p>
        <p>We have received the following communica- tion from a town in the South of England., where our Territorials, the 2ndl4th South Lancashires are in training : , Every evening St. Luke's Parish Room is crowded with Lancpshire " Tommies " billeted in the neighbourhood of , , and while they iuily appreciated a concert. by the town arlistes, they were anxious to exhibit the musi- cal talent that exists among theifiselves Accord- ingly, Sergeant F. • Wright and Sergeant Shuttleworth undertook to arrange a pro- gramme for last Saturday evening, when many of the boys in khaki found it impossible to obtain admission, so popular was the event. Pte. T. O'Neill sang "Get out and get under"; Pte. P. F. Flanagan, " Li-ftle grey home in th 4 West" .and '" Irish eyes"; Sergt, IL Ellison, "Your King and country need you,'"'" So good- bye, daddy, good-bye" and That dreamy; dreamy .lullaby"; Pte. A. Lee, "Mountains ot Moore": Pte. R:.Delaney (who was .the accom- panist)," I woke up "; Sergts. F. Wright and A. Shuttleworth " Old bull and bush "; and, Pte. B. Kendrick, `_! Pontypridd disaster." Ptes. A Williams and G. Gilligan gave a clever bones duet; and Sergt. F. Wright contributed " The lost chord " as a cornet solo. The soldiers joined, as perhaps only " Tommies " can, in the choruses. What more than one described as the jolliest evening since they had been in --,-----7- 61osed with Sere... I': Wright praying "The soldier's farewell," followed by the regimental march, ‘` God,. bless the Prince of Wales," at the first bars of Which every-man smartly sprang tb attention, And the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>PRIVATE S. J. HOLLOWAY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE S. J. HOLLOWAY.</p>
        <p>"One 01 Th Best-liked In The Company."</p>
        <p>News has been received that Private Samuel James Holloway, of .Catherine-street, Bewsey, Warrington, has been killed in France. He was only 21 years of age, and before the war was employed at Dallam Forg,e. He enlisted in the RA.M.C. on the 12th (~-pteniber, 1914, but after a time he saw that his detachment was nct likely to go to the Front Very, quickly, and then migrated to the Ist Northamptonshire Regi- ment. He went to the Front in. September of last Year, and a. few Weeks 'later figured in th., Battle Of Loos. His wido,w, to whom he had been married three yiars, received news.on Wed' nesday, his friend. Private. E. Keeling, writing that ." he died well. doing, his duty, for he was nnt one of those fellows 'one had to ask twice to do anything, no matter how dangerous the ex.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE S. .T. HOLLOW 1-17. Killed.</p>
        <p>pedition was. He was one of the best-liked men in the. Company. As an athlete, he took some excelling, and there was not a. man who heard of his death who did not shed tears for one who was dear to us."</p>
        <p>It Was only on Sunday morning that 'Private Holloway 'wrote saying that he was expecting to ''get leave very soon. and the hopes, which Mrs. Holloway had built up in this respect only made the Lc ws of his death More acute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>RIFLEM IN J. W. STOCKTON.</p>
        <p>Possessor Of P.C.M. Wounded En</p>
        <p>Thigh And Elbow.</p>
        <p>Rifleman John William StOckton, of the 3rd Rifle Brigade, son of Mrs. Stockton, a. widow; re- siding at 2, Stout's-yard, Foundry-street, and the holder ,of the which he Won for services at La Breque. on September 10th, 1915, when under fire he . dug out three inen and carried them to a plifte of safety, has been wounded in the left thigh and elbow.</p>
        <p>Two letters have readied Mrs. Stockton from her son's comrades,. one of whom, Rifleman A. Daley," wrote :—".John has-been wounded in the „leg and elnow, the wound in the leg being rather a• nasty one. It happened this way. John and I could not find a position for our gun, for the Germans had blown all the trench in, the -previous day. Well, they sent over 'whiz-bangs' at night, .and as soon as daylight broke they sent over shells from all directions. It was terrible, and we could • not get into a place of safety, for there was none: It was</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN J. W. STOCKTON, D.C.M.</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>about 4.30 p.m. when Jvhn was hit by shrapnel, the. same piece catching me on the foot. A braver lad than Sohn never stepped on to the battlefield."</p>
        <p>Rifleman W. Allen wrote, wishing Rifleman Stockton .the bet of luck, and hoping • that he would Pull through all right. .</p>
        <p>Rifleman Stockton is now an: inmate of St. Bartholomew's Hospital, London, and is making satisfactory progress. He is-22 years Of age, and cousin to Rifleman Edward Walmsle3,-, whose death in action is reported in another, column He enlisted in September, 1914, and was drafted to the Front four months later. Previous to enlisting, he was employed as la hawker. by Mr. James Stewart, of Turner-street. He was educated at St. Ann's School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>DIED OF WOUNDS.</head>
        <p>DIED OF WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE G. BAXTER.</p>
        <p>Dies From \\ minds In Head .I.rid Side.</p>
        <p>Private George Baxter, a married man, whose home address was 3L .Scott-street, has died. from wounds, received whilst in action with the Welsh (Pioneer) Battalion. lie was wounded on the 23rd February, and died, on the 2nd March.</p>
        <p>' Writing on February 25th,. from - France, - a Chaplain said Mu. writing to let you know that your hushand,-: Private Baxter, has been wounded in the scalp, skull-and left side. Itis difficult tn'tell-hoiv'theSe wounds will turn out, but you should hcipe for the best and be pre- pared for the Worst. Your husband is receiving</p>
        <p>every care and attentibn from a medical point of view." •</p>
        <p>An official communication from the War Office was received on Tuesday, to say Private Baxter had died from wounds.</p>
        <p>Before enlisting, Private Baxter, who was 31 years of age, was employed as a cooper's labourer by Messrs. Jos. Crosfield and Sons, Ltd. He enlisted' on the 4th Oetober. He .was a good bowler, and won the last CroSfield</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>CAPTAIN CROSFIELD NICHOLSON.</head>
        <p>CAPTAIN CROSFIELD NICHOLSON.</p>
        <p>Killed AVliile Flying In England.</p>
        <p>Captain George Crosfield Norris Nicholson, of the Royal Flying Corps, only sorn of Sir Charles Nicholson, . Bart., M.P., cousin of , Sir Arthur Crosfield, and a grandson on his mother's side of the late .Mr. George. Crosfield, was killed on. Saturday while- flying in England after .several Months' service at the Front. Captain 'Nichol- .mas born in November, 1884, and educated at Eton and Clare,Csollege, Cambridge. In 1907 he was appointed assistant private. segretary• to Mr. E. Robertson, Parliamentary Secretary.. to the Admiralty, and in the following year went to the War Office as private Secretary to .Colonel Seely, then Under-Secretary for War. He be- came' prinAipal private secretary to Colonel Seely on his appointment as Secretary for Win in 1912.</p>
        <p>• He leaves a young 'wife, to whom his death will be a tthible blow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>TILLEY'S D.C.M.</head>
        <p>TILLEY'S D.C.M.</p>
        <p>lIONV Till' 17041 i hailer IVO!' Tht' 'MN Cie('</p>
        <p>honour.</p>
        <p>Two months ago we announced that company- Sergeant-Major J. H. Tilley, First sth Battalion South Lancashire Regiment (Ti?.), had been awarded the D.CAL The "London Gazette" this week records the fact: that the honour was conferred " For con spicuons gallantry.; he, . with three others, rescued :it buried man from a dug-out\under very heavy fire. Six ,shells fell in the farm, and One within five yards of the- party. On another .occasion., with: a small -party,' he attacked the 'enemy, and ;advanced I.oo'' yards' down their trench: 'As bomb sergeant at that time he was, continuogsly harassing': the enemy." • ' _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Sergeant-MajorATilliry formerly played- foot- 1N1.11..f0r Warrington, and is a 'member of the Widnes 'Territorials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>UNDER SHELL FIRE.</head>
        <p>UNDER SHELL FIRE.</p>
        <p>Midshipman Allen Gains The Distinguished Service Cross.</p>
        <p>Probationary Midship Man Reginald Allen, R.N,11., son Of Ml'. and Mrs. Edwin Allen, The Limes, Grappenhall, and Buttermarket-street, has had the D.S.C. conferred upon him for serviceg° perforthed,.by hlm under shell-fire on the beaches and in the-yessels' off:the beaches, as an . officer of the Eastern Mediterranean Squadron between the time of the landing in</p>
        <p>.Photograph by Parkinson. MIDSHIPMAN REGINALD ALLEN</p>
        <p>the Gallipoli Peninsula in April, 1915, and the evacuation in December, 1915—January, 1916. It will be remembered that Midshipman All was on the 'triumph when that vessel Was sunk in the Dardanelles last Whit-week azid that a great, sense of relief was felt when news came that he was • safe. That he was saved to some purpose is proved by the fact that his Sovereign has honoured him for the services lie had and has since rendered to, his' country. On the, sinking of the Triumph he was placed hi command of a motor lighter. These, he states, are most weird things, diffi- cult .to manoeuvre, with a tendency to go where the wind takes them. It is rather rough living, he says, but very amusing, if it were not so serious. He has been to all the opera- tions round the .Dardanelles, except Salonica. Holiday At His Old School.</p>
        <p>The boys at the Warrington Grammar SchoOt were given a holiday yesterday in celebration of . the .distinction that has fallen to the lot ot this "old. boy." Another;favoUr, came the way of his brother, Gunner William Allen, R:F.A., who is , training in Yorkshire, and whose- commanding officer sent him home for four days' leave ot absence in honour of the 'event.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Training Ship Proud of Him.</head>
        <p>Training Ship Proud of Him.</p>
        <p>From the 'School Ship 'Conway, on which Midshipman Allen spent two years in training, have come the following congratulatory letters .</p>
        <p>Mr. George D. Kelly: tlie-chainhan, writes: "On ,behalf of the Committee of MAnage- meat of the School Ship Conway, I beg to con- vey their warmest congratulations on the distinction conferred upon you by His Maje§tY the King fort gallant services performed under fire at Gallipoli. The award D.S.C. will afford great. gratification to your many friends ani to none" More than the members, of. my ,cora- mittee who rejoice to see such honour conferred on an 'old boy." Captain Br adbent, of the Conway, 'writes from Rock Ferry :</p>
        <p>"Dear , Mr. was glad indeed to see your son's name in the honours list 'yesterday and congratulate you on him. The Conway gets C.M.G. and two D.S.C.'s in Yesterday's est, and I know many men live done excellent work in small craft at Gallipoli. We have been very strongly represented in that part a the world."'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Mayor's Congratulations.</head>
        <p>The Mayor's Congratulations.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman Peacock), writing to Mr. Allen, says:—</p>
        <p>"My Dear Friend,—The Mayoress and my- self were highly gratified to learn of the dis- tinguished services rendered to this country by your , son Reginald. This notable event</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MARRIED MEN.</head>
        <p>THE MARRIED MEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Parker Explains The</p>
        <p>Local Position.</p>
        <p>DELAYS THROUGH UNPRE- PAREDNESS.</p>
        <p>An explanation of the position.of married men cid their grievance under the recruiting scheme and a declaration that, the Warrington Advisory Committee is doing its btst to eliminate from industrial concerns as many single menl,as pos- sible, were the main points in a speech de- livered bY Mr. C. E. Parker at the annual meet- ing of the Liberal Association, held on Wednes- day evening. He said undoubtedly the married men had been called up earlier than ,they had been led to expect. They anticipated when they attested that single men would be called to the Colours before them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>OUR UNPREPAREDNESS.</head>
        <p>OUR UNPREPAREDNESS.</p>
        <p>They undoubtedly had a grievance, but in this Matter it was necessary to consider the whole of the circumstances which had arisen. There were many things in connection with the war which could only be explained by our unpre- paredness. It was a fact that in, many ways they were unprepared. They had had to face difficulties set up by new machinery, and do a great- many things which were entirely new to them. They had been done hurriedly, and the consequence was that all along the line there had been a certain amount of confusion: The Advisory Committees and the Tribunals in War- rington and elsewhere which had to deal with appeals had found they had A big task before them. Such appeals had to be dealt with in a very careful manner,, because after all, they were dealing with family issues, which were of vital importance to the individual person con- cerned. The appeals could not be scamped or hurried in any way, and in this way longer time had been taken than was 'anticipated. The difficulties had been further increased by the alterations and revisionh made by the War Office and Board of Trade. ...Thik was responsible for a great .deal, of the delay. For instance, several lists of starred men had come forward for re- vision', and had brought about considerable con- fusion. Whilst on the one hand they had to con- sider industrial efficiency and the production of munitions, on the other they were faced with the problem that more men were wanted for the Army. They had the various training, (limps getting empty, and the officers were ready to train the men. The danger .was that they were losing time, and the result had been that 'the first of ,the married men had been called; before all the 'single men, had, gone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>'STARRED" MEN BEING THOROUGHLY OVERHAULED.</head>
        <p>'STARRED" MEN BEING THOROUGHLY OVERHAULED.</p>
        <p>An important point for everyone to know was that - the cases of the starred 'Single men were being thoroughly overhauled, and practi- cally a toothcomb would be put through the different works so that every single man who Could be spared would be sent to the Front, and the grievances which the married men held at the present time would be minimised. In Many cases, even where single men could not be replaced they would have to come forward. for, men were badly needed at the Front. He appealed to his hearers that as far as possible they should • try to assuage the feelings of .the married men, explaining to 'them that the Advisory Committees and Tribunals were work- ing hard in order that their Case might be justly met The difficulties had arisen as a result of unpreparedness and the launching of a scheme which was quite new and unknown to them. In France and s Germany they had the machinery, and there they had _merely to call up the men, because conscription was universal In dealing with this matter -there were vital interests and various businesSes to be considered, and in addition, besides making war products for themselves they were making them for their Allies-. It was not an easy thing to bring about the .dilution of labour and keep up the efficiency of industry and get at the same time as many men .as possible for the Front. To all married men who were concerned he would say that in Warrington- no single man now " starred " or " badged " would remain without his position was thoroughly investi- gated ti) see if it were possible for him to, be spared for the fighting line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>THRIFT IN LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THRIFT IN LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parker touched upon the importance of thrift, especially where it effected a saving in labour. If they could possibly manage without anything in particular they. ought . to do so, because all - consump- tion meant that labour was necessary for production. The question of the saving of labour was an economy quite as important as saving money to put in the War Loan. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>NO POSTPONEMENT.</head>
        <p>NO POSTPONEMENT.</p>
        <p>The Central News, telegraphing last night,</p>
        <p>ays:--</p>
        <p>It is not the government's intention to postpone the calling up Of the married men in Groups 25 to 32, inclusive, They are liable for service as already announced, April 7th: .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>COL.-SERGEANT MA EN HUDSON.</head>
        <p>COL.-SERGEANT MA EN HUDSON.</p>
        <p>Heath And Funeral Of Head Of</p>
        <p>Patriotic Family.</p>
        <p>•With 29 years' Army service .to his credit, Colour-Sergeant Owen Hudson, of 21, Warbur- ton-street, Winwick-road, has died full of honour and years. In 1857, when only 18 years of age, he joined the sth Northumberland Fusiliers at West- minster. Shortly afterwards he was drafted to India, and after seven years was sent to Ireland. Here he served for two years, at the close of which time he proceeded to Jersey for a similar p*riOd. Later he was transferred to Orford Barracks, where he served for 15 years, then obtaining his discharge. Later, however, he again entered the Army, this time as. drill instructor to the 6th Leicester Rifle Volunteers .at Loughborough, and served in this capacity, for three years. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Five Patriots.</head>
        <p>Five Patriots.</p>
        <p>Four of his sons have sitved in the A rmy,- one of whom, Owen Hudson, jun., has been killed during the present war. Colour-Sergeant Hudson was buried with full military honours on Monday, the Band of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment, under the conductorship of Mr. R. E. Cahill, heading the cortege. Buglers sounded the "Last Post" at the graveside. The Rev. Martin, curate of St. Peter's Church, officiated,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>UNDER SHELL F I R E.—Continued.</head>
        <p>UNDER SHELL F I R E.—Continued.</p>
        <p>standing to his honour. .must be extremely pleasant to your 'wife and self, particularly when we remember how sad we all felt in Warrington folio time during Whit-week, when we almost feared your son would have been lost in the disaster to H.M.S. Triumph. We had soon after that the good news of youir'sOn's safety, and now Warringlon will be doubly ,proud to find that he has added lustre to its fame."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>MIDSHIPMAN ALLEN.</head>
        <p>MIDSHIPMAN ALLEN.</p>
        <p>At The time of the disaster to the Triumph we gave an account of Midshipman Allen's career, but we may 'repeat that he was born In Warrington and will be 21 in May. On the Conway he won the Langton prize for seaman- ship and many certificates for nautical profi- ciency. He was one of four boys nominated from 220 Conway, scholars as a Royal Naval Reservist for midshipman rank. His first ship was. the 'Blleman. Company's City of Lincoln; he was transferred to the Walton Hall, \arm then •to the City Of Norwich, on Which he ter- minated his apprenticeship. When the war broke out, Midshipman Allen was on a return voyage from India, and Upon his arrival home obtained Special permission from, the Admiralty to sit for the second Mate's Board of Trade certificate. This he obtained. Then Called. up by the .Admiralty, he was appointed to mid- shipman's rating, and on -February 19th :took part in the first bombardment of the Dardan- elles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>rdttrs to or (Ettitar</head>
        <p>rdttrs to or (Ettitar</p>
        <p>UGH fING HINTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Those who have Venetian 'blinds should note that when they are turned one way the light is reflected up ißto the sky, and therefore possibly dangerous;" but when turned the other way all reflected light is thrown downwards and is harmless; • also unshaded or inadequately shaded. lights in the backs of houses are quite as dangerous as those in the front, although some in Warrington do not seem to think so. OBSERVER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTORS.</head>
        <p>CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTORS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Raving granted space in your last issue to a writer' %i-ho signs himself "-Patriot" to diS.l play his ignorance regarding the conscientious objector, I wonder if you will have the.fairness to insert a reply to the sane?. The game of bull-baiting and misrepresenting .the conscien- tious objector is evidently great sport to certban cowards who write anonymously in newspapers: It would be interesting if "Patriot" had the mot liness to declare himself, so that it could bq seen what claims he has to the title behind which he hides' his identity. I have no inten- tion of entering into controversy with one who, when he makes a scurrilous attack on others, has a "conscientious objection" to signing his name to his effusions, but if, such attacks are permitted in these columns; it is surely only fair play to allow the conFientious objector in the same way,to state his case.</p>
        <p>Let me; then, quote from a leaflet issued by the No-Conscription Fellowship "Upon what grounds do we base our refusal to take part in far? We have reached our decision in many different ways, some through Christian thought and influence, others thro</p>
        <p>association with movements which aim at the betterment of human life, both. - within the nation and among the nations. All of us are united in the conviction that every human per- sonality has a value .and sanctity that compel our respect and that we cannot violate.</p>
        <p>"We can-not assume the lesponsibility of end= lug the life of any man—triZ life that has come to him from the. Source of all life. We cannot decide for another the issues of life and death. Therefore we cannot take life ourselves, nor have any part in helping others to take life, And since the military machine • involves the wholesale slaughter of our fellows, we canlot assist its functions in any wa..j.</p>
        <p>"It is true that none of:us Can avoid contribut- ing indirectly to the work of war, in .which the whole nation is.enkaged. But we refuse to take any direct part in assisting the prosecution of .war, whether in, combatant or non-combatant 'capacity. All, war to us is Wrong, for-whatever purpose it May be Waged. We believe in solidarity and oneness-of mankind, which -4.-L•r directly- violatei. We believe in the brothr- hood of, Man, and we will not knowingly inflict Injury or loss upon our brother men. "All of us desire to serve our country, and we are .trying in our several ways to help for- ward all that is best in our country's life, air: to care for the welfare of our fellow Cl:allti7 "In declining the particular service which the State requires of us at this time, .vc think we are not acting against our countrY's truest interests. It is surely possible to love and help one's nation without dealing injury to any. other. We believe that rvice of the oin- munity in this sense is incambent upon evety citizen, but we ,protest against any attempt to enforce military service upon ourselves or 'our fellow men, or to organise our work in order to make the nation efficient in war. No man should be compelled to hand over absolutely' his will and conscience to the direction of an- other, especially when the purpose of this claim upon his obedience is repulsive to him. For this reason we cannot take the military oath, directly or by implication, or set ourselves under military authority." . The conscientious objector is one who believes that militarism cannot be destroyed by militar- ism, and that liberty and right cannot be secured_ by killing the conscript slaves of another State. He believes that by promoting goodwill amongst men be is doing more for the security of the nation than can ever be done by the building of fleets and the piling up of arinaments. He knows that wars are not between nations, as such, but between rulers; that the good of one nation is the good of all, and that an injury done to one is an injury to all; that nations as such cannot feel jealous of one another and cannot quarrel with one another, but that all quarrels spring from the aggressive imperialism of rulers. And, knowing this, he. sees that when an increas- ing number of people refuse to fight out such quarrels, wars will cease to be. Finally, the conscientious objector isione who cannot turn a moral and intelleetlial sbmersault and sacrifice all his religious and humanitarian convictions because he is bidden to fight out quarrels which are not his.</p>
        <p>H. T. DIXON. "Higlifields," Penketh, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>CHIIISTADELPHIAN CONSCIENCE.</head>
        <p>CHIIISTADELPHIAN CONSCIENCE.</p>
        <p>read from time to time the that is put before the Tribunals by in- dividuals who have suddenly developed a con- scientious objection" to military service, I re- ceived a very different impression in your town to-day.</p>
        <p>Occasion bringing me to Warrington, and hav- ing on my hands cA little time, I called in at your Tribunal, and the case of the Warrington Christadelphians was before the Court. Their case was the most exclusive and the strongest I have ever heard or read of before a Tribunal or elsewhere. . ,</p>
        <p>Their representative (Mr. Hobson) brought such facts to hear that, to my Mind, actually demonstrated that the Christadelphians' con- science was real and worthy .of respect. , He .(Mr. Hobson) said the Christadelphians as a body never exercised the franchise; were not represented in Parliament; accepted the laws and ruling of this country without complaint or comment; paid their rates and taxes gladly; were not shirkers; and kept their registers in such a fashion that none could enter only arter a very thorough examination, and none could contravene their laws without elimination from the register. In addition to this, the five objectors had,been connected with the body for over four, and. some ten, years. The representative said that their objection was a religious, Biblical, conscientious object- tion, amir they could not, under any circum- stances (irrespective of consequences) take any part or lot in the military machine, either com- batant or non-combatant, as they as a body had sworn allegiance to Christ,. Who was coming to set up His Kingdom and destroy all other king- doms.</p>
        <p>He therefore claimed absplute .exemption, which was provided for in Clause 16 of "Instrue, lions to Tribunals."</p>
        <p>Mr. Hobson further said the ChristadellAians, had been granted absolute exemption by the American Government in the American Civil War, and he claimed like treatment. Yet in spite of these glaring " facts" the Tribunal re- fused their appeal, and relegated them to non- combatant duties.</p>
        <p>The Chairman remarked that seeing some of the applicants were engaged in.. wire-drawing, he failed to see much 'distinction between help- ing the war in this way—and fighting. It occurs to me forcibly that on this line of reasoning a conscientious objector cannot exist, seeing Lord Selborne only said last week that "turnips were as important to the country as shells."</p>
        <p>I again say, surely our local Tribunals defeat the very object the Government had in view when they overrule such a conclusive and exclu- sive case as was presented by the Christadel- phians to-day. • Members of the Tribunals are instructed that "they must interpret the Act in an impartial and tolerant spirit," giving every consideration to the man whose "objection genuinely rests on religious and moral convictisns."_ It occurred to me that the very conditions required for this clause to apply to were em- bodied in the documentary evidence of the ap- pellants', especially seeing some of them were alitsady *exempted on reserved Goveininental work, but who preferred to appeal on religious conscientious grounds Only. Surely such action of the ' Tribunals nullify the very letter and spirit'pf the Act as applic-'• • ! able to genuine Conscientious oases. : ‘l, The appellants noticed .their. intention of 'ap- pealing to the Higher Con •t:, FAIRPLAY:.</p>
        <p>1 t 1 ' I i . , I Fl' 4 jt t .11t- ',i-,' 1 , ' , w i „. I</p>
        <p>, 4 r .</p>
        <p>**:</p>
        <p>' • t</p>
        <p>! ;; '4,-,</p>
      </div>
