      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>OVA \ 41, )141 . •</head>
        <p>OVA \ 41, )141 . •</p>
        <p>r ZZZA I</p>
        <p>-• . -It 4 - --......, -, • _ ,' •:::: ..,' ' / --.1.• . L ' .:..: ,V, V w • . . . „' r :'.... '-X,.' lIIIA iCTI ~. f ....,.... • ...: 1.,. ~..,, - :.-...•-•-• ;'- . -. e ....,,...., ......." , . ... - -,- . ..... .. - ''' : ..."--: ......;.•</p>
        <p>Magistrates on the Bench.—At I,ynarn Petty Sessions, on Wednesday, Mr. Alfred Watkin presided, and the other justices present were: Messrs. Ti J. Ridgway, J. Coxon. S. qould, S. Davies and R. H. Gotobed.</p>
        <p>Private S. C. Percival.—Private S. C. Perci- val, who is in the East, in' a letter to Mr. NV. Moston, of 20, Newfield Cottages, Lymm, writes to say he never worked so hard in his life as he is working now. He thinks we shall soon have sufficient men to give the Germans what they are asking for.</p>
        <p>Suspension of Postal Collections.—The Post- master begs to announce that, commencing on Monday next, the collections of letters from Booth's Hill Post Office at 8.45 p.m., the Cross letter-box at 9 p.m.,' Lymm Post Office at 9 p.m. and the Railway Station letter-box at 9.15 p.m. will be suspended until further notice. The Lights That Failed.—Jeffery L. Woolas- ton, who works at St. Helens and lives at Dirleton House, Priory-road, Sale, was charged with riding a motor cycle on the Warrington- road, Lymm, at night without either front or rear light.—Defendant's father stated that his son was out for his firstride on a second-hand machine that he had bought on approval with the assurance that everything was in order. The lamp, had, however, gone out.—Fined 5s Corporal Joseph Johnson.—Corporal. Joseph Johnson, who for upwards of seven years was a member of the local Territorials, has been to Lymm on a short furlough this week. A former resident. of Lymm until a short time ago, he joined the Lymm Territorials, but after a period of about two years he obtained a situation at Hampstead Heath, and was transferred to the 7th Battalion of the Middle- sex Regiment. Interviewed by our representa- tive yesterday, he said he was xery pleased to visit the old place, but although he had met a good many friends in ,the village, Lymm did not seem the same without the old boys. He is now medically unfit fOr father active service and has been given a berth as drill instructor over Derby recruits.</p>
        <p>Gained 5d.; Lost 25s.—A passenger from War- rington to Heatley was charged with travelling on the L. and N.-W.'llailway.from Warrington to Lymm without having paid his fare on January 23rd. On the arrival of the train at Heatley Station defendant, who had no ticket, represented that he had travelled from Lymm and paid his fare from that place. He had, earlier in the day, taken a single ticket to Warrington. When a detective visited ' him, upon a report made by the Heatley station- master, he admitted that he came from War- rington and must have forgotten his ticket through being in conversation. He had no in- tention of defrauding the :company.—The Chairman, commenting •upon the defendant's respectability and honesty to date, said people thought they could rob the railways and were doing nothing wrong.—Fined 10s. anti. 15s. costs.</p>
        <p>LIYMM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Lynun Platoon. Orders for week commencing March 18th:— Monday.—Fall in Drill Hall, 7 p.m., Shooting (sealed handicap). Tuesday.—Fall in Drill Hall, 7 p.m., Shooting (sealed handicap). Thursday.—Fall in Drill Hall. 7.15 p.m., Shooting; 8 p.m., Platoon Drill. By Order, G. H. WILLIAMSON, Platoon Commander.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>USEFUL GUIDE</head>
        <p>A USEFUL GUIDE</p>
        <p>If one thing more than another is useful Surely it is the succession of helpful messages from men and women which have been fcr so many years-a feature of.these columns. _ .</p>
        <p>Earnest statements like the following are a guide to many who might etherwise become disd couraged. On March 16th, t 907, Mrs. K. Dooney, of 51, Forster-street, Orford-lane, Warrington, said I suffered for a long time with my kidneys, azd pains in my back. These pains were so acute that at times I could scarcely get up and down stairs. If I stooped they became much worse, and I found it hard to straighten my- self. I had bad headaches, , and often came over so, dizzy that I was afraid of falling. Th., water was cloudy and caused great uain when relieving the bladder. _ _</p>
        <p>'At fast I made up my mind to try Doan'.l Backache Kidney Pills. They soon easwd me or the pains and remedied the water trouble. 1. felt a different woman after taking Doan's Pills, and I picked up wonderfully. I have told many of my friends what these pills did for me."</p>
        <p>On February 18th, 1916—nearly nine years later —Mrs. Dooney said :—" I am keeping very well now, and I have only Doan's Pills to thank for it. They have been a real boon to me." Kidney complaint occasions many ailments, for when the kidneys are clogged ,and faulty, uric acid and fluid waste are thrown back upon the system, causing rheumatism, stone, bladder inflammation, dropsy, sciatica and obstinata pain and discomfort. Incurable disease may follow if treatment is neglected, but timely help is generally success- ful. Doan's Pills have saved thousands of men and women from uric acid poisoning and kidney disease, and even in advanced stages have effected many cures. Of all dealers, or 2s. 9d. a box, from Foster- McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for backache or kidney pills—ask distinctly for Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, the same as-Airs...Doomq</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>PRIVATE SAMUEL JE'NNINGS.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE SAMUEL JE'NNINGS.</p>
        <p>Killed In Action.</p>
        <p>News has this week been received to the effect that Private Samuel Jennings, of the King's Liverpool Regiment, was killed in action on Sun. day. It was conveyed to Mrs. Wm. Jennings, of Newfields, Lymm, the deceased soldier's mother, throngh means of a letter sent by the deceased soldier's wife, Who resides at Bootle, Liverpool, and reads as follows :—" Dear Mother,-1 don't know how to tell you the sad news I have to tell, but it's about poor Sam; he was killed in action last Sunday. I had a letter from his commanding officer, which in- formed me of his death, but I can hardly be- lieve it, for it came so sudden. This will be as big a shock to you as it was to me, but be brave, for it is the Lord's wish.—Your broken- hearted daughter, Lizzie." Private Jennings was a married man, anct resided with his wife at Bootle. He was 28 years old, and prior to living at Bootle, resided with his mother at Moston-street, Newfields. He was a well-known local man, being educated at Lymm School, and for several years served in the local company of Volunteers (now Terri torials) During the early part of his life ho was apprenticed to the late Councillor F. 0 Moston as a fustian cutter, and after the great dock strike he went as a dock labourer to Bootle, where he remained until he joined the Colours soon after the outbreak of war. After a long spell of training in different parts of the country he was drafted out to the Front two months ago. His brother, Private James Jen- nings, is also serving with the Colours. paving joined the 115th Manchester' Regiment, and is now with the Mediterranean ,Expeditionary Force •</p>
        <p>This gallant soldier, prior to joining the Forces, was employed for several years as a vamnan for Messrs. E. Brown and Sons, grocers, of Lymm, and later at the Co-operative Soap Works at Irlam. His" brother-in-law, Driver W. Barker, is a member of the West Lancs. Division R.E., and is now stationed at Bangor, North Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>CORPORAL HENRY BROOKES.</head>
        <p>CORPORAL HENRY BROOKES.</p>
        <p>Thanks The Villagers For Their Gifts</p>
        <p>Of Comforts.</p>
        <p>Corporal Henry-Brookes, of the 1/sth Cheshire Regiment, writing to his mother this week at 1, West, Hyde, Newiields, gives a graphic account of the recent heavy fighting of his regiment in France, and states The Germans have been sustaining enormous Casualties lately, and the way. things are going on there is every likeli- hood of them cracking up before long. Sergt.- Major Clare pealed through some kind friends last week for some socks for the Lymm fellows, as we were all bad/y in need of them, and I am glad to say that a supply reached him yester- day. They were just the things we required for the long marches. Will you please state this in 'The Examiner,' so that they can see we appreciate such a kindness? We are all glad to hear of Sergeant Moston's arrival at Belfast, as we know that there he will be assured of having a well-earned rest."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LYIVIM CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH</head>
        <p>LYIVIM CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH</p>
        <p>Social And Annual Report.</p>
        <p>A veil pleasant evening was spent on Tues- day in the Congregational Church in connec- tion with the reading of the annual report. It took the form of a social and entertainment, about. 80 members and friends of the 'church being present. Songs were very effectively rendered by Mesdames 31. Appleton, C. Lewis and E. Lewis, and Messrs. C. Leather, B. Walker and E. C. Barrett. Recitations were also given by Miss Pva Walker and Mr, E. C. 'Barrett, and the humorous part of the pro- gramme was in the able hands of Mr. Samson, who highly delighted the audience with a, capital rendering of "The Vicar's Presenta- tion." A special feature of the entertainment was the singing of "The Last Hymn" and The Day,' by Miss Hegg, of Oughtrington. At -various intervals the choir gave selections, under 'the direction of Mr. A. Leather, and during the interval light refreshments were provided.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Hewitt (church secretary), in pre- senting his report, said that there were about SS members on the church books, compared with 39 for the year ,pievious. This wa-5 pr- ticuiarly pleasing to report, especially in view of such trying times. The &amp;why evening serricn were being better attended. _</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Holt (treasurer) stated tfrat last yeai they began with 02.34 98. 6d. on the wrong side, but by arduous and strenuous work they had managed to wipe off .218 17s. id. of the debt, which left a balance of £l5 12s. sd.</p>
        <p>In presenting the Sunday school report the Superintendent (Mr. G. Willan) said that there were on the register 160 scholars, and that the average attendance had been 128, which was considered satisfactory. The Clothing Club had 123 members, and up to the present there had been deposited £5. The Band of Hope had about 60 members, and the average at- tendance was 50. On the Roll of Honour were 50 names and, said the Superintendent, many of their young men had sacrificed all on behalf of King and country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>THEFT FROM A FARM.</head>
        <p>THEFT FROM A FARM.</p>
        <p>Labourer Who Was Spending Alone)</p>
        <p>Freely.</p>
        <p>Harold Hampson, a farm labourer, of no fixed address, was charged with stealing two ,currenrjr_notes. or 1 each, between March .3rd</p>
      </div>
