      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 18, 1916.</p>
        <p>and March 6th, the property of Samuel Sed- don, Camsley Farm, by whom he was formerly engaged as labourer.</p>
        <p>Mabel Seddon stated that on March 3rd she left home in the morning and returned at night. She placed the key of the house under the rafters of the shed near the house. On the following Monday she noticed the kitchen win- dow was unfastened. On going to the cup- -board in the dining-room for her purse she found two .21 notes had been taken. On the sth she met Hampson going towards the house. He had been employed on the farm for some months, but had left the week before.. He had 'seen her go tinto..the dining-room for money, when her husband wanted to pay him. He knew 'she put the key in the-wash-house, but not in the rafters of the shed. He subbed his money through the woek and had only a few shillings to draw at the week-end. She had always found him honest and. she would be glad if the Bench would give him another chance:</p>
        <p>Mary Ellen Daniels, Newfield, barmaid at the N,ag's _Head, said on Sunday,,-March 6th, Hampson came to the house at four o'clock in the afternoon and spent 9d. on Oxo„ tea and a sandwich. He paid with a otl note, and said to her, when .she was going for the change; "Remember this is a pound note Sergeant Davies ,said that when he saw pri- soner and -told him he was-making inquiries about the robbery, he replied: "I was near the Nag's Head. A man I did not know paid for my tea and I gave him the change in the yard. It is two years since I last saw the man." On taking prisoner to the Police Station he was found to have only a halfpenny in his possession. Prisoner said he was as innocent as a baby. Mrs. Seddon had trusted -thim in charge of the house on several occasions. Sergeant Davies, in answer to the Bench, said he had learnt that the prisoner spent- money freely and left the district early on the Monday morning for Manchester, Sale and Altrincham. The Chairman said there was only circum- stantial evidence in the case, but the Bench were quite sure prisoner took the notes. Mrs. Seddon had made an appeal for another chance for the prisoner and they were going to give him one. He would be bound over to come up for judgment if called upon. Prisoner: Thank you, very much.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>LICENSEES AND DRUNKEN MEN.</head>
        <p>LICENSEES AND DRUNKEN MEN.</p>
        <p>A -Publican's Discretion.</p>
        <p>Mary Moston, licensee of the Anchor Inn, Lymm, was summoned at the Petty Sessions on Wednesday for permitting drunkenness to take place on her licensed premises on Feb- ruary 12th. Mr. Caldecutt, Knutsford, who appeared for the police, said that if he satisfied the Court that a person was drunk on the licensed pre- mises the onus was on the licensee to prove that she took all reasonable care to prevent it. At. 9.30 p.m. on Saturday, February 12th, Sergeant Davies and Acting-Sergeant Griffiths visited the hOus? to serve a notice under the Liquor Control Order. In the bar they saw three men—one named Creean, who was alleged to have been drunk, and two other men. The husband of the licensee, Henry Moston, was serving behind the counter. Creean was sitting with his head down between his knees. The Sergeant spoke to Creean, who took no notice. He then roused him, but the man denied that he was asleep. On request, the man tried to stand up, but fell against the table and sub- sequently against a petition. The Sergeant asked Mr. Moston whether he saw that the man was drunk. Mr. Moston made a noise with his lips, evidently meaning. "Dear, dear !" Mrs. Moston came into the room and said: "Well, you had better go out"; and Creean staggered towards the back-door. Told of his mistake, he turned to the front door and was in so helpless a condition outside that he was locked up. Mrs. Moston told the officers she was not very well and had not been up long, but the man had not been served there; and jle was not going to suggest that he was served. Mr. Moston said he did not notice the man's presence, being busy at the time, but there was no evidence that he was particularly busy. Creean was fined subsequently; and a licensee had no right to have a man on the premises one minute if he was in a iltunken condition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Tenant for 45 Years.</head>
        <p>Tenant for 45 Years.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Davies bore out Mr. Caldecutt's statement; and in cross-examination by Mr. Hockin (Manchester), who appeared for the defence, said he believed one woman came into the house to be served, but not more. Creean was absolUtek quiet. Mrs. Moston had held the licelice :!5 year's and her former husband held it 20 years before, or 45 years in all, without a conviction.</p>
        <p>And the only thing against the licensee on this occasion was that this man was found there seated on a stool, perfectly quiet, and he turned out to be drunk? It is not sug- gested that he was supplied?—We have no evidence.</p>
        <p>Then, without evidence, a police-sergeant does not, or should not, make the suggestion? Am I right in supposing he had not been served?—Yes.</p>
        <p>Acting-Sergeant Griffiths said Creean stated that he had been served, but this Mr. Moston denied. Creean appeared as if -he was either asleep or drunk. Mr. Hockin : If the alternative for all men were that they are either asleep or drunk, it would be God help the magistrates and many others. (Laughter.) Thomas Dolly said he was out with Creean on the day in question. They had had some drink. He went into the Anchor Inn about nine o'clock and Creean, followed him in five minutes before the officers entered. He was not served, but sat down quietly to wait for him. There were five or six men in the bar and women were fetching their aupper , beer. Mr. Moston could not see Creean, who was sitting behind the customers. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Publican's Discretion.</head>
        <p>A Publican's Discretion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hockin said it was clearly a case for investigation, but no case had been made out for conviction. Mrs. Moston had occupied the house 45 years without complaint, and a char- acter that had stood so long a test in a public- house was something to go upon. Dolly left Creean, who did not want any further drink; but the latter followed him in, unnoticed by. Mr. Moston, who was engaged serving in what' was the busiest part of the day. It was a fundamental principle of the law that one could not "'permit" unless one had a know- ledge of which was being permitted, unless the licensee were guilty of neglect and of wil- lingly closing his eyes. Mr. Caldecutt said they had no right to allow a drunken man to remain, on licensed premises for a minute; but Judge Bankes had ruled that a licensee must be allowed discretion in the ejection of a drunken person, and said that the wisest time to act was when the man was likely to go away quietly. Had the Judge's advice beep acted upon in this case, Creean would hai-e gone away quietly • ith his friend, whereas he was made to go out and so got locked up. Their case was stronger than the one dealt with ,by Judge Bankes, for they did not know the man was in the h^use.</p>
        <p>Henry Moston, husband of the licensee, said he was exceedingly busy when the officers arrived; and William. Moston, .his son, who was waiting upon the rooms other than the bar, said Creean was not in the louse five minutes before the arrival of the officers, Dismisseo By Majority Decision.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, stated that the Bench, by a majority, had decided to dismiss the summons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>£7lO LESS.</head>
        <p>£7lO LESS.</p>
        <p>Guardians To Ask For 2,19,000 From The Ratepayers.</p>
        <p>It was decided at a meeting of the Board of Guardians last week to make a call on the ratepayers for £19,000, although the esti- mates for the half-year ending 30th Septembe^ next amount to .219,980. For the correspondin,, pericit last year the ratepayers' toll was £19,710: so the demand is slightly less. Despite careful attention to economy, the- in- creased cost of provisions has resulted in it being estimated that it will take £4,500 to main-. tam n the patients in the Workhouse, as 'against £4,161 for the corresponding period last year. It may be added that there is not a single able- bodied man in the institution.</p>
        <p>Out-relief (including non-resident poor and boarding-out) is to cost £3,000 against £3,065; and the maintenance of lunatics in aulums 2,3,000 against e. 2.981.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>The Bench.—The following were the magis- trates on the Bench at the Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday :—Messrs. F. •A. Frost, Henry Roberts, J. Ilepherd, J. A. Ransome, C. Jackson Holmes and T. Baxter.</p>
        <p>Advisory Committee.—The Stockton Heath Advisory Committee held their longest sitting on Wednesday evening at the Police Station. Some 90 'odd cases were dealt with, but the list • was not finished, and the committee adjourned to this (Saturday) afternoon at 2.30. Dog Has His Night.—Joshua Starkey, Brook House, Stockton Heath, was summoned for allowing his dog to be at large through the night.. fie explained that men wishing to get cattle to a slaughter-house had released the dog; but he was fined 2s. 6d. and advised to chain the dog up somewhere else. Negligent •.Driver.--At the Sessions on Tues- day, Harold Wibberley, 9, Taylor-street, lower Walton, was fined 3s. 6d. for sot having proper .control of two horses attached to a lurry he was driving at Stockton ,Heath on February Ist.— Defendant, who was sitting on the shafts, Admitted the offence, but said he had reins' to the Ichain horse.</p>
        <p>Missing Lights.—At the Sessions on Tues.day, Henry Law, 17, Alfred-street, Stockton Heath, was fined 10s. for riding a motor bicycle without lights in Walton-road at nine o'clock at night. When stopped by a constable he said it was moonlight, and he thought it would be "all right."—Richard Butler, 18, Blue Coat-street, Warrington, was charged under the new Light- ing Order with being in Fharge of a lurry in London-road, Stockton Heath, at 7.40 p.m. with- out carrying a rear red light. When stopped, the defendant told the constable that the firm could not supply the lamps yet. The constable asked him why ,he had not lighted an ordinary carriage lamp that was on the lurry which would have shown some red. Defendant said there was no place to fix it at the back, but it was pointed out that one of the two men he ;was carrying might have held ,the lamp; the reply was that it was too cold to hold a lamp. Superintendent Owen pointed out that the maxi- mum- penalty for this offence was £lOO. De- fendant, who said he worked-for Cunningham's, explained that the lamps had arrived that morning. He was fined ss:—John Arnold, 4, Greenall's-avenue, Warrington, was summoned for having failed to carry a light at the front and at the rear of a lurry he was driving at 6.55 on the 4th February at Acton Grange. He told Constable Hough, who asked him for an explanation, that he thought lights were "due" at seven o'clock. Fined ss.—Joseph Thompson, 70, Victoria-terrace, Stockton Heath, for a similar offence at the same time and place, was fined ss. He told the constable he was sorry, but he did not think it was lighting up time.</p>
        <p>in the circumstances press such a case. By Mr. Hough: When did you ask these con- tractors to dor this work P—ln Januaky, Febru- ary and March. And you got your notice last October?—No. Talking about consciences, you receive rents from this property ?—Yes. And you say the Council should not consider the tenants as well as the landlord?—They are there for that purpose, but they should not expect un- reasonable things. Defendant was fined 205., and an order was made for the work to be done at once.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>UNDARKENED WINDOWS.</head>
        <p>UNDARKENED WINDOWS.</p>
        <p>Manxman Who Went To Bed Without Drawing Blind.</p>
        <p>At the Daresbury Petty Sessions, held at Stockton Heath on Tuesday, John Lewin Gawne, who said he Came from the Isle of Man to work at Whitecross, and who lodges at 15, Fairfield- road, was summoned for failing to shade or screen the light in his bedroom at 10.45 on March sth.—lnspector Breeze stated that .when he was in Ellison-road he saw the light coming from the rear of houses in Fairfield-road. On approach- ing the place he could see defendant, who had not drawn the blind, in his bedroom. Defend- ant, on being spoken to next day, said it was pure neglect, and the light was not in more than ten minutes.—Superintendent Owen said the man's landlady had been served with a copy of the Order, as others had been served.— Defendant said he and his son occupied the same room, and they would willingly do without a light if it was necessary in the interests of the country. He was earning 4s. a day, out of which he had to support his wife at home. There was a blind in the room and it was iEnorance and thoughtlessness that caused the omission to draw it.—Defendant was fined 55., and told that that was very cheap. He was understood to say that he had not ss. in the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>" Forgot" the Zeppelins.</head>
        <p>" Forgot" the Zeppelins.</p>
        <p>Louisa Hayes, 46, London-road, Stockton Heath, summoned for having failed to shade or reduce an inside light, or to screen the window as required by the recent_Order at 8.10 p.m. on the 7th inst., was reported to have left the dis- trict for Newcastle-on-Tyne.—lnspector Breeze said when he saw light coming rrom the defend- ant's dwelling-house he asked Mrs. Hayes why she had not screened the window, and she said she forgot all about it. There was neither. blind nor curtain to the window.—Fined 10s. Defendant's Denial.</p>
        <p>' Elizabeth Moss, 55, London-road, Stockton Heath, who keeps a draper's shop, was sum- moned for not screening her window at 7.55 p.m. on the 7th inst.—lnspector Breeze said there was a brilliant light in the shop and, no blind to the window, fanlight or door. Miss Moss told him that she thought she would be right so long as there was no light in the window.— Defendant said that before the officer's visit she had the fanlight pasted over with paper and the gas cover painted,blue. There was no light in the window itself, which was full of goods, and the door blind was down.—A fine of 10s. was inflicted\</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>COUNCILLOR HIS OWN HOUSE REPAIRER.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR HIS OWN HOUSE REPAIRER.</p>
        <p>But Fined For Neglecting An Order.</p>
        <p>Samuel Gough; 106, Winwick-street, Warring- ton, was summoned at the Sessions on Tuesday for having failed to comply with a notice served lupon him to abate a nuisance caused by damp- ness at houses owned by him, Nos. 1 to 7, White- field-road, Stockton Heath. Mr. W. E. Hough, acting clerk of the Runcorn Rural District Council, stated that tie defend- ant Was the owner of these substanSal houses in Whitefield-road, for which ss. 6d. a week rent was received. So far back as last October it was reported to the Council that the tenants were seriously inconvenienced to the extent of a nuisance from the dampness on the walls of the houses, particularly the bedrooms, due to defective roof and gutters. Informal notice was given to Mr. Gough by the inspector who, in December, reported to the committee and was instructed to give statutory notice for the, abatement of the nuisance within 21 days That notice was served January 4th, and since then some attempt had been made to deal with th, nuisance, but the essential part of the work had been neglected. He understood the excuse would be shortage of labour, but, every opportunity_ had been given to Mr. Gough, who seemed to be rather indifferent, and allowed the nuisance to continue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>Defendant and Magistrates' Clerk.</head>
        <p>Defendant and Magistrates' Clerk.</p>
        <p>George W. Lloyd, inspector of nuisances, said he found a nuisance existing at these• houses throggh defective spouting and roof. The order he had served on the defendant had not been complied with. By the defendant: Did I not explain it was, almost an impossibility to get men?-7.—Yes•—..A.m.l you know yourself ithat those who do not employ men regularly find it difficult to get men?—l know the time you have been allowed has been plenty, in all the circumstances.— Answer n;ty question, is it difficult to get men? Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. henry Greenall): That is common kw :,!:•ilga. Defendant (to witness): Do you know that local authorities are having the greatest diffi- culty in the world to get the necessary men—? Magistrates' Clerk: I don't think you can get any One to answer questions of that sort. He is not here to</p>
        <p>Defendant (to the Bench): I ask for your Worships' protection. I don't think the Clerk ought to interrupt me while 1 am asking a witness questions. Magistrates' Clerk: 1. am here to advise the Bench.</p>
        <p>Defendant: Yu may lc' that, but You al— interfering with my questicns. Magistrates' Clerk: You are not gcing to piP: questions that are immaterial to the ease.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Councillor Gough Falls From a Ladder.</head>
        <p>Councillor Gough Falls From a Ladder.</p>
        <p>Defendant proceeded to ask questions as to the requirements of men by the military authorities and the state of the weather during the last two months; and to show that he himself had been working on the houses and had hurt him- self through falling from a ladder. Giving evidence, Councillor Gough mentioned six local firms he had approached to do the work, with- out success. No men with 'any conscience would</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>COUNCILLOR ROBBED.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBBED.</p>
        <p>Erring Housekeeper Promises To Abstain From Drink.</p>
        <p>Margaret Smith, 57 years of age, of no settled address, but lately. employed as housekeeper by Councillor W. H. J. Jenkins at Stockton Heath; was charged at the Sessions on Tuesday with stealing between December 4th and 'February Bth, a quantity of wearing apparel and bedding value £l2 15s. 6d., the property of her employer. Superintendent Owen stated that these articles had been stolen and pawned in Warrington. where the transactions became so numerous as to raise the suspicions of the pawnbrokers, who notified the police.</p>
        <p>Representatives from Mr. Doraville's shop, Knutsford-road; Mr. Tyrer's shop, 62, Winwick- street; and Mr. Houghton's, Mersey-street, proved that goods produced were pawned on various dates.</p>
        <p>Detective Skelton, to whom she admitted the thefts, arrested the ,prisoner and handed her over to Constable Crompton, of the Cheshire police. There were found upon the prisoner. 13 pawmtickets, and on Constable Crompton and Acting-Sergeant Wilcockson searching Mr Jenkins's house they found 23 more, with the result that the whole of the stolen property was recovered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>First Offence At 57.</head>
        <p>First Offence At 57.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Owen said this was the woman's first offence. She had been living with friends in Warrington and was going out char- ring. She had now gone back to a place she had for two years before going to Mr. Jenkins. She was married and living apart from her husband.</p>
        <p>Prisoner was bound over for twelve months, and placed under the supervision of the Pro- bation Officer, Mr. Laws. The Chairman said it was understood that the cause 'of this trouble was that prisoner had taken to drink, but had now signed the pledge. This lenient treatment was given her on the understanding that she would keep the pledge. If she did not do so, or if she offended again she would have to come up for judgment. Prisoner gave the assurances asked.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>"BRITANNIA'S AWAKENING."</head>
        <p>"BRITANNIA'S AWAKENING."</p>
        <p>United Band Of Hope Present Interesting Cantata.</p>
        <p>A grand concert was given in the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Monday 6vening, the Rev. George Curtis (president) occupied the chair. The first part of the programme consisted of a welt- chosen cantata entitled "Britannia's Awaken- ing," which was admirably rendered by the members of the Band of Hope, the performance demonstrating that Britannia is at last awaken- ing to the evils of intemperance. Miss Sarah Gamble, as Britannia, took her part exceedingly well, filling that dignified position in a graceful manner. Other leading characters were: Prime Minister, William Tabern; Secretary, Arthur Walton; Lord Bung, Joseph Williams; Temperance, Isabel Clarke; Religion, Ada Itamsbottom; in addition to May day lasses, messengers, Empire maidens, &amp;c. Solos were sweetly sung by A. Ramsbottom, I. Clarke and A. Walton, whilst the chorus singing and recitations were exceedingly well done.</p>
        <p>Miss A. Bell created much fun by her mono- logue, "My Golosh." Miss Evelyn Todd gave a splendid representation of " Grace Darling," her singing of the old-time song being much enjoyed. A Lancashire recitation by Miss May Elliott was much appreciated. ' Miss Ada Hamer proved herself to be a very promising young vocalist by her rendering of "Down the Vale." A . very pretty effect was introduced by an action song, "An Umbrella Song," by a number of girls in English and Japanese costumes. An action song, "The Wakenfuffen Band," with Arthur Walton as conductor, caused much amusement by their singing and comical actions, and were deservedly encored. These action songs and the cantata reflected great credit on their trainer. Miss May Isherwood, a former May Queen of the Warrington Band of Hope Union. A humorous sketch was ably performed by the Warrington Temperance Guild Sketch Party, entitled "Tea and Scandal," Misses S. Fox, A. Bell, M. Elliott, V. Smith, E. Fox, A. Hamer, M. Tanner, E. Oakley and Mr A. Mellor taking their respective parts. Miss Elsie Whitfield and Miss E. Oakley ably acted as accompanists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>'WARE THE CANAL.</head>
        <p>'WARE THE CANAL.</p>
        <p>Darkness Makes Motoring Risky Police Approached.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee" held in the Council Schools on Tuesday evening, it was decided to tell the householders that in future the ash- bins would have to contain nothing but ashes when collected. This step .was arrived at in con- sequence of the fact that there was a difficulty in finding another tip, that paper from the-pres- ent tip was constantly blowing over the road- ways, and complaints had been made by several residents.</p>
        <p>The charges of the cost of the maintenance of lighted and unlighted street lamps, as suggested by the Warrington Corporation, were discussed, and the price quoted by them, viz., ss. per lamp, per annum, was considered to be excessive. Consequently, it was decided to approach the Corporation' with a view to getting a modified charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Unlighted Lamp Dangers.</head>
        <p>Unlighted Lamp Dangers.</p>
        <p>The Chairman stated that the three places at which street. lamps had been extinguished in Latchford Without were now very dangerous corners, and there had been some very narrow escapes. The lamp which had been extinguished at the bottom of Fairfield-road leading into Common-lane, should be lighted, for the spot was a most dangerous one, Several motorists having almost run into the Canal at night. He had approached the police .on the Matter, but as yet none of the lamps had been re-lighted. There were present at the Parochial Com- mittee meeting Mr. G. Pilling (presiding), and Messrs. H. J. Westbrook J. Hallows, W. Mclntyre, J. H. Clarke, F. Pearson, A. Bottom- ley, J. W. Hallows, J. McDermid, W. E. Hough (clerk), A. S. Wright (surveyor), and G: W. Lloyd (sanitary inspector). At the conclusion of the .Parochial Committee meeting a Parish Council meeting was held, over which Mr. W. Mclntyre presided, but it only lasted about three .ininutes; no business of public interest being transacted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 19th March : Sunday.—Route , March. Fall in Victoria- square, 2.15. Monday.—ln place of the usual drill there will be a Lecture by the Company-Sergeant-Major in the Hall Cale at 7.30.</p>
        <p>Wednesday.—Shooting Competition between teams representing the Warrington, Lymm. Grappenhall and Stockton Heath Detachments at the Drill Hall, to commence at 6.30 p.m. Saturday.--Uniform Parade. Fall in Victoria • square 3 o'clock sharp. By Order, F. WARBURTON, OffiCar Commanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>GREAT SALNKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SALNKEY.</p>
        <p>Lights Seen From a o!stan:e.—Snmmoned" before the magistrates a the Warrington Petty Sessions on Wednesday, William Wilson, of Brookside, Sankey, was fined 10s. for an offence against the Lighting Order. A policeman stated that he saw the lights in his house when stand- ing a quarter of a mile away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Funeral of the Late Mr. H. Buckley.—The funeral of the late Mr. Harry Buckley, of Canal Side, Grappenhall, who was accidentally knocked down by a motor car'on March 2nd and died in the Warrington Infirmary on March 9th, took place at Grappenhall on Monday, a great num- ber of Grappenhall residents attending to pay the last tribute to one who was well respected in.::the neighbourhood. A great number of beautiful wreaths were sent by friends and rela- tives, amongst which was one from Walker's Tannery Company, where he had'been employed Lir some years. Much sympathy- is felt for the widow, who is left with eight children, the youngest only a month old.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 19th March : Sunday.—Route March (Uniform). Fall in at Milling's Corner (junction of Chester-road with Hill Top-road) at 2.20 p.m. sharp to meet the Stockton Heath and ;Warrington Detachments. Monday.—Musketry. Meet at Range at 7.30 p.m. sharp. Range Officer, Q.M.S.G.T. C. F. Read. ' . .</p>
        <p>Ttiesday.—C,ompany and Squad Drill. Fall in at Headquarters at 7.45 p.m. sharp. Wednesday.—Musketry. Match with Stockton Heath and Warrington Detachments at the Drill Hall, Bath-street, Warrington. commencing at 6.30 p.m. sharp. See notice at Headquarters for team selected. _</p>
        <p>Friday.—Musketry. Meet at Range at 7.30 p.m. sharp. Range Officer, Sec.-Corporal W. Tranton.</p>
        <p>Saturday.—Parade (Uniform). Weather per- _</p>
        <p>mitting the Detachment will' proceed to the DaresbUry Rifle Club's Range for lefuslcf,tri, Practice, motor transport being provided. Fall in at Reaclonarts at 3 p.m. sharp. A prompt start is absolutely necessary. Orderlies.—Sec.-Commander F. Short, Private W. C. A. Landon. By Order, F. A. FROST. 0.c.. Platoon Commander.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Young Leaguers.-On Wednesday evening a lantern lecture under the auspices of the Young Leaguers' Union was held in the Wesley Hall, presided over by Mrs. Morton, who expressed the need for more recruits for the Padgate branch of the Union. A large number of lantern slides were shown illustrating the homes of the National Children's Home and Orphanage, and past and present workers, connective readings being given by Mrs. J. Bennett. A number of the Young Leaguers also contributed recitations. The proceeds were in aid of the National Chil- dren's Home and Orphanage. There was a good attendance and the lanternists were Messrs. Plinston and Appleton.</p>
        <p>Wedding.—Yesterday, at the Padgate Wes- leyan Church, the Rev. A. J. Morton being the officiating minister, the marriage was solemnized of Mr. George Shaw, son a Mr. and Mrs Thomas Shaw, Sunnyside, 82, Chester New-road, and Minnie, youngest daughter of Mr. and Mrs. John Dilliway, Fearnhead. The bride, who was attired in a cream tailored costume and carried a bouquet of lilies, was given away by her father. She was attended by Miss Nellie Dilliway (sister) and Miss Florence Rotheram, bridesmaids, who wore navy costumes and hats to match, and carried daffodils and sweet acacia. Mr. Harry Galloway, of Darwen, was the best man. Miss H. Bennett (Bruche Heath), who was at the organ, played the Bridal March from Lohengrin and Vendelssohn's Wedding March. After a reception given at the home of the bride the happy couple left for Llandudno for the, honeymoon, the bride's travelling dress being a navy costume with hat to match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MR. J. HAMBLET.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MR. J. HAMBLET.</p>
        <p>A wedding of considerable local interest was solemnised at the Mornington-road Wesleyan Church, Southport, on Thursday afternoon, the officiating minister being the Rev. A. J. Morton, of of Padgate. The bridegroom ' was Mr. J. Hamblet, Summerfield, Padgate, who is well known throughout Warrington and district as a metal broker. He wore a gold diamond ring, which was the gift of the bride. The bride was Miss Alice Hotchkiss, of Bishops Castle, Shrop- shire, youngest daughter of the late William Hotchkiss, Lindley Drive Farm, and Mrs. Hotchkiss, Bishops Castle, Shropshire. 'Until leaving for her ,marriage she was senior nurse at the Nursing Institution, Erskine-street, Liverpool, which position she held upwards of 16 years, leaving with the regret and good wishes of the Ladies' Committee, matron and staff.</p>
        <p>The bride was given away by Mr. A. McGau- ley, of Liverpool, an old friend of her family. She was attired in a blue military costume and French hat with plumes, and wore a diamond marquise ring, gold pendant and chain, and a spray of choice flowers, all the gift of the bride-.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <p>groom. The only bridesmaid was Nurse Ruby Roberts, a colleague of the bride, and sheyore a gold brooch set with diamonds and emeFalds, the gift of the bridegroom. She was dressed in a navy blue costume with picture hat to match. Mr. George Proffitt, of Padgate, acted as best man. The organist was Professor C. Greasier, who played "Gigue Corelli," Bridal March from " Lohengrin," and Mendelssohn's Wedding March as the happy couple left the sanctuary. After the ceremony a reception was held at Hayes' Cafe, Chapel-street, Southport. Several of the bridegroom's friends travelled to South- port specially to witness the ceremony, amongst these being Colin. 'Dickenson and his son, of Fearnhea,d; Mr.i R. W. Cook and Mr. J. Gittens. Many local friends were also present at.thii re- ception, amongst these being Mr. and Mrs: E. Lunt (Padgate), Mr. and Mrs. Proffitt (Padgate), Rev. A. J. and Mrs. Morton (Padgate), and Mr. and Mrs. Levi Richardson (Warrington). A toast to the bride and bridegroom was proposed by Mr. J. McGauley. to which the bridegroom very suitably replied. A toast to the brides- maid and best man was proposed by Mr. Bert Hotchkiss, and replied to by Mr. Geo. Proffitt. Congratulatory speeches were also made by the Rev. A. J. Morton, Mr. E. Lunt and Mr. Levi Richardson. After the speeches Mr. Richard- son sang "The Perfect English Rose" and "Sally in our alley," and Mr. Bert Hotchkiss contributed "Homeland," the accompanist being Mr. Richardson. An extended honeymoon tour has been arranged, after which the happy couple are expected to settle down at Summerfield, Pad- gate. The following are a few of the numerous pres- ents which have been received :</p>
        <p>Mr. Wm. Hamblet, electro-plated cakestand; Mr. and Mrs. T. Hamblet, silver fish servers.; Mrs. Hotchkiss, cut-glass and silver table ware; Mr. and Mrs. G. Hotchkiss, painted table centre;</p>
        <p>Mr. Ernest Hotchkiss, Co-alport cup; Mr.i B. Hotchkiss and Miss Roberts, plated table toaster; Miss Annie -Richardson. plated butter dish; Mr. and Mrs. Lunt, case fish eaters; Mr. and Mrs. Proffitt, one dozen damask serviettes; Miss Bullock (Liverpool), silver serviette rings; Mrs. Ellis (Liverpool) cut-glass salts; Mr. Ridge (Liverpool). cut-glass celery dish; Mr. and Mrs Higgins (Liverpool). 'hand-embroidered Irish teacloth; Miss Roberts (Liv4rpool),. tea cosy and blotter:. Mr. and Mrs. McGau-lev (Livernool), Wedgwood vases; Miss ,Perera (1.0. M. hand- nainted tea cosy: Miss rinff (1.0. M. cushion: Nrr. and Mrs. Reuben Higham, table cutlery; Mr. and Mrs: J. Brunt (Holyhead). sea swallow in cal4e : ladies of the Committee, Erskine-street Institution. Liverpool. case of solid silver spoons; matron-and staff of the institution, solid lv rrwp bovri : Pinnloves of Mr. Hamblet, Hardy-street, Warrington, case of silver spoons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>SCRIPTURES IN WAR.</head>
        <p>SCRIPTURES IN WAR.</p>
        <p>Bible Society's Great Programme For</p>
        <p>'Reaching All.</p>
        <p>A very interesting and instructive British and Foreign Bible Society lecture, illustrated throughout with lantern slides, was given by the Rev. Tom Dring, of Liverpool, the subject being "The Bible in camp, trench and hospital." The meeting was held in the Wesley Hall and was presided oNwer by the Rev. J. Hirst, curate-in- charge, supported by the Rev. A. J. Morton The Rev. C. P. Pitt, who should have been present, was unable to attend. The Chairman said that we ought to be very thankful for the good work that was being done by the, Bible Society, especially amongst the various missionary societies throughout the world. _ _</p>
        <p>• Speaking of this work, the Rev. T. Dring said that during war time the expenses of the society had increased more than at any other period in its history. The object of the lecture was to show the contact between the 'Bible and the theatre of war. He likened the publication of the Scriptures to a huge munition factory, and showed many interesting slides of the headquar- ters of the society and the work \done therein. Theirs was the only house at present who were allowed to export their produce to Berlin. Speak- ing of the work in Siberia, he incidentally men- tioned that at the present time there were over 1,000,000 German and Austrian prisoners in- terned there, and they were distributing free copies of the Scriptures amongst them. The Russians do not charge a penny for carriage, and even during war time they have carried the Scriptures through the port„of Archangel. He paid a great tribute to the Australian contin- gent and to the first Expeditionary Force, all of whom were given copies of the Scriptures free. They also distributed copies to the refugees in different languages, and all the men in the different internment el:imps were also sup- plied. They would not rest satisfied until every locker in every military hospital throughout the nations engaged in war had been supplied with a free copy of the Red Cross Testament. It was estimated that China could put into the field an army of 60.000.000 soldiers. A few years ago not a copy of the Scriptures could be sold in China, but last year over 6,000,000 copies were distributed in this empire A vote of thanks to the Chairman and lecturer was carried amidst much applause. The lanternists were Messrs. Plinston and Apple- ton.</p>
      </div>
