      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>LET THERE BE DARKNESS }</p>
        <p>More Shopkeepers And Householders</p>
        <p>Fined.</p>
        <p>Police visits to houses and shops illegally disclosing lights are still productive of fines. . At the Police Court on Monday the following defendants were fined: William Kay, manager of the Co-operative Stores, Church-street, 205.; William Francis Chenhall, photographer, Bridge Foot, 60s; George Hill, hairdresser, 149, Sankey- street, 205.; Lily Smith, milliner, &amp;c., 13, Rylands-street, 205.; George Bowcock, butcher, Buttermarket-street, 205., and 10s. for his house in Stafford-road; James Appleton, 39a, Horse- market-street, 205.•, Walter Sutton, Market- street, 205.; and Phcebe Lee, Dial-street, was ordered to pay costs. In the case of Chenhall it was proved by Sergeant Hayes that his workshop—almost wholly a glass structure—was brilliantly lighted at 3.40 on Friday morning. The defendant was working at that hour. The Chairman (Mr. Henry Roberts) stated that it was a very serious case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>. Motor With Headlight.</head>
        <p>. Motor With Headlight.</p>
        <p>On Patrick Maginn, of Sankey-street, being charged with driving a motor car with a head- light, in said that he only did it for safety as it was a very dark night. The Chief Constable :There are alit of people on the East Coast who also have had headlights for safety, and they have caused a lot of damage. Fine: 20s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>Open Gate Danger.</head>
        <p>Open Gate Danger.</p>
        <p>John Horne, of Dial-street, was fined 12s. 6ds for ,causing an obstruction by leaving a gate open in front of his house. It was proved that a lady caught against it when walking in the dark and blacked her eye, and, later, a man collapsed on the footpath through striking it. Mr. J. Rymer Young (a magistrate) remarked that it was a very dangerous proceeding to allow gates to remain open.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>MORE LENIENT ON WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>MORE LENIENT ON WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Wednesday, before Mr. J. W. Hayward (presiding), Alder- man Smethurst and Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, W. T. Williams and J. Upson, the following householders were fined five shillings each for, infringement of the lighting regulations:— Charles Wilcock, 61, Turner-street; John Forster, 75, Haydock-street; Richard Lee, 2, -Mersey Mount; William Garner Ackley, 8, Ship- yard; Edward Forster, 2, Piling's-court; Henry Swindles, 9, Jockey-street; Samuel Dickenson, 15, Jockey-street; Henry Fogg, 50, West-street, and Mary Ann Makinson, 15, Portland-street. Peter White, 16, Ship-yard. was fined 10s. John Clarke, of Fairclough's-avenue, was oharged with driving a two-horse lorry minus a front light, while a horse attached to the back of the lorry obscured the red rear light.— Fine: 10s.</p>
        <p>.4-" O-r driving a vehicle without a red rear light, James Beckett, 24, Hopwood-street, was fined 55., while Robert Jolley, of Newton-le-Willows, who had no red rear light to his motor bicycle, was fined 10s. Cuthbert Newton, Himtrei House Paddington, for riding his bicycle without a red rear light had to pay lOs., while Georg_e Welsby, 460, Knutsford-road, had neither a front white light nor red rear light on his vehicle, and was fined ss. in each case.</p>
        <p>_ Gilbert Titchard, 6, Priestley-street, drove a motor van in Liverpool-road with head lights, and no red rear, light, and was #ned 10s. in each case. For driving a motor car without sidelights and no red rear light, George A'Courts was fined ss. in each case, while Arthur Hoare, chauffeur, Walton Hall, Cheshire, was fined 20s. for having both the head lamps lighted on the car he was driving. A ss. fine was imposed on Joseph Cowburn for not having the identification plate distinguish- able on a motor van.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>MORE ON THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>MORE ON THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>More shopkeepers and householders were before the Bench on Thursday, when the magistrates, Mr. J. Rymer Young (in the chair),. Mr. A. J. Jolley and Dr. Naden, imposed the following fines :—William Heath, 73 and 75, Winwick- road, 60s; Frederick Jefferson, 32, Winwick- street„ 20s; John Pontifract, 72, —Winwick- street, 205.; William Harrison, 114, Winwick- road, 10s.; Joseph Partington, Bold-street, 205.; Richard Smith, 38, Chorley-street, 20s; Louisa amforth, 74, Winwick-street, 20s; Messrs. Forshaw and Forshaw, 15, Suez-street, 20s; Edward Lee, 66, Buttermarket-street, 20s; Joseph Harrison and Thos. Richardson, Pig-hill slaughter-house, 10s. each; Jardine Bros., 113, Bridge-street 1.08.; George Critchley, Wellfield- street, 55.; Gilbert Owen, 104, Birchall-street, 55.; James Cargill, 16, Cartwright-street, 55.; John Shaw, Helsby-street, 58.; James Swindles. 3, Antrobus-street, 55.; John Cunningham, 339. Winwick-road, 55.; George Boardman, Winwick- road, 55.; William Ashley, 2, Bostock-street, 55.; William Riley t 46, Bostock-street, ss; Samuel. Wilson, 37, Golborne-street, ss; Joseph Rotha- ham, Farmers' Stores, Bewsey-street, ss; Arthur Walker, Mill-lane, 55.; Ernest Moores, 14, Whalley-street, 55.; Thomas Basketta, 2, Milner- street, 10s.; Christopher Horning, 39, School Brow (recently fined ,for having too much light over his stall ;in the market) 20s. The charge against Albert Barnell, 6, Hepherd-street, who has five sons at the Front, was, on the applica- tion of the Chief Constable, withdrawn. For driving a motor car with head lights in Legh-street, Frederick Pollard, chauffeur, of • Preston Brook, was fined 10s.</p>
        <p>Harry Carter and William Wainwright, two motor van drivers employed by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., were fined 10s. each for not having red rear lights attached to a motor van.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>THROUGH HOLE IN THE WALL. Wife Detected Handing Beer Into Orford Barracks.</head>
        <p>THROUGH HOLE IN THE WALL. Wife Detected Handing Beer Into Orford Barracks.</p>
        <p>Observing a woman handing a bottle of beer to an invalided soldier through a hole in the wall round Orford Barracks, P.C. Gibbs, stand- ing in the shadows, made a slick movement and dispossessed the reCipient. Names and addresses were taken, and the woman appeared at the Police Court on Tuesday morning. Also the bottle of beer—full—on the Chief Constable's table in the Court.</p>
        <p>She was Mary Taylor, of 2, Brown's-yard, and the charge against her was an offence under the Defence of the Realm Abt—that of supplying in- toxicants to a soldier receiving hospital treat- ment.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls) explained that that was the first case in War- rington, and the delinquent was liable to a fine of £lOO, or six months' imprisonment, although it was her husband she was supplying. The man was a private in the Ist King's (Liverpool) Regiment, and was a patient in the hospital at the Barracks. When P.C. Gibbs caught the woman she said her husband had asked her for some cigarettes, and she thought a drop of beer would do him good. Magistrates' Warning.</p>
        <p>"I was quit ignorant," the defendant 116 W pleaded to the magistrates, "and didn't think was doing any harm, or else I would not have done it for E. 1,000. My husband has been wounded, but is not in hospital for that, as he got better. He has come from the 'trenches be- cause he has a rash." , -</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. 11. Roberts) said that in view of the husband's services to his country and all the circumstances associated with the offence, the defendant would be dealt with leniently, but if other cases came before them they would be adequately punished, P.C. Gibbs was to be congratulated for the smart way in which he had detected the offence. Pine: 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>SHOP ASSISTANT'S FALL.</head>
        <p>SHOP ASSISTANT'S FALL.</p>
        <p>Kept Money Handed Him By</p>
        <p>Customers.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Thursday Samuel Dunealf, of Wargrave-road, Tarlestown, formerly employed by Messrs. Edwin Richard- son and Sons, wholesale grocers, 41, Bridge- street, was charged with embezzling the 'sum of 2s. 3d:-, the money of his former employer.— He pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable stated that Duncalfe had been in the employment of Messrs. Richardson and Sons as. a shop assistant off and on since last June. At first he worked four days a week, but lately had been in continual emoinyment.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Richardson had been satisfied for some time that there had been a shortage of money and goods. Observations were recently made, and a shop assistant named Jenkins saw the prisoner take the sum of 2s. 3d. over the counter, and this sum had not been recorded. Detective-Sergeant Reddington arrested prisoner, who, upon being charged, said: "Yes, I took the money, and yielded to the tempta- tion." His house at Earlestown was searched, but nothing was found there. Mr. Richardson said he had no desire to press the case, for he Anderstood the prisoner's family were very respectable people, a son being in business at Earlestown. He brought the case, however, because prisoner had charge of a large counter, and things might have developed. The Chairman (Mr. J. Rymer Young) said that there was no excuse whatever for the prisoner, who was not short of money. Seeing, however, that Mr. Richardson had no desire to press the case, they would deal as leniently as possible with him by fining him £5, or, in de- fault, he must go to prison for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>FROM STABLE TO HOME.</head>
        <p>FROM STABLE TO HOME.</p>
        <p>Before Patrick Higgins, carter, of 7, Hart's- place, was remanded for a week at the Borough Police Court on Monday, on a charge of stealing a horse-rug,, the property of Messrs.. Stanley Carter and Son, Bridge-street, from a stable in the yard at the Lion Hotel, and also with steal- ing some articles of Army clothing from the Lord Derby Military Hospital, Detective- Sergeant Reddington said that when he arrested the prisoner he said he had only borrowed the rug because of removing from one lodging to another.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>COUNTRY LOSES £lOO.</head>
        <p>COUNTRY LOSES £lOO.</p>
        <p>Puddler Neglects His Work And Is Fined.</p>
        <p>Edward Woodward, 33, Bostock-street, was summoned for absenting himself from work</p>
        <p>without leave on several occasions.</p>
        <p>A representative of the firm stated that he had been asked by the Workmen's Association themselves to make a rather strong point in this case. Defendant was a puddler, and during the last two months he had lost 37.3 per cent. of his work By his absence from work it had meant a loss to the country of £lOO, and roughly speaking, the loss in wages to the defendant, and:Ahose of his fellow-workmen, amounted to no less than £4O. If the defendant had worked full time one mill would not have been kept standing. Defendant stated that he had sent a doctor's certificate 'to the effect that he was unable to work. He had suffered from ulcerated toes and feet. He asserted that he worked at a furnace last week that was not fit for any white man to work at owing to the condition:, The Chairman: You will be fined 305., to be deducted from your wages at the rate of se. a week.</p>
        <p>Defendant: I shall not pay. I will go to prison first. Put time on me and I will do the time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>LABOUR ON TRIBUNAL.</head>
        <p>LABOUR ON TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>Trades Council Complain Of Being Passed Over.</p>
        <p>MAYOR CRITICISED.</p>
        <p>Not having been directly consulted by the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) regarding the appointment of labour representatives on the Recruiting Tribunal, the Trades and Labour Council, at their monthly meeting on Tuesday night, strongly protested, ' declaring the omission to be against the instructions of the Local Government Board, and expressing them- selves trenchantly regarding those they con- sidered responsible. The Secretary (Mr. Chas. Dukes) reporto that the Executive Committee believed the method of selecting the labour representatives to be a violation of the Government regulations, these stipulating that "Where there are bodies in the district which are largely representative of local labour it will be well that the local authority should consult them before making their appointments. But it must be under- stood that the appointments rest with the local authority. .. . What is desired is that the Tribunals should contain a member or members of the working classes, in whom the latter will have confidence."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>Suffering Under Castigation.</head>
        <p>Suffering Under Castigation.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Hamblett moved that the Council strongly protest against the action of the local authorities in passing them over. He referred to "the castigation passed upon us in the past," and declared that "they seem deter- mined to carry it to this issue." At the meeting of trade unionists convened the Mayor said he was going to ignore the Trades Council so far as the appointment of the Tribunal was con- cerned, and his (the speaker's) union (the Railwaymen's) had already written to the , Town Clerk protesting. If the appointment of the labour representa- tives had been left in the hands of the repre- sentative at the Town Hall the names of those adopted would have been different altogether. "That is why we protest," he declared. "It is a direct insult to the trade unionists of War- rington. We feel we have not a true labour representative on the Tribunal."</p>
        <p>Postman Disgusted.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shimmin (of the Postmen's Federation) : I attended at the Town, Hall ancfnever felt more disgusted in my life. I was disgusted with the attitude of the trade union secretaries in taking it lying down as they did. I could not under- stand their attitUde in sitting down to it and take everything from the Mayor as they did. I was under the impression that our Mayor was a democrat, but I don't think so now. I should like to know who the Tribunal labour members are?</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Howard: If you went to the Tribunal for an exemption you'd soon know! (Laughter.) [Mr. Howard appealed to the Tribunal on conscientious grounds, and had his claim refused.] Mr. Shimmin: I have been told that one of them stood as a Tory candidate and the other is a member of the National Service League. Mr. Shimmin was in favour of making an at- tempt to have the appointments cancelled. Mr. Berry did not wish to doubt the integrity of the labour representatives appointed, but he protested because the - appointments had been made unconstitutionally and placed the trade unionists of Warrington in a false position. It was necessary that on the Tribunal there should he representatives who had the confidenee of the trade union movement.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Roole was afraid that the pebple who were loudest in the condemnation of the Trades Council a few weeks ago were now seeing the result of their action, for the Town Council had come to the opinion, as a result of the agitation. that the Trades Council was not properly repre- sentative of labour in Warrington. Not a Dead Body.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. E. Plinston) said that the curious part about it was that although the Council had been stated by the Press to be a dead body there were still thirty-two societies affiliated with them—rather a- fair representa- tion of the trade 'unionists of Warrington=and because they happened to have got into ill repute in the Press they had now been absolutely ignored. lie considered that in this matter the Town Council were going in excess'Of their duty just as the Town Council considered the Trades, Council had acted in excess of their duty in the other matter. On the Tribunal there should have been someone who was not so absolutely on the side of enlistment. The proper method would have been to have got into consultation with that Council.</p>
        <p>The resolution of protest was carried unani- mously, and it was decided to send it to the Local Government Board, as well as to the local authorities.</p>
        <p>Ambition. Oh, mother! how I wish I was an angel!" "Darling! what makes you Say that?'" "Oh, because then, mother, I could drop bonibs on the Germans."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>4:411P • •:* • Oa* 4. 0, 44) 40 •••404) 4* A) c• C. • •,) 4) C• • • •)4.4</head>
        <p>4:411P • •:* • Oa* 4. 0, 44) 40 •••404) 4* A) c• C. • •,) 4) C• • • •)4.4</p>
        <p>THE FOOTBALL FIELD.</p>
        <p>...:«C..X.•:•41.+0.44•4.+4.4.4••••••:.04.11.4)•+•:•••!..:,0.).)17.••••:•&lt;4.:•4•••:*</p>
        <p>AND COMMENTS AMONG THE LOCALS.</p>
        <p>Keenan and Clayton; Helme, Jackson, Cole- burne, Bibby and Lee. Referee, Mr. Cahill, of Manchester.</p>
        <p>The home captain won the toss and set the visitors to defend the lane goal. Coleborne started the ball rolling, and G. Whitfield gain- ing possession, set his forwards going with a neat pass, Smith transferring to the extreme wing, where a good opening was not accepted After s9me even exchanges in midfield, a good pass by Voster to his partner was rather too wide to be taken advantage and Dunn cleared with a big kick, and then good .play in midfield was spoiled by Henderson getting offside. The game for a few minutes was in the visi- tors' half, and Dunn and Silcock played a fine defensive game, and Helme, gaining possession, transferred to the home end, where Roberts proved too good for him. Visitors Checked.</p>
        <p>A visit to the home end was initiated by Helme and Lee, but this was checked by Roberts and Leah. Then followed another 'spell of even exchanges in midfield, but gradually the home team got into the visitors' half, and good passes came to nought owing to the visitors' backs continually playing the home forwards into offside positions.</p>
        <p>Froni a free-kick Helme led the way to the home goal, and after the ball had been crossed and recrossed, Coleburne scored for the visitors after twenty minutes' play. The offside tactics of the visitors' backs spoiled all the attempts of the home forwards, but good manceuvring by Smith and Henderson resulted in the former testing Ellison with a low ground- shot, which was safely negotiated. When Foster was on the point of breaking through, he was badly brought down by Dunn. The resultant penalty- kick was entrusted to Broster, and Ellison suc- ceeded in diverting the course of the ball, and rebounding from the crossbar, it was finally kicked wide of the goal.</p>
        <p>Half-time :--rAth erton, 1 goal; Warrington Town, 07 On resuming the home forwards showed promising form, but failed to get past Dunn and Silcock, and from a good clearance by the latter the visiting forwards showed good combination, riffside sending them back, and good play by Foster, Henderson and Cookson again took play to the visitors' end, and the latter centring well towards goal, -Foster was again the victim of the vigorous play of Dunn, and required the atten- tion of the trainer for a few moments. Vigorous Defence.</p>
        <p>I At this stage the home team were haring the best of the play, but when they reached the danger zone the visitors' full-backs did not hesi- tate to. show their powers, and cleared in a ;vigorous fashion, Smith, when well placed, shoot- ing over the bar. The home defence now began I to adopt the same tactics as their opponents, and offside was very frequent and the play was not very interesting. The visitors became more aggressive and Lee shooting hard and straight, Kite saved with difficulty, and a little later Whitfield had to kick over the bar to save his ; goal. The home team gained several corner- kicks, but could not improve upon them. I Final :—Atherton, 1 goal; Warrington Town, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>RECORDS</head>
        <p>RECORDS</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION. Southern Section. SECOND COMPETITION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Goals— P. W. L. D. F. A. P. Stalybridge Celtic Atherton Eccles Borough South Liverp00l Tranmere Rovers A ltrincham Hyde Warrington Town lO 3 6 1 13 16 7 Walkden Central Glossop</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Owing to the wintry conditions prevailing last week-end, none of the fixtures which were arranged in the Warrington and District Junior League were fulfilled. The Orford Juniors turned out with a full team to meet the St. Elphin's, but owing to only. four players of the latter club putting in an appearance the League match had to be cancelled, and sides were chosen and a practice game was played.</p>
        <p>Thesllowley team arrived at their ground in anticipation of playing their return fixture with the Penketh Juniors, but the latter-failed to put in an appearance.</p>
        <p>The Mersey Juniers turneil up on the Arpley ground expecting to meet the Fairfield United Reserves, but the latter could not raise a team, and another date will be arranged by. the League Secretary.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath Scouts have tendered their resignation to the League Secretary as they are unable to get a team together to com- plete their fixtures.</p>
        <p>For similar reasons as the Scouts the Bewsey A.F.C. have also resigned their membership of the League, and in all probability the points for the remaining fixtures will be awarded to the opposing clubs they have yet to play.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>SANKEY ATHLETIC v. HEATHSIDE.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on the Sankey ground, and the home team winning the toss, had choice of ends. Dale set the ball rolling for the visi- tors, but. the home forwards gaining possession made a 'raid ,on the visitors' 'goal, when Booth had to, deal with a good effort from Brinksman. Immediatelyrafterwards A. Gleave, by good judg- ment, neatly robbed iienyon when the latter was in the act of shooting; and at the opposite end Harter over-ran the ball and lost a good open- ing. The home forwards appeared to lack strength in their final shots, but at the end of thirty minutes' play Hunt scored from a corner. Half-time :—Sankey Athletic, 1 goal; Heath- side, 0.</p>
        <p>The second portion was resumed very much in the home team's favour, as they had the wind at their boars, and Booth and his backs had a very busy time. Taylor obtained the second gqal for the Athletic with a long shot. Foster was playing a good game for the Heath- siders, but did not get much support from his colleagues, and Booth also did very well in goal. Final :—Sankey Athletic, 2 goals; Heathside,'o.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</p>
        <p>LON %I, ORDER OF ANCIENT SHEPHERDS, A.U.</p>
        <p>"Late Spring Rose" Lodge, No. $2B.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of members in connec- tion with the above Lodge was held last Satur day at the Club House; Barley *Mow Hotel. A very creditable report •of the past quarter's working was. given by thaSecretary, P.P.C.S. W. E; Clegg. The balance after meeting all ex- penses showed ;a gain Of. £56 Us. 7d., bringing the total worth of the Lodge to £2,810 7s. 6d. After the election of Lodge and District officers, P.P.C.S. James Parr presented Bro. Frederick Mills with a Past Master's badge and framed .emblem of merit for services rendered to the Lodge. In making the presentation, Bro. Parr spoke highly of the good work and general interest taken by Bro. Mills to further the in. terest of the Order during his term of office. His Worship the Mayor (Bro. Peter Peacock), Bros W. E. Clegg, Frederick Massey, Harry Johnson, Fradcis Taylor and Joseph Hough added testi- mony to the good fellowship shown by Bro Mills.</p>
        <p>Bro. Mills, replying, stated that it had been a pleasure to him to go through the offices in his Lodge and to see the progress the Lodge had made both numelically and financially. The emblem of merit and, Past Master's badge he would always prize, and do whatever he could to further the interest of the Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>RUGBY.</head>
        <p>RUGBY.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION</p>
        <p>HAPPY DAY FOR HOME TEAMS.</p>
        <p>A feature of Saturday's Northern Union League matches was that not a single point was scored by the eight losing teams. Swinton were the only successful visiting team, and by their victory at Leigh, they spoiled the latter team's .home record, for, pre- vious to Saturday, the Leigh line had not been crossed at Mather-lane since early in November. Wigan put up a good fight at Leeds, and only lost by two tries scored in the closing stages of the game, while St. Helens Recreation continue in their winning' vein, their latest victims being Runcorn. DevM3ury at home just beat Oldham by a goal to nil, while Huddersfield and Salford gained easy victories. Results:— • • Swinton, 5 points; Leigh; 0. St. Helens Rec., 48; Runcorn, 0. Bradford, 11; Brighouse, 0. . Hull Kingston R., 8; Halifax, 0. Dewsbury, 2; Oldham, 0. Leeds, 6: Wigan, 0.. Huddersfield, 24; Rochdale H., 0. Salford, 21; Hunslet, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>NO MATCH LAST SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Rviands's and St. Thomas' should have met at Wilderspool last Saturday, but owing to several of the St. Thomas' players being engaged on munition work, and also, we under- stand, one or two having enlisted, it was im- possible for them to raise a team. However they will try to raise a team for to-day to meet Rylands's.</p>
        <p>RESULTS UP TO AND INCLUDING SATURDAY, MARCH 4th. Points P. W. L. D. F. A. P. Parochial Heathside Rylands's St. Thomas' Bolton Beamont St. Mary's St. James'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>TO-DAY'S MATCHES.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>At Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>Rylands's v. St. Thomas'. Referee, R Williams. Kick-off 3.0. Heathside v. Bolton. Referee, Barber. Kick-off 4.0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>The team to represent the Sankey Athletic in their fixture with Emmanuel Institute at Victoria Park will be selected from the following players :---J. Gleave, W. Houlding, W. Payne, J. Hands, H. Ashton, J. Gough, D. Beddard, E. Taylor, W. Brinksman, W. Taylor, G. Kenyon, G. Hunt, W. Beddall and A. Brinksman.</p>
        <p>HEATHSIDE v. BOLTON COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>At Wildrspool.</p>
        <p>HeathsideAH. Griffiths; W. Lingard, T. Rutter, A. Gleave and B. Foster; James Varley and E. McEvoy; J. Booth, H. Harter (capt.), AA Herbert, H. Hewitt, IL Dale and F. Grif- fiths.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>RYLANDS'S RECS. v. ST. THOMAS'.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS'S RECS. v. ST. THOMAS'.</p>
        <p>At Wilderspool. Rylands's.—Archer; Thomas, Littlewood, Britton and Hill; Savoury and Swanton; Dum. bell, Barker, Sutton, Lee, Butler and Mulvey. Reserves: Sloane and Latimer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The Town goes to Altrincham to-day on the 2.26 train from Bank Quay (Lew Level), with the following team:—Kite; Roberts and Leah; Broster, G. Whitfield and Bullen; Connor, T. Whitfield, Foster, Cookson, W. Taylor and Illidge.</p>
        <p>No doubt the wintry weather of Saturday was the main cause of the gathering of spectators at Hood-lane not being as large as expected After the keen game of the previous week on the Atherton ground, which resulted in the defeat of the Town team by the odd goal in three, it was fully expected that the result would be reversed in the return game, more especially when the spectators saw the pub- lished teams on the green leaflet distributed be- fore the match. It was then seen that the home club were minus several of their regular players owing to injuries and illness, but confidence in the Town's abilities was restored when it was seen that there were five players on the team who have participated in the highest circles of League football.</p>
        <p>The Atherton, committee were hard pushed to be able to get a full team, as four of their players who took part in the first game were not able to make the journey to Warrington, but in their eleven was Included an Old War- rington junior who was a great favourite on the Lovely-lane ground a few years ago—Bert Keenan, who played some very fine games for the Wellfield Club before he joined the Atherton Club. He has been in the Army since the war broke out, and finished his military. term of duty last week, and he got back home just in time to assist his old club when they were badly in need of players. For two or three seasons previous to the war Bert had been a constant playing member of the Blackpool EC.</p>
        <p>is now only too well known, the Town Club had to acknowledge defeat on their own ground, and much disappointment was caused to their supporters by the result and the class of play that was shown. Only on few occasions did the home forwards cause the visitors' defence any serious trouble, and Dunn and Silcock played such an effective and robust game at full-back that Ellison had not many shots to deal with. There were a few occasions when Smith, Hender- son and Broster showed some very deft footwork, but no goals followed.</p>
        <p>There was a great contrast in the 'display of the visitors' extreme wingers. They were Very speedy; and did not hesitate either to shoot or centre what approaching the goal, There was some drp isaointment when .Bro4fer failed to equalise the scores from the penalty-kick, bir.: credit must be given to Ellison for the way in which.- he saved his charge from being captured.</p>
        <p>The " Stars " that were expected, to shine at Hood-lane had their brilliance burnished by the Atherton defenders. The manner in which Dunn and Silcock worked the offside rule was not much appreciated by the spectators, but they did not grumble when the home backs retaliated in a like manner. Fancy a team including such , players as Wally Smith (Birmingham), Hender- son (Liverpool), Broster (Queen's Park Rangers), R. Roberts and Cookson (Manchester United) being defeated by a team of locals from Ather- ton !</p>
        <p>Had it not been for the smart work of Kite in goal the visitors in the last few minutes would have increased their score. On at least two occasions the Atherton forwards broke through the second line owing to a clear mis- kick by Leah near the halfway line. Kite proved equal to all demands, however.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
