      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, M</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>CONSCIENCES NOT AT EASE,</head>
        <p>CONSCIENCES NOT AT EASE,</p>
        <p>Disturbed By Critical Questions Put By The Tribunal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>ONE PRO-GERMAN IN MANY THiNGS.</head>
        <p>ONE PRO-GERMAN IN MANY THiNGS.</p>
        <p>Full of €.ght for, their own cause, conscientious objectors have aounnazed the meetings of the arrington Trinunai this week, and many in- teresting and sharp Contacts between the appir- cants and the assessors pt their .consciences have taken place. On Irionday night, especially there prevailed an ugly feeling; clue to nick or sympathy 'between the parties., There was a considerable attend- ance of members of the No-Conscription Fellow- ship, and twice they. applauded what they con- sidered to be points scored against the Tribunal in cross-examination, provoking the Chairman (Councillor Joseph). to threaten to clear the Court—a threat, which, though unconvincing by reason Of the fact that the anthorities were outnumbered, effected peace. To all the conscientious objectors, excepting in</p>
        <p>two or. three cases, the same answer was given, "Application refused," followed, with the retort, "May I have an appeal form, please?" and the Clerk's "Certainly." • Once when an applicant, besides being a conscientious objector, was a widow's only s•%n, and backed up his application. with Mr. Asquith's statement that it would ,be a mon- strosity if' force were used in such circum- stances, the Chairman remarked, "We are nqt here to discuss Mr. Asquith's speeches." after- wards refusing the application. . . .</p>
        <p>claimed, "You want proper soldiers, not toffee ones." (Laughter,) • The Chairman: The strongest part about you is yoyir conscience, isn't it? (Laughter.) • - Applicant: My conscience tells me it is wrong to. kill any man. I W-Ould rather lift a man up than push him down. One man is as good as another. - There will be Germans in heaven, as , well as Englishmen. Alderman Tinnion: You can be sure there will be some somewhere e4e. (laughter.) Captain Mathams pointed out. that the appli- cant Was btidged as a war-worker,- and the Chair- man said "You are exempt, not because of your conscience, but because of your work. We respect your work a great deal more than your conscience."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>FROM THE TRADES COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>FROM THE TRADES COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>One of the liveliest cases was that of Thomas Howard (26) of 3, Frederick-street,- who is a prominent member of the Trades Council. Be- sides claiming .exemption'on medical grounds he did. so on conscientious, stating that his con- science, would be greatly violated by participat- ing in war. He added,. The world •is -my country; Mankind my brethren; to do good my religion." . He had been ppposed to war since he was eighteen years _old, and had never been able to give material vent (through the ballot box) to 'his opinions upon the war, which was declared unconsciously to him. . The Chairman: You mean you were not con- sulted? Applicant: I had no opportunity of registering my opinion about it. Mr. Joseph Elwell (member of the Tribunal) : Supposing the Germans came? Applicant: That is purely hypothetical and imaginary. Alderman Tinnion: Are you an Englishman?</p>
        <p>..- _ Applicant: I happened ta have been born with- in the geographical confines of what is known as England, but I am not responsible for Eng- land. '</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion : Neither is England responsible for you. , Applicant: I am an Internationalist, recog- nising- that there is an affinity between myself and then of all nations. The German proletariat did not want war.- There is no animosity be- tween the working men of Germany and myself, and I am not prepared to shoot them. Alderman Tinnion: Do you think they would shoot You? _ _ .</p>
        <p>i• t App ican : Perhaps, but ' I would not be responsible for them. Alderman Tinnion : Do you profess any re- I am a Materialist, recognising no god.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>Humanity or Inhumanity.</head>
        <p>Humanity or Inhumanity.</p>
        <p>Captain Ilathams: Then what is your religion that you spoke of at first?—The religion of humanity. • ,</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I should say, it is the religion of inhumanity, if you will not make a single effort to prevent our enemies from over-running this country. Applicant: That is purely hypothetical. Alderman Tinnion : Would you resist force by force F—No, except under certain circumstances. The Chairman: If a man were going to try to kill you what would you do F—lt would de- pend upon which was the better runner. (Laughter.) • The Chairman: That kind of attitude is spelt with one word.</p>
        <p>Applicant: Of course, there might be certain circumstances when iny reason would be over- come by my passion, but as far as possible I will always try to keep myself under the con- trol of my reason. Mr. Elwell: As a Materialist you cannot have a conscience, heeause conscience is not material. —To me conscience is a knowledge of things, as to what is right or wrong. The Chairman: Is it knowledge of duty to your country ?—I do not recognise one.- Captain Mathains : Do you believe in the strike as an instrument ?—Yes, under certain circum- stances. Applicant was understood to say that he did not believe in violence during strikes, and if workmen did not get satisfaction from their employers they should stay in bed and fold their arms. "I wish they had all stayed in bed and folded their arms on the 4th August, 1914, and then there would have been no war, -Y he detonated. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Application refused and appeal form aske4 for, RETORT DEFINITE TO COLONEL.</p>
        <p>Of a. totally different character, but equally spirited, was the application of 'Fred Broad- hurst, a joiner for the C.L.C., and residing at 89, Marsh House-lane. Well-set and with a jowl expressing apparently invincible determination, he declared that he had conscientious objections to becoming part of a military machine, which aimed at the taking of life. Alderman Tinnion: Would you save life?— Yes. I have done that many a time. For years I was a member of the Runcorn, Widnes and Warrington Grappling Corps, and we turned out gratuitously for everyone, whether rich or poor. Alderman Tinnion : That Corps has done good work, I know. Applicant: I am also a member of the St. John Ambulance Brigade, and if we have any visits from hostile aircraft I shall do my bit then. When war broke out I was mobilised with the Brigade and worked in. a military hospital for three months. But I refused to take the oath, although the Colonel asked me to do so several times. Once he said he would court martial me, and I told him he couldn't, because I had not taken the oath, and, there- fore, was not a soldier. Then he told me to get out of the hospital and gave me sixteen hours in which to quit. I replied that I didn't want sixteen hours and could earn a living any time without his benevolence, and I left the same day. I returned next day for something, and he, again asked me if I would take the oath, and I told him definitely that I wouldn't and I won't, either.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: This is all irrelevant. It has nothing to do with the case. Applicant: I should think it has a good deal of weight in ascertaininw whether a man is conscientious or not.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Would you agree to non- combatant service?—No. _ _</p>
        <p>Captain Mathams : You'are deemed by the Act of Parliament to have taken the oath and are now held to be a soldier.—l don't believe in obeying all those over me, because there are a number of less capable men than myself. Captain Mathams: We have all got that opinion sometimes. (Laughter.) Recommended for non-combatant service. 'Appeal form asked for.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>CHOCOLATE SOLDIER.</head>
        <p>A CHOCOLATE SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>A maker of barbed wire was also in fighting mood. Having declared that he objected to going into the Army on conscientious grounds. he was told by Mr. Elwell that apparently he did not object to picking up his wages for making barbed wire entanglements. Applicant: Well, if I pick the wages up, I have to work for them. •</p>
        <p>It was here that the audience applauded, and the Chairman threatened eviction.</p>
        <p>, Mr. Elwell (to applicant) : You are engaged on war work?</p>
        <p>Applicant (emphatically): Yes, but I was doiug the same work before the war started, and I hope to do the. same when it is dyer. I make barbed wire to guard our troops; otherwise they would be cut up. Mr. Elwell: Your conscience is very elastic.</p>
        <p>Applicant further pointed out that he suffered from bronchitis, and had done so all his life. "You don't want my sort for soldiers," he ex-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>BAKER TO TALK TO HIS BOSS.</head>
        <p>BAKER TO TALK TO HIS BOSS.</p>
        <p>A baker named John Edward Mather, of 51, Orford-street said he would not go into the Army owing to conscientious reasons, and added that he was carrying out his duty to the State by following his ordinary occupation—the pro- duCtion of bread</p>
        <p>Captain Mathams: You would not supply loaves to Orfolid Barracks, would you?—No, I could not really. You have baked bread that has been con- sumed at the Drill Hall?—lt has not been to my knowledge.. . If it were, would you do it P—No, I should tell the boss I would not.</p>
        <p>Now you have learnt that your employer sup- plies the military from time to time, will you feel it your duty, to leave his employment?—l will ask him if he will stop supplying them. If he .won't; I will have to chuck it. But I think he will.</p>
        <p>Provisional exemption granted so long as applicant is employed as a baker, this being a certified' trade. His claim on conscientious grounds failed. A WAR-MAD SECT.</p>
        <p>Samuel Smyth, an export clerk, residing at, 25, Roome-street, another Conscientious objector, was asked what place of worship he attended, and he replied that he had attended the Gospel Hall for some time, but was considering leaving -them as they ,taught one thing in peace time and acted differently in war time. "They are practically cowards to their own principles," he added. "They teach separation from this world ,-'and at the same 011ie they are hand in glove with the world. They are as war-mad as any other sect.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: There . are different kinds of cowards, you know. Later, applicant said: I am p. 4ojourner in this country. I do not re6ygnise it as my country. I am travelling to ,a far better country than this. Mr. Elwell: Christ never condenaned. military service.</p>
        <p>Applicant: Yes. lie did. He denounced Peter when he took up the sword. ' • . Mr. Elwell: That was not condemning mili- tary service. Appeal disallowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>AN OBJECTOR AND HIS MINISTER.</head>
        <p>AN OBJECTOR AND HIS MINISTER.</p>
        <p>Another conscientious objector. Owen Owen, of Pinner's Brow, .aged 22. said he attended Bewsey-road Wesleyan Chur'cli. - Alderman Tinnion: Then you will have heard the superintendent minister pray for the success of the British arms in order that the war may be brought to a clqse. How can you have success without fighting? • Applicant: It is against my conscience.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion: HaVe you. consulted Mr. Derry and told him you objected to.his teaching and praying P—No. Alderman Tinnion: I attend Bewsey-road, and I have no conscientidus objeetion. Appeal refused. . There were several other Claims on similar bases, all Of which were refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>MEDICAL STUDENT CONSIDERED.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL STUDENT CONSIDERED.</p>
        <p>Represented by Mr. T. S. Steel, a medical student, aged 19 years of ( age, was granted exemption until after his second examination, which is to take place in December. It was on the understanding that he will then offer, his Services to the Navy. He had contended that if he joined the Forces now he would have to go as an ordinary soldier, and if, a delay were allowed he would be of more use to his country.</p>
        <p>An applicant having three brothers in the Army and his. father. an Army pensioner, hav- ing recently died while serving, a three months' postponement was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>DIFFER FROM OTHERS.</head>
        <p>DIFFER FROM OTHERS.</p>
        <p>Christadelphians Do Not Even</p>
        <p>Play Football.</p>
        <p>. At Tuesday's sitting. Maintaining that though they were in the world but not of it,, five young men, belonging to the Christadelphians, claimed' absolute ex- emption. Their names were.liarold Greenhalgh, William Maddocks, Ernest Laycnttk, Arthur Whittingham and William Ludgate. The three first named are employed .as wiredrawers at controlled establishments, and upon this fact some, pertinent questions • were asked by the Tribunal. .</p>
        <p>Their case was presented by Mr. Hobson, a leader in the Society, who said that the Society had always held a conscientious objection to war, always maintaining that those who were related to the purpose' of God in Christ could not take up arms. During the American Civil War Christadelphians were granted absolute exemption, and Mr. Gladstone had said that if ever there were compulsory service in England their case would receive his sympathy and support In the work of the Society conscience played a very prominent part, members not even being allowed to attend football matches, speakers not being thanked by vote, and no worker receiving any money for his or her services. To the Christadelphian the broad ways of the world were closed. Distinctions which men of the world were always striving for 'were let alone. No Christadelphian dared become a member of Parliament. By reason of their association with the purpose of God they did not vote to put men in power,. though they paid rates' and taxes, as they were commanded to do, in the words "Pay unto Ciesar the things that are Ciesar's." They paid, though they never said how the money should be spent. It might be contended that they received the protection of men in arms, but they did not require it. All that they asked was to be allowed to -serve their God and lire their lives in obedience to the dictates of Christ. Nothing less than absolute exemption would suit them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>Would Not Fire a Gun.</head>
        <p>Would Not Fire a Gun.</p>
        <p>In reply to Captain liathams, Laycock said he, had a brpther in France who had asked him to enlist, Mit his conscience would not allow him. te do so. . •</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion: Some are engaged on v.-ar work. now. do You reconcile that with your conscience? . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Hobson: They have been engaged on this work for •a good many years, and they are not responsible for the purpose for which it might be used. .</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion: If they were to fire a gun aimlessly-and it were to hit anyone, would they be responsible? Mr. Hobson: I cannot imagine a Christa- delphian firing a gun. He added that they objected to being part of the 'military machine which aimed at the destruction of people. Alderman Tinnion: The British Forces are to protect some millions of lives in this country. Would you submit to death at the hands of anybody?</p>
        <p>Mr. Hobson: We might be able to run away. Alderman Tinnion: Supposing you could not run aavay ? Mr. Hobson: We should have to take the con- sequences. After the Tribunal had whispered among themselves, Alderman Tinnion said they were quite-prepared to respect the consciences.of the applicants, but they could not be absolutely exempted, and would be recommended for non- combatant se rv,ike. - . .</p>
        <p>..... Mr. Hobson: We will appeal. Your decision does not agree with our. consciences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <p>A</p>
        <p>RCH 18, 1916.</p>
        <p>Mr...F. A. Frost: We are also bouiid by 0111.</p>
        <p>"'THIS HORRIBLE•CRIME."</p>
        <p>. . . „ . . . store;; 'clerk in the eraplo -of the. C.L.C. said he wOUld haye. nothing to do with this horrible crime." Although an Independent Methodist he attended Elmwood Congregational Church for convenience.</p>
        <p>Asked if he would do non-combatant work, applicant replied, "If I were prepared to go into the Army at all, I should be ready- to take my stand along with the others in anything I was called upon to do." Appeal refused. WOMEN CINEMATOGRAPH OPERATORS. On a cinema house manager applying for the exemption of his operator, he was asked Whet4er' a Woman could not be obtained for the post', and the' reply *as that it wOuld, be too danger-</p>
        <p>Appeal dismissed. A paving, sewering end carting contractor, who hos been carrying on the business since his father's death two years ago. was granted ex- emption until May 31st. Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for him. The appeal of the groundsman of the Warring- ton Cricket and Bowling Club wa§ refused. It was purely on domestic ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>GONG TO GERMANY.</head>
        <p>GONG TO GERMANY.</p>
        <p>But Not With A British Battalion. .</p>
        <p>Perhaps the most remarkable ease yet heard in Warrington Was that on. Thursday, after John Richardson, of 46, Mill-lane, and of no occupation, had been refused a private hearing of his appeal for. exemption. He said he strongly .objected to taking human life,, espe- cially the Germans, because he had been to Germany so many times, and was hoping to go again. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion : I hope you will go witl: one of our battalions.</p>
        <p>Applicant: I will do anything but that. But I Won't take life. There are as good Germans as English. The Chairman: You would, perhaps, sooner take an Englishman's life than ,a German's? Applicant: No; won't take life. • Mr.. Elwell: Your sympathies • are with the Germans?</p>
        <p>Applicant: Oh, no; I don't want you to think I am admiring the Part they are playing, if What we read is true. But- no doubt when I next, go to Germany they will tell me the same tales about us.</p>
        <p>Applicant asked for his statement of appeal to be read.. In this he stated: "I most strongly object to taking human life. Am quite willing to do all I, can in any other Ivay to the best of my ability. I 'will not take part in the killing of any man. I would much rather help to patch a man up than knock him to pieces. It is en- tirely opposed to my nature. I would much rather take the consequences, and I am pre- pared to. -I have been in Germany, and always very kindly treated there, and hope to go again. In fact, in .many- things I am still a pro-Ger- man, and in many things; admire the Germans more than some things in the English." The Chairman: But in case you were in non- combatant service your sympathies would not lead you to betray your comrades? Applicant: No, sir. J may say I don't ad- mire the part they are playing in the war. It is because I know them in their own country. The ; Chairman: They are very plausible, pleasant people when they like to be. APplicant: Oh, more than that. They are a great deal smarter than we in many things. ; The Chairman -(dryly): Perhaps so. The appeal was disallowed. Applicant obtained an appeal form.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>NO TEETH, BUT A CONSCIENCE.</head>
        <p>NO TEETH, BUT A CONSCIENCE.</p>
        <p>Frederick Blease, 19, Belmontiavenue, a skip maker at Messrs. Armitage and Rigby's, aged 49 years and 6 months, appealed on the grounds that he was medically unfit and had a conscien- tious objection.</p>
        <p>The applicant said he had not a tooth in his head. As to his conscientious objection, he thought God, the Creator of all, had placed within him a conscience, a free will, to guide him in such a crisis as the present, and he maintained that neither .King, Parliament, priest, nor any individual had a right to force him into a thing that the Creator of all human life did not ask him to do. He gave him the power to say yes or no, and he (the applicant) did not think any country or any individual had a right to usurp the power God had given to the individual. They asked him was he will- ing to serve, and he replied "No." He could not say more.</p>
        <p>----- The Clerk: Do you object tO non-combatan service?</p>
        <p>Applicant: No. I don't take that line or the attitude that I would not help a man who is dying, It is the free will of man being inter- fered with. My conscience dictates to me. It is the only guide I have between me and my Maker.</p>
        <p>The, Chairman: You do not recognise any laws except your own, will and conscience? Afiplicant : No. I am a law-abiding citizen. Captain Mathams : Have you a vote for a Member of Parliament? Applicant: No I have not a vote. I have no voice at all in. affairs. The Chairman said the Tribunal disallowed the conscientious objections. On the ground of medical unfitness, applicant was referred for medical examination. BLIND IN ONE EYE.</p>
        <p>A temporary postman appealed on the ground that he was blind in the right eye, and had lost the first joint of the forefinger of the right .hand.—Exemption granted. Harold Wilson, aged 28, 32, Norris-street, a fitter working on the Cheshire Lines, appealed on the grounds that he could not take a mili- tary oath, and that war was against the-teach- ing of Christ. He objected to non-combatant service under military control of any descrip- tion,</p>
        <p>Captain Mathams: What is the difference be- tween military and other Government control? —Because I have not the right of my conscience and freedom of thought.</p>
        <p>Captain Mathams ;-13ut you arc, under Govern- ment control on the railway.—Yes, to 'a certain extent, but the Government control on the rail- way' does not interfere with the management. Captain Mathams : Are you prepared to leave your occupation for conscience sake?—Yes, but that employment is of national importance, without interfering with my conscience. , The Chairman: You are not prepared to take nor-combatant service F—No. The Chairman: The claim is disallowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>ASKED TO MAKE SACRIFICE.</head>
        <p>ASKED TO MAKE SACRIFICE.</p>
        <p>A master window cleaner appeared on a busi- ness objection. Ile said be purchased the busi- ness last June. .</p>
        <p>CaPtain Matharas : When you were canvassed you wanted to know what we were fighting for? Applicant: I don't remember. The Chairman :.Perhaps if the Germans came you would realise that it would have been better to have sacrificed something. .</p>
        <p>Applicant: I am not appealing- on conscien- tious gronndg. The appeal .vas adjourned for a 'month, the applicant being advised to try to sell his busi- ness in the meantime.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>WOULD HELP RECRUITING.</head>
        <p>WOULD HELP RECRUITING.</p>
        <p>David Thomas, aged 21, 47, Priestley-street, claimed absolute exemption on the ground that he had a cOhscientious objection to military ser- vice. "As a follower of Jesus Christ," he said, "as I try to be, He tells me I should have no enemies. He treats all men as sons, and there is no difference between any man regarding his nationality. I believe in the Fatherhood of God and the brotherhood of all mankind, and can- not deprive any man of his life, which" is sacred in the eyes of God." The Clerk: Do you object to non-combatant service—helping to save life?—l do, under mili- tary control. The Chairman: You would not say that all these men fighting for their country were not followers of Jesus Christ? You are not the only one who follows the 'Great Master?—l am not prepared to answer that. I don't know one man from another. That is left between God and man. The Chairman: It is an awkward question, I admit. Captain Matham‘s produced a letter, which the applicant acknovv !edged was in his hand- writing. It was in ieply to a request that he should assist in recruiting. He wrote: "As you will see, they (his employerp) permit us to offer what assistance we can with the nature of our work. My time is very well occupied, but I shall be glad to make any effort on behalf' of</p>
        <p>your committee. TruSting I chn be of 'service" Applicant I did. not mean when I wrote that I should 'be under Military .control. Captain Mathams : In other words, you were quite willing to help and encourage others to go and fight, while you had a; Conscientious. objec- tion -ourself? Applicant: No. What cannot do myself I d•c:n't expect any other 'man to do. The Chairman': There is no doubt your con- scientious objections are not conscientious, so that the Tribunal refuse your 'application. Claimant asked for an appeal paper.</p>
        <p>FIGHTING BROTHERS ARE WRONG. I Harry Johnson, aged 20, 17. Stamford-street, appealed on the, grounds that he had -a consci- entious objection and the family had been depleted by the.war. There were seven brothers altogeth.?r TL ree are already fighting, the ap- plicant said, another was called up for 'next week, the eldest was married, and the other was only 16. Asked to explain his conscientious objection, applicant said 7he did not believe in killing a man. • • Alderman Tinnion : Do you believe in protect- ing Tour country?,--No. ' Alderman Tinnion: Why ?—No answer. Captain Mathams : What is your frank opin n of your. - brothers who are fighting?—l don't understand what you ihean. Captain Mathams Do-you think your brothers who are fighting are ,doing wrong ?—Yes. Captain Mathams: How long have you held those view?—Always. Captain Mathams : Did you try to stop your brothers from going?—No. Qaptain Mathams : Do they know you are a •conscientious ' objector?—Yes. Application refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>RECTOR MENTIONED.</head>
        <p>THE RECTOR MENTIONED.</p>
        <p>Stanley -Betley aged 28, 5, Fennel-street, life insurance agent, was anotatz. conscientious 'ap- pellant. He Objected to military control. He had a conscientious objection to anything ruled by the military. Captain Mathams: What is the Christian Church of which you are a memnerF—Church of England. Captain Mathams: How long have you held these views?—About 12 years, •ever since I was confirmed and took over my responsibilities.</p>
        <p>_ Captain Mathams : Have you any friends you cold bring who know you have held thßse views?-only the religious representatives. Pressed to name one, the applicant mentioned the Rector of, Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Do you think he agrees with your views?-7-This has nothing to do with views; It is a matter of conscience. Appeal disallowed. Another conscientious objector appealed in the person of John Alfred Pownall, aged 33, of 137, Wellfield-street. He said he thought it was the lowest condition a man could descend to to take human life by machinery. Applicant added that he had been the Sole support of his home for 11 years. s</p>
        <p>The applicant was given •a month in which to make other home arrangements His appeal on conscientious grounds was disallowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</p>
        <p>CONCERTS AND WRIST DRIVES</p>
        <p>ON THEIR BEHALF.</p>
        <p>A very enjoyable concert, arranged by Mrs. Fred Short, was given on Monday evening at Ttaddon Court Hospital. It was unique in its way, the programme being sustained entirely by ladies. Mrs. Haviour was heard to advan- tage in her solos on the violin, 'while. Miss Marie HOughton was, as usual, a delight to the soldierS in her songs. Miss Xenaky con- tributed some delightful and very well-received pianoforte solos and also accompanied some ot the. songs. Mrs. Garnett received enthusiastic eneores for her. recitations. Mrs. Waddington was deservedly encored for her songs, and the little Misses Cooper were heartily applauded for their two dances. Mrs. Short was heartily recalled for her songs and thanked for arrang- ing the concert. The result of the two whist drives held on February 29th and March 3rd in aid Of the hospital was very successful, for the expenses only amounted to 16s. .6d., while the profits were ,{:4O. All, the prizes were given by local ladies and gentlemen and almost everything provided, free of charge, including the rooms, programmes, cards, printing, &amp;c. The sale ot tickets drew 0f.:20 18s., and the draw for the water-colour presented by Miss C. Broadbent 9s. 4d. Donations received amounted to ~ClO 9s. 2d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>CINEMA AT LORD DERBY HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>CINEMA AT LORD DERBY HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>The main picture which Mr. Ashton, of the Grand Cinema Theatre, Warrington, has shown to the patients at the Lord Derby Military hos- pital, Winwick, was entitled "The Coin." Especially to those who have been unable to get out to see matinees in the afternoon, the performances have been particularly interest- ing. It may be added that twice every week Mr. Ashton makes the journey to Winwick, and presents a programme of pictures to the patients in the hospital.</p>
        <p>Hippodrome. ' BRITANNIGASSURANCE CO. LTD A turn I very much enjoyed at the Hippo- drome this week was that of Les Odeons in their speciality dancing act. The male member hail% from Lodz, in Poland, and from him I learned that he and his wife had only recently collie over from Paris.</p>
        <p>Said he to me: "Provincial audiences are sO different to those one meets in your beloved capital. Somehow or other they do not seem to. understand our (lancing, which I very much regret. We love England, and in Consequence ,we always try to give of our best.</p>
        <p>"We have toured practically all over the world. We played in one theatre in India for 71 months, giving a different exhibition of dancing every week. The ' Tango ' dance we learned whilst playing in Buenos Ayres."</p>
        <p>Many of us have seen that particularly clever turn "A Major's Reflections" before.</p>
        <p>Premier place on the bill next week is Mortitt, the illusionist. Morritt was for many year 3 with Maskelyne and Devant, the wizards of the century.</p>
        <p>Walter Keinp• and. Co. are also booked to appear •in a Scottish sketch entitled "Jock's Inventions."</p>
        <p>Star Kinema. " Carmen " has been and nearly gone, and -we- regret it, for it is a masterpiece in cinematp- graphy. •</p>
        <p>In the title part Miss. Geraldine Farrar superb. Prior to playing for the movies, Miss. Farrar was one of America's most famous prima- donnas. Her first remark on joining the Lasky. CoMpany was "Let's get to 'work," and this has become the catch-phrase in the studio ever since. , •</p>
        <p>Another strong .attraction is booked ' next. week in: " Jelfs," featuring Henry Ainley, thc., eminent London actor.</p>
        <p>About the New. Year, in answer to a query from a London editor, some 36 out of 40 London, dramatic critics gave as their opinion HenryAinley's. performance as being the best they had witnessed during 1914.</p>
        <p>Gerald Ames, who also figures in " Jell's." has appeared in person at the Royal Court Theatr on several occasions, for he was One of the " leads" with F. R. Benson's Shakespearean. Company.</p>
        <p>The latter part of the week is mostly devoted to a Mary Pickford film entitled " Esmeralda."</p>
        <p>Look out for a sensational attraction at the " Star " the week after next, for to be shown there are the exclusive pictures photographel by Ashinead Bartlett, war correspondent, wIto• was with the Dardanelles expedition. They are the first and only pictures taken et the Dardanelles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>Claims Paid, £733,000.</head>
        <p>Claims Paid, £733,000.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. T. Jefferson, presiding at the fiftieth ordinary general meeting of the shareholders of the above company, held at Birmingham, said the directors had from the first given -every en- couragement and every inducement to the inside and odtside staffs to enlist in the forces of the King. The directors undertook to ' reinstate every man who returned from the service fit for duty in a position equivalent to that which he had surrendered, and they further undertook to render assistance where necessary to the de-, pendents left behind. That promise had been faithfully carried out. (Hear, hear.) With regard to the contributions of the com- pany towards the national effort, they increased their holding in British Government securities up to December 31 by ..t318,000, and since then had inyested another £lOO,OOO in war stock. They considered it their duty to place from time to time all their accumulated reserves at the service of the Government. (Hear, hear.) In response to an appeal from the Chancellor of the Exchequer, they placed the whole of their resources in American and Canadian dollar securities at the disposal of the Government. Those resources amount to something like a million and a quarter pounds sterling.</p>
        <p>Another contriblition made to the national re- sources was in the matter of income tax.. They had paid no less a sum than A:23,203, or an in- crease upon last year of A:10,723. "But the contribution which perhaps after all is most important," continued the Chairman, "is our method of construing our obligations to those of our policy holders who have served and who are now serving their country. Our pre-, war policies contained a reservation against war risks, and our premiums were not calculated to cover such risks. .But we have continued to Pay in full all claims arising on policies existing. before the outbreak of hostilities on the lives of - policy holders belonging to or subsequently joining His Majesty's forces, and we have 'done that • without charging any extra premium to cover Wa r risks on such policies. The sum paid out in respect of claims directly arising out of the .war is over £30,000, and it is the intention of the Board of Directors, unless 'further bur-, dens are placed 'upon us, to continue this gen- ergus policy to those who are fighting our battles on land and sea.</p>
        <p>"The tptal amount paid in claims during the year was .1733.000. This is the heaviest outgo in the history of the company, and represents no less thlt 53.14 per cent. of the premium income It will be interesting to you to know that we have paid • four claims to representatives of policy holders who had taken advantage of our house-purchasing scheme. Each of these men, by the aid of that scheme, had pm-chased his own home, contemplating, no doubt, a period of peace ,and prosperity under his own roof tree. "The amounts paid during the yeat, added to the amounts previously paid, Itring the total to .116,850,000."</p>
        <p>The gross income amounted to £1,516,852, showing an increase of A:33,218. Total payments amounted to i'.1,247,08. leaving a gross surplus of £269,034. The result of the year's transac- tions was a net addition .to the funds of £249,548. This addition of practically a quarter of a mil- lion to the reserve funds was highly satisfactory under the exceptional circumstances, and brought the funds to over £4,000,000. The report was adopted. Mr. T. Boyd, 14, Winwick-street, is, the War- rington superintendent.</p>
        <p>Printed and published by John Walker and Co. (Warrington), Limited, at the EXAMINES General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, War- rington; also published by them at the EXAMINER Offices, 2, Victoria-road, Widnes; 32, George-street, St. Helens; 3, Market-street. Barlestown, all in the County of Lancagters and at Church-street, Runcorn, in the Counts of Chester.</p>
      </div>
