      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-03-18_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>By " FOYER."</head>
        <p>By " FOYER."</p>
        <p>Royal Court.</p>
        <p>A very pretty and effective musical comedy revue is this week being played to larg3 audiences, to wit, "Pretty Darlings," and from the bevy of charming ladies it does not belie its title.</p>
        <p>Mr. Geo. West, who 'plays the part of Sandy, is mainly responsible for the rich humour, and his impersonation of Charlie • Chaplin is both exceedingly funny and clever.</p>
        <p>George, before playing in revue, was a charac- ter mimic of big music hall stars, and he tells me that when the present production is running in a city where any of the latter appear he does 'away with the Chaplin mimicry and introduces his original turn.</p>
        <p>" Sandy " was born in Glasgow, and he tells me he delights playing to Lancashire audiences- Ile was in Warrington only a short time ago with "All the Nice Girls" and, to use his own words, says " I was highly delighted at Mon- day's matinee to see that your townsfolk still remembered me."</p>
        <p>Next week we are to be taken to the realms of drama, for " Fis Mother's Boy" is due to occupy the boards.</p>
        <p>Those theatre patrons who like the type of play such as "East Lynne" will find the forth- coming week's entertainment Very much to their taste.</p>
      </div>
