        <p>BOWLIk G. -- LANCASHIRE COUNTY AMATEUR ASSOCIATION ISSUE FIXTURES. The fixtures for the coming season were issued at the annual meeting of the Lancashire County Amateur Bowling Association, and are as follow: May 6, Championship Competition, first round; 13, Championship Competition, second round; Second Team Competition, first round; 20, Second Team Competition, second round; Second Team Consolation, first round; Associa- tion Competition, first 'round. June 3, Lancashire v. Derbyshire; Champion- ship Competition, third round; Association Com- petition, second round; Second Team Competi- tion, third round; 10, Individual Merit Compe- tition, first day; Second Team Consolation, second round; 17, Individual Merit Competition, second day; 24, Individual Merit Competition, third day. July 1. Championship Competition, fourth round; Association Competition, third round; Second Team Consolation, third round; 8, Lan- cashire v. Staffordshire; 15, Individual Merit Final; Second Team Competition, semi-final; Second Team Consolation, final; 22, Champion- ship Competition, semi-finals; Association Com- petition, semi-finals; 29, Second Team Competi- tion, final. • August 7, British Crown Green Individual Merit Championship; 12, Association Competi- tion, final; 19, Lancashire v. Cheshire; 26, Cham- pionship Competition, final; September 2, Clos- ing, Officials' Handicap; October 28, Annual Meeting and Presentation of Prizes. RIFLE SHOOTING. FLETCHER, RUSSELL'S v. APPLETON. Teams representing these clubs met at the range of the former on Tuesday evening. The conditions were ten rounds at 50 yards, twelve a-side, all to count. Much interest was taken in the match, being between 'the parent and junior organisations of the district, and more especially as Fletcher, Russell's team won the last match over the same distance. The Appleton men made no secret about' their being out to reverse the order of things, but were doomed once more to disappointment, for although the verdict was in doubt, practically until the last pair went down, the home team ran out winners by 13 points, after a well- contested shoot. Scores:— Fletcher, Russell's.—F. Hare, 96; G. Legge, 94; J. F. Fergusson, 94; J. Phillips, 94; J. MeDermid, 93; H. Whiteley, 93; E. Daintith, 93; J. Buchanan, 93; R. Turner, 92; A. Den- nett, 92; G. McPhie, 92; J. Towers, 91.1; total, 1,117. Appleton.—F. C. Bennion, 96; M. A. Piercy, 95; R. M. Swinton, 95; A. Shepherd, 94; H. Warburton, 94; J. W. Elliott, 94; F. Black, 94; P. Harrison, 91; L. R.. Rowson, 90; E. L. Rob- inson, 89; It. W. D. Cook, 87; V. J. Grocott, 85; total, MO&amp; Majority for Fletcher, Russell's, 13 points. ADMINISTRATIVE CENTRE v. WARRING- TON VOLUNTEER COMPANY. Deciding shoot at the Drill Hall Range, Tues- day, 14th March:— Warrington Volunteer Company.—Private F. Garside 93, Private F. W. Wright 92, Sec.- Commander Wilding 88, Private J. Pickton 88. Private Page 87, Sec.-Commander Broadbent 86 —s34—Private Hill 84, Private Hinde 84. Administrative Centre.—Captain Matharns 88, S.M. Morris 88, Sergeant Griffiths 88, Corpora, Makin 87, Private Reynolds 86, Dr. Robinson 86 —s23—Sergeant Goodwin 85, Private Dixon 83. Warrington Volunteer Company won by 11 points and thus won the rubber. _ Oa le THE LATCIWORD CADET CORPS. Headquarters, Enville-street. ORDERS. By J. B. Starkey, Commanding Officer, Week ending March 24th. Parades. ' Tuesday.—Fall in at Bridge-street Rooms at 7.30 prompt. . Physical and Company Drill Officer in charge, C.S.M. Cartwright. Friday.—The Corps will parade as strong as possible at Headquarters at 7.30 sharp, and march to a selected spot for a Tactical Bxercise. Dress—Marching Order. Signallers and Ambu- lance Section will attend. Band Practice 8 p.m. to 9 p.m. If wet, at Bridge-street Rooms. Officer in charge, Lieut. H. Kitchen. Orderlies for week.—Lance-Corporal H. Smith and Cadet Hackwell.</p>
